return {"research:r1:per-block-background": {"candidates": [{"slug": "epoch-boundary-fast-path", "title": "Let epoch-boundary blocks use the pipelined fast path instead of a synchronous checkpoint-drain barrier", "mechanism": "Bench and testnet genesis use epoch_length = 100, so every height divisible by 100 is an epoch boundary. The `pipelined` predicate (app.rs:3014-3019) sends every boundary block down the serial path. pipeline_barrier (app.rs:2377-2391) first calls checkpoint_drain, which closes the open checkpoint window into a forced checkpoint, then worker.wait_idle, so the exec thread blocks until W has written that checkpoint. At 300m even a 2-6 s window is about 54-170 MB, because the book blobs dominate. The boundary block then executes serially, including its own ~53 MB flush on the exec thread (exec_flush_seconds: 26 calls, 3.09 s, 119 ms each).\n\nProposed change: a node-local flag, default off, lets boundary blocks with no EVM tx and no slash take the existing fast path. The block's rows then go into the checkpoint layer. The height-%100 attest checkpoint is already `due` on the fast path (app.rs:2626-2631), so it is submitted asynchronously. The exec thread no longer waits for the drain write or does the serial flush.\n\nWhy the original reasons no longer force a barrier:\n- F2 (the EVM account resync): W already gathers evm_addrs in Digest jobs and resyncs them after the checkpoint lands (exec_pipeline.rs:818, 872).\n- F8 (a consensus-thread staking writer): removed by bug (b) (732c78ed). Consensus now only reads the plan, after the durable applied height reaches H-L (app.rs:6541-6551).\n\nOnly the timing of the plan's durability changes. It becomes durable when the async checkpoint lands, within about 1-2 s. Consensus needs it about 34 or more blocks later, because the exec queue holds at most 66 heights and L = 100.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:3005-3023 (pipelined predicate `!EpochManager::is_epoch_boundary`, barrier call)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:2377-2413 (pipeline_barrier: checkpoint_drain + wait_idle + mark_chain_stale)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:2714-2783 (checkpoint_drain, checkpoint_submit)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:2573-2685 (checkpoint_upkeep_with; height%100 attest checkpoint due at 2626)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:4139-4273 (serial flush path, exec_flush_seconds)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:6516-6586 (epoch_validator_set_updates: durable applied >= H-L, else refuse/retry)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:7096-7104 (build_proposal refuses a boundary until ready)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/exec_pipeline.rs:520-569 (submit / wait_idle), 774-873 (Digest gathers evm_addrs; Checkpoint writes, then resync_evm)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:12522-12578 (process_epoch_boundary: all writes through ctx.staking on the overlay)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-state/src/running_hash.rs:187 (STATE_HASH_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL = 100)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/docs/perf/design-exec-pipeline-2026-08-20.md:133-140, 254-257, 580-584 (F2/F8 rationale, written when the bench epoch_length was 100,000)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/docs/plans/consensus-bug-b-epoch-race.md:63-78, 118-121"], "evidence": "Measured on current-main code: the hsdb arm B cells, crates/ byte-identical to a44000e0. Method: /tmp/liqlens/epoch2.py over val*.log.gz. The barrier gap is the time from the previous block's 'block done' to the boundary block's 'executing finalized block'. Section 64.3's 538-vs-358 ms comparison does not include this gap.\n\n300m, 600 s cells (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r{1,2,3}-B, 9 node-cells):\n- Every height%100 block in the bench window ran pipelined=false.\n- Barrier gap: 499-526 ms mean per node.\n- Serial block: 364-479 ms, against a pipelined mean of 344-349 ms.\n- Excess per epoch: 534-639 ms. With 18-19 epochs per cell, that is 1.60-1.98% of exec time lost.\n- The boundary block was committed 5-22 s before exec finished B-1, so it was already queued and the gap is entirely exec-side.\n\nCheckpoint data (metrics-after-val1 and ckpt-samples.txt):\n- W wrote 59 checkpoints in 46.2 s; 14 of them fall in the 0.256-0.512 s bucket. These are the epoch drains, 54-170 MB each, 2-8 s after an interval checkpoint.\n- exec_flush_seconds: 26 serial flushes, 119 ms mean.\n\nOther cells:\n- 300m, 120 s (ozarchy-p4j4h-300m-r{1,2,3}-A on bdd59888; ozarchy-p4gate-300m-*): 1.5-2.6% of exec time, with only 4-6 epochs per cell.\n- 10m, depth 4 (ozarchy-p4tail10-10m-r1-{a4,b4}): 1.3-1.5%.\n- 10m, depth 1: the gap is only 80-90 ms and exec is not the limit (busy 0.77).\n\nAt 300m exec busy is 0.995 and the exec queue holds 60 of 66, so lost exec time is lost fills.\n\nWhat this does not affect:\n- Consensus timeouts: only 2-4 of 13-18 per cell fall near a stall, against 18% time coverage.\n- Block p99: 0-57 ms per node; not resolvable.", "est_gain": "Recoverable time per epoch is about 0.5 s: W's drain write (~0.38 s) plus the serial flush (~0.12 s). The take_layer/merge upkeep stays, because it runs on the fast path too.\n- 300m, 600 s: matched/s +1.3 to +1.8%, native blk/s about the same. Exec block_ms -4.5 to -5.5 ms per native block (-1.5 to -1.8%), almost all of it out of residual_untimed.\n- 300m, 120 s gate cells: +1.5 to +2.5%.\n- 10m depth 4: +1.0 to +1.3%.\n- 10m depth 1: about 0.\n- Device writes: about -0.9 GB per node per 600 s, because the ~53 MB serial flush is coalesced into the checkpoint.\n- CPU-s/1M: 0 to -0.3%.\n\nThe change also removes a hidden source of variance in 120 s cells: the 4-6 barriers per cell are each worth about 0.4-0.5% of the cell.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 1.5, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Byte-identical state. The flag only routes no-EVM, no-slash boundary blocks to the existing fast path, which more than 99% of blocks already use and which is differential-tested against the serial path. Every boundary write goes through ctx.staking on the block overlay into the same FrozenPending: rewards, inflation credits, the rotation plan, the epoch_vset rows and validator statuses. Dirty EVM accounts are resynced on W after the write, as for any pipelined block. Crash replay is always serial from the durable marker. The flag changes only when the plan and marker become durable, which affects the liveness-only `applied >= H-L` check: consensus refuses and retries, it never reads different content. Mixed fleets agree.", "flag": "TORUS_EPOCH_FAST_PATH=1 (node-local, default off). Off is exactly main's predicate: `!is_epoch_boundary` stays in `pipelined`.", "impl_size": "S: about 10 lines (a OnceLock env parse plus one predicate term), optionally an exec_barrier_seconds timer around pipeline_barrier so the stall shows in summary.json, plus about 3 tests.", "test_plan": "Write the tests first (torus-consensus app.rs tests):\n1. epoch_boundary_fast_path_identical_to_serial:\n   - epoch_length 4 and 100 (205 blocks), flush worker attached.\n   - Checkpoint policies: per-block (None), every 5 blocks, never (attest-only).\n   - Random native blocks plus a delegation/stake change at boundary-1 and a key rotation.\n   - Compare flag off against flag on: dump_all_cfs after each block (epoch_vset rows, CF_STAKING_*, CF_ACCOUNTS and CF_HASHED_ACCOUNTS after wait_idle for F2), the running hash per height, and the action status.\n   - Assert boundary blocks did not hit pipeline_barrier when the flag is on. RED until the flag exists.\n2. epoch_boundary_fast_path_plan_ready_for_next_boundary: epoch_validator_set_updates(H+L) returns Err(EpochPlanNotReady) while W is gated before the %100 checkpoint, and after wait_idle it equals flag-off.\n3. Crash: gate W before the boundary checkpoint, drop, restart; the serial replay matches (reuse ckpt_crash_replay_on).\n\nThen run `cargo nextest run -p torus-consensus` and `-E 'rdeps(torus-state)'`.\n\nBench on archy:\n- 300m, 600 s, ABBA, n=3-4, flag unset vs =1. Also 10m depth 1, n=4, to show no regression.\n- Mechanism checks: pipelined=false count goes to 0; the gap at h%100 drops from ~500 ms to ~0; exec_flush_seconds_count goes to ~0; torus_exec_checkpoints_total is unchanged; the 'validator-set plan not ready' warn count stays 0.\n- Report matched/s, native blk/s, block_ms and residual_untimed, CPU-s/1M, device writes, consensus timeouts and block p99. Every cell must be ACCEPT AGREE.", "novelty_check": "Not proposed before, as far as I could find:\n- Memory: searches on 'epoch boundary barrier / unpipelined / checkpoint drain' and 'pipeline_barrier epoch F2 F8' found no prior proposal.\n- The AVOID and revisit lists have no epoch item.\n- Git (all refs) has no epoch knob or branch.\n- Section 64.3 and the bottleneck brief only note that epoch blocks run unpipelined (538 vs 358 ms, 'executing->done'), which excludes the ~0.5 s barrier gap.\n\nWhat changed since boundaries were made serial (exec-pipeline design, Aug):\n- That design assumed bench epoch_length 100,000. Bench and testnet now use 100: devnet/wsl, testnet/genesis-weighted-base.json, and the hsdb-a-46428bfa worktree's generated genesis-3val.json.\n- Phase 3 made every barrier a forced synchronous checkpoint drain (app.rs:2381-2387), about 10x the old wait_idle-only barrier.\n- Bug (b) (732c78ed) removed the consensus-thread staking writer behind F8.\n\nNo collision with Phase 5 (it does not touch the pipelined predicate) or the s123 branches. app.rs is shared with item 7, which keeps `!has_evm` serial, and with propose-defer, in a different area.", "metric_targets": ["matched/s", "native blk/s", "exec block_ms", "residual_untimed", "bytes written", "CPU-s/1M"], "regime": "300m exec-bound (primary); 10m at tail depth 4 (secondary); 10m at depth 1 about 0 (exec not the limit)", "risks": "1. Liveness at boundary H+L. The plan stored at H becomes durable only when the async %100 checkpoint lands. The margin is about 34 or more blocks (queue at most 66, L = 100); the worst case is a refused and retried boundary proposal.\n2. The fast path at a boundary is untested today. The existing %100 pipelined test uses epoch_length 1000, so the differential test above is required.\n3. The F8 audit should be redone after bug (b): check that no other consensus-thread or RPC reader of CF_STAKING_* or epoch rows assumes the boundary is durable when it executes. Candidates are the async-validate validator count, the leader state and the state-hash submitter, which reads `durable`.\n4. The gain scales with epochs per second. It holds for epoch_length 100 (bench and testnet); a long mainnet epoch shrinks it.\n5. If the %100 checkpoint falls under 2 s after an interval checkpoint, the exec thread waits in checkpoint_submit. That wait is bounded by and smaller than today's barrier, so never worse.\n6. A one-line conflict surface with item 7 in app.rs, which is unpushed on 18c."}], "notes": "I found one solid new candidate. Other per-block maintenance work is either small or will be rewritten by Phase 5.\n\nPer-block maintenance on the exec thread, last 300m cycles profile (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p1-mperf-w1-exec.script.gz, d3cba5e2; my scripts are in /tmp/liqlens):\n- **Liquidation step:** 6.1% of exec on-CPU. On main, post_engine_tail is 11.8-13.1 ms per native block in 600 s cells, 9.4 ms in 120 s cells and 2-3 ms at 10m. 58% of the step is liq_view -> pos_sums -> dirty_sums (resident_changes, then a borsh decode, then sums_with_changes). About 21% is traders_after cloning every pending position key through layer_keys (about 25-30k keys). Phase 5 steps 2 and 5 replace both. Rejected for the same reasons as liq-scan-parallel-prevalue.\n- **Liquidation remainder outside Phase 5:** clear_cooldown/set_pending, adl_bases, put_mark_rows and telemetry total about 0.5 ms. Too small.\n- **Oracle step (begin_block_oracle):** 0.33-0.40%, about 0.7 ms.\n- **Governance, fees, CoreWriter drain:** about 0.\n\nOther checks:\n- **D12 nonce prune:** no measurable cost. residual_untimed was 21-22.7 ms at ~70-72k fills before D12 (p4gate k1, 600 s) and is 22.2-22.6 ms on hsdb B.\n- **Cancel-all stale visits:** now 223 visited / 108 hit per cancel-all (hsdb val1), against 189/92 in section 30. The prune would still save only about 1 ms per block, so it stays AVOID.\n- **Funding:** not implemented.\n- **ADL:** idle in the bench.\n- **Trade history and streams:** off.\n- **SnapshotManager:** not wired into the node.\n\nSide finding for 18c / item 7 (in flight, so not a candidate). Every serial block pays the same barrier: an EVM tx, a slash or an epoch boundary each force a synchronous checkpoint drain, about 0.5 s at 300m, plus about 0.12 s of serial flush. item7-evm-lanes.md:88-90 sizes the serial-block cost from 'executing->done' spans, which exclude the barrier. At N=5 with steady EVM traffic at 300m, that could cost on the order of 25-35% of exec time unless EVM-lane blocks avoid the drain. This bears on D1 and step 3.\n\nMeasurement artifact: 120 s cells contain 4-6 epoch barriers, each worth about 0.4-0.5% of the cell. This is a hidden source of noise in 120 s A/Bs.\n\nFallback if the owner keeps boundaries serial: trigger the checkpoint asynchronously a few blocks before the boundary. That would recover only about half, because book blobs make even a short drain 60-100 MB.\n\nMemory saved: the epoch barrier finding and the item 7 side finding, both tagged project:torus-hyperbft."}, "research:r1:match-core": {"candidates": [{"slug": "parallel-match-setup", "title": "Build each market's match inputs (policing positions, account margins, match requests) inside its match worker job instead of serially on the exec thread", "mechanism": "Today Phase 3 (inside the match timer) loops over every market batch on the single exec thread before any worker starts: per prepared order it inserts the sender's pre-batch position into a fresh ReduceOnlyPositions HashMap (`known`, native_executor.rs:7645-7660); per checked sell it does AccountMargins get + pools.get + insert/insert_taker_only (7664-7700); and it builds one MatchRequest per order with taker_margin_limit (an Arc clone of the market tiers plus reserve_price, 7701-7711). Only then does match_parallel_with (7726) hand the books to the exec pool, and the exec thread sleeps in run_jobs. All of this work is per market and reads only immutable data: the market's prepared slice, its own book, the frozen AccountReader (already Sync and used by the workers as MakerAccountSource), pools, markets and margin_configs. With TORUS_PARALLEL_MATCH_SETUP=1, the exec thread only takes the books, which costs one HashMap remove per market. It passes (market, book, &prepared) with the same LPT weights (prepared.len()) to a new MarketWorkerPool entry. That entry runs a Sync setup closure inside each job, under run_contained, right before match_market. About a third of the match phase leaves the serial chain.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7611-7714 (serial per-market setup inside match_timer)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7726-7762 (match_parallel_with call, match timer end)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:9494-9515 (margin_tiers, taker_margin_limit)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:10048-10074 (reduce_only_positions_for)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:115-195 (match_parallel_capped_with: LPT chunks, exec_pool::run_jobs)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:293-384 (process_chunk, run_contained, match_market)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-core/src/order_book.rs:419-453 (ReduceOnlyPositions, never iterated)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-core/src/order_book.rs:649-770 (AccountMargins, never iterated)"], "evidence": "Call-graph profiles on d3cba5e2, 300m, val1, window W1, cycles:u at 499 Hz: /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p1-mperf-w1-exec-inline.script.gz and p2-... A git diff shows the setup region is unchanged on main except cloid fix 3. Exec-thread samples in the setup are 811/17,912 (4.5%) and 746/17,829 (4.2%): ReduceOnlyPositions insert 1.35-1.5%, the MatchRequest collect 1.3%, insert_taker_only 0.8-1.0%, AccountMargins get 0.45%, pools.get 0.15-0.19%, reduce_only_positions_for 0.02-0.05%. W1 had 191 native blocks, 71.5k placed orders per block and a match timer of 24.4-25.7 ms per block (ozarchy-p4s0-300m-p{1,2}-mperf/prof-metrics-w1-*.txt). So the setup is 7.8-8.5 ms per block, about 0.115-0.12 ms per 1k placed orders, about 1/3 of the match timer. The match timer is linear in placed orders at every shape: 0.33 ms per 1k placed (300m 120 s, p4j4h-300m-r1-A: 22.4-23.1 ms at 69.0k), 0.335 (main 600 s, hsdb-300m-r1/r2-B: 30.4-31.4 ms at 92.3-93.6k), 0.335 (10m depth 1: 10.0 ms at 29.9k) and 0.37 (10m depth 4: 35.1-35.8 ms at 96.2k). On main, the serial setup is therefore about 8 ms per native block at the 120 s gate shape and about 10.5-11 ms in 600 s cells. 300m is exec-bound (busy 0.995-0.997, queue 60 of 66).", "est_gain": "300m: exec block_ms -6.5 to -7.5 ms per native block at the 120 s gate shape (about 226 ms, -3%), and -8.5 to -9.5 ms in 600 s cells (about 313 ms, -3%). The setup leaves the exec thread and adds only about setup/P_eff = 1-2 ms to the parallel match wall, with P_eff about 5-10 on the shared host. Engine ms per 1k fills drops about 3% at constant fills per block. Expected: matched/s +2 to +3% (above the n=4 CI of about ±1.3%), native blk/s about +3%, CPU-s/1M about 0 (work is moved, not removed). 10m depth 4: about -10 ms of 244 ms (+3-4%). 10m depth 1: block_ms about -5%, matched/s about 0 (the bench tail sets the pace).", "est_gain_pct_matched": 2.5, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "Byte-identical by construction. Each market's setup is a pure function of its prepared slice (flat order), its own book, the frozen reader backend, pools, markets and margin_configs; the workers run the same code that runs serially today. The maps it builds (ReduceOnlyPositions, AccountMargins.traders) take only point reads and writes and are never iterated (documented at order_book.rs:419-421 and 658-662). LPT chunking keeps the same weights, and results are reassembled and sorted by market id as today. This is a node-local scheduling change only.", "flag": "TORUS_PARALLEL_MATCH_SETUP=1 (default off; unset keeps today's serial setup loop)", "impl_size": "Medium, about 120-180 lines: a generic setup-hook entry in market_workers.rs (process_chunk runs setup, then match_market, inside run_contained); the per-market setup closure in native_executor.rs, which is the moved body of 7616-7711; a OnceLock flag; and a differential test.", "test_plan": "Write tests first. Differential test, flag off vs on, over fuzzed multi-market batches (reuse fuzz_batches in crates/torus-bridge/tests/parallel_settle_tests.rs and the run_observed helpers in cancel_batch_exec_tests.rs). Cover reduce-only senders and books with resting reduce-only orders, stops, cloids, FOK, a market without a book, a single-market batch, and a planted panic in setup, which must fail-stop like a match-worker panic. Assert equal NativeBatchResult, full state digest, book bytes, and trader and cloid indexes. Run cargo nextest -E 'rdeps(torus-bridge)' with the flag on and off. On archy: 300m 120 s ABBA n=4 (unset vs =1), then one 600 s n=3 pair. Report matched/s, native blk/s, exec block_ms, phase_match_ms (expect -7 to -10 ms), engine ms per 1k fills, CPU-s/1M and consensus timeouts, with ACCEPT AGREE in every cell. Add a 10m depth-4 pair to check for regressions.", "novelty_check": "Not in s123: the 4 implemented, 3 dropped or folded, and 3 named refuted items do not touch the Phase 3 setup, and the exec/matching lens memory b6d2198b lists only cancel-all, PARALLEL_ENGINE, the prep stage and the liquidation scan. TORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE shards only the Phase 2 prepare (native_executor.rs:7458-7503); the Phase 3 setup loop is serial in every mode. Earlier work (results doc sec 11.3-11.4 item 8, which became item 6 M1 pre_pos, 49df3ebe) only removed the setup's position re-reads; the remaining map and request building was never moved. No commit or memory moves it. The Phase 5 plan does not touch it (step 3 makes match workers return dirty sets, which is the same file, so coordinate). 11 s123 refuted candidates are still unrecovered by name; confirm with 18c.", "metric_targets": ["matched/s", "native blk/s", "exec block_ms", "phase_match_ms", "engine ms per 1k fills"], "regime": "300m, exec-bound (main target); 10m at tail depth 4; at 10m depth 1, block_ms only", "risks": "A panic in setup becomes a contained MarketWorkerPanic (fail-stop) instead of an exec-thread unwind; both are fatal. The first reader-fault message can differ (node-local only). The net gain shrinks if the pool's effective parallelism on the oversubscribed host is low; the worst case is still about -6 ms. Merge coordination: the s123 cancel-all branch (run_contained made generic) and Phase 5 step 3 also edit market_workers.rs. Optional: also pre-size `known` and AccountMargins.traders in the worker (the rehash shows as 0.8% of pool CPU)."}, {"slug": "sell-top-up-stitch-list", "title": "B-blind sell top-ups from a stitch-time candidate list with a per-sender run cache", "mechanism": "sell_top_ups (native_executor.rs:9858-9916) is in the margin phase and runs serially on the exec thread. It first scans every PreparedOrder of every market batch (about 176 bytes each, about 90k per block) for top_up_candidate and sorts the hits by flat index (9871-9877). Then, per candidate, it does markets.get plus sell_top_up_price (i128 mul and div); market_batches.get_mut plus a Vec index, which is a cold read of the PreparedOrder and its params; basis.get; try_reserve_for_qty_cfg; and four sender-keyed hash probes (bal_cache.load, pools.get, excess_by_sender.get, bal_cache.set). With TORUS_FAST_SELL_TOP_UPS=1, four things change. First, stitch_outcome (8190-8228) already runs in flat order for both the serial and the PARALLEL_ENGINE stitch; it appends (flat index, market, k, sender, quantity, reserved) for each candidate, so the scan and the sort go away. Second, the top-up price and cfg are computed once per market. Third, consecutive candidates of one sender share one running balance and one pos_net and excess lookup; the sells of a PlaceOrderBatch are contiguous in flat order, about 200 per batch. The balance is loaded only when a candidate first needs a grant and is written back with bal_cache.set when the sender changes. Fourth, the per-candidate PreparedOrder access becomes a pure store: margin_reserved = reserved + grant, plus res_price on a full grant.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:9858-9923 (sell_top_ups, sell_top_up_price)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7578-7594 (call site after the Phase 2 fold)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:8190-8228 (stitch_outcome, flat-order push into market_batches)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7505-7562 (both stitch call sites: sharded and serial prepare)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:335-380 (BalanceCache load/set, dirty list)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:453-479 (PreparedOrder layout)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:2159-2174 (Phase2Market bid_floor/cfg)"], "evidence": "Profiles on d3cba5e2, 300m, val1, window W1 (p1/p2 exec-inline scripts in /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/): sell_top_ups is 1,029/17,912 (5.74%) and 973/17,829 (5.46%) of exec on-CPU, i.e. 10.2-10.8 ms per native block. The W1 metrics (prof-metrics-w1-*.txt) show 191 native blocks, 71.5k placed per block and 6.71M top-ups, i.e. 35.1k top-ups per block at about 300 ns each. Visible parts: sort_unstable_by_key 101 samples (about 1.0 ms), BalanceCache load and set plus map gets 222 (about 2.3 ms), Vec deref 113, try_reserve about 67. The remaining 464 self samples are field loads and stores on cold PreparedOrders and the scan. On main, torus_sell_top_ups_full_total is 91,156,649 over 2,051 native blocks (ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B/metrics-after-val1.txt), i.e. 44.4k top-ups per block, 49% of placed orders, all full. That puts sell_top_ups at about 13-14 ms per native block, about 4.4% of the 313 ms exec wall. The function body is unchanged since the profile except for the typed-load parameter (diff checked).", "est_gain": "Removes the full scan, the sort, the params deref and the cold PreparedOrder read, plus about 4 of the 6 hash probes per candidate: about 300 ns falls to about 100-150 ns per top-up. 300m 600 s: -5 to -9 ms per native block (central -6.5 ms, about -2% block_ms). Gate shape: -3.5 to -7 ms of about 226 ms. Expected: matched/s +1.5 to +2.5% (exec-bound); CPU-s/1M -0.3 to -0.5%, because exec-thread CPU is removed, not moved. At 10m depth 4 the gain is smaller (about -3 ms): with only 10 market Vecs, the PreparedOrder accesses are less random.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 1.7, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Byte-identical. Each sender's grants are still computed in flat order against its own running balance. Senders' balances, pools and excess entries are disjoint, so the order across senders never mattered. BalanceCache ends with the same values and the same dirty set (flush_all sorts the dirty list). The balance is loaded lazily, only when a candidate's extra > 0, as today's code does, so the cache's key set is unchanged. Full, partial and none counts are unchanged. Candidates with no market, no bid floor, or an overflowing price or reserve skip exactly as today.", "flag": "TORUS_FAST_SELL_TOP_UPS=1 (default off; unset keeps today's scan + sort + per-candidate path)", "impl_size": "Small to medium, about 80-120 lines: stitch_outcome records candidates into a Vec owned by execute_batch_phases; a sell_top_ups_fast function with the run cache and the per-market price table; a OnceLock flag. Plus a differential test.", "test_plan": "Write tests first. Differential test, flag off vs on, under both the serial prepare and the TORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE=N stitch, over fuzzed batches with: interleaved senders; one sender with several actions in a block; partial and none grants (tight funding, as in the Option-B tests); basis overrides (reduce-only and market orders); a market without a bid; and a planted balance-read fault, which must fail-stop and give the same skips. Assert equal PreparedOrder margin_reserved and res_price, NativeBatchResult, top-up counters and full state digest. Also run the existing r02_sell_top_up_* tests with the flag on. Bench on archy: 300m 120 s ABBA n=4, unset vs =1, plus one 600 s pair. Report matched/s, exec block_ms, phase_margin_ms (expect -5 to -8 ms), CPU-s/1M and AGREE; add a 10m depth-4 pair.", "novelty_check": "Not built. Since 31cea698 the only commits that touch sell_top_ups are fault handling (4fea98e2) and typed loads (2a245321). Memory 99c9e4d7 (s92 ubench) noted in passing that 'collecting candidates at stitch would drop the sort' (+0.10-0.13 ms/1k margin cost in the ubench); it was never implemented or measured on a node, and the per-sender run cache was never proposed. TORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE leaves sell_top_ups serial: one call after the fold, at 7578. It is not among the named s123 items. Results doc sec 11.4 item 2 targeted the old same_batch_bid_top_ups, which B-blind replaced.", "metric_targets": ["matched/s", "exec block_ms", "phase_margin_ms", "CPU-s/1M"], "regime": "300m, exec-bound; 10m at tail depth 4 (smaller gain)", "risks": "The split inside sell_top_ups is partly inferred (464 self samples are not line-resolved), so the gain may land at the low end, about -4 ms. A balance-read fault must keep today's per-candidate skip semantics: fall back to the per-candidate path for that sender run. Phase 5 step 2 replaces BalanceCache with in-place Users writes; the run cache must be ported then, while the stitch-time list stays valid. This is margin-phase code, so another lens may report it too."}], "notes": "I found two candidates. Both move or cut serial exec-thread work on the per-order path into the book. Together they are worth an estimated -12 to -17 ms per native block at 300m, about 4-5% of the 313 ms exec wall.\n\nData basis and caveats:\n- The newest call-graph profile is d3cba5e2 (300m, val1, W1, cycles:u at 499 Hz, files /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p{1,2}-mperf-w1-*.script.gz). I diffed both code regions against main a44000e0; they are unchanged apart from cloid fix 3 and a typed-load parameter.\n- Exec-thread numbers are robust: two independent profiles agree to within 0.3 percentage points.\n- Pool-thread sample counts are approximate. Threads that wake often can be biased under -F (memory 68626b6b). I used pool numbers only to bound the match-phase dilution.\n- I wrote the analysis helpers to /tmp/ob-research. No cargo, bench or profiler was run.\n\nChecked and not proposed:\n1. **Classic book journals.** row_journal and level_journal BTreeSet inserts are discarded in Classic mode (native_executor.rs:6276-6278). They cost 3.3% of pool CPU (match_at_level and insert_order) plus 0.9% of exec CPU (discard). Skipping them would conflict with Phase 5 step 3, which builds Classic rows from a book dirty set at the same sites.\n2. **Reduce-only sweep.** After every placement with fills, the sweep machinery (a touched BTreeSet<Address>, sweep_reduce_only per trader, and an order_index probe per fill) runs even when reduce_only_index is empty. That is 2.7% of pool CPU. Smaller trims: one eager order_seq.get per fill for mark_chunk_dirty while chunked mode is off, and rehash of AccountMargins.traders and the MAKER_FREE map. Together these are about 4-5% of pool CPU: about -1% CPU-s/1M and under +0.5% matched/s. Too weak to propose alone.\n3. **Cancel-all locate.** The plan's partition_point does an order_seq hash probe per binary-search step: 2.4% of exec on-CPU (sec 25.1 'locate' 9.7 ms on 707f132f). With only 5 levels per side at band 5, one linear pass per level could halve it. But the s123 parallel cancel-all branch moves this work to the pool, so its standalone value is about 1%. It is complementary (torus-core only, read-only plan), so revisit after that branch.\n4. **Maker account snapshot.** maker_account (AccountReader::get_position binary search plus maker_free per worker) is about 17% of match CPU. It belongs to Users-layout and Phase 5 territory.\n5. **Smaller exec-thread items.** Post-match TraderMarkets::absorb (1%), MatchResult drops on the exec thread (0.9%) and record_margin_cuts (0.3%) are each 1-2.5 ms per block. Possible riders, not candidates."}, "research:r1:locks-threads-tail": {"candidates": [{"slug": "exec-pool-fine-grain-jobs", "title": "Split the exec-pool match and pass-A work into K jobs per worker so threads that start late do not hold back the phase, and optionally let the exec thread run jobs too", "mechanism": "Today match and settle pass A cut their markets into exactly as many LPT chunks as there are workers (min(cap, markets) = 32 at 300m). On a 32-thread host shared by 3 nodes, the 32 woken pool threads start their single chunk anywhere from 0 to about 8 ms after the phase begins, because they wait for a CPU. The chunks hold equal work, so a phase ends only when the latest starter finishes. Threads that start early go idle while late starters are still working. Proposed change, behind TORUS_EXEC_POOL_GRAIN=K: make min(markets, K x cap) LPT chunks but keep the same pool size, so early-running workers steal the extra chunks through rayon's existing scope work-stealing. The phase then ends at about total work / concurrency plus one small chunk. Optional sub-flag TORUS_EXEC_POOL_CALLER=1: run_jobs uses pool.in_place_scope plus an atomic cursor, so the exec thread, which is already on a CPU, processes chunks right away instead of blocking on a LockLatch. That also removes the exec-to-worker start latency and most of the end wake-up, which together measured about 1 to 1.7 ms per phase. Scheduling only: every chunk's result comes back by market id or input index, and the per-market computations are independent. State stays byte-identical.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:146-151 (workers = max_workers.min(n); assign_chunks(&counts, workers))", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:174-191 (chunks -> exec_pool::run_jobs)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:235-249 (chunk_indices)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:8629-8701 (pass A weights, chunk_indices(&weights, max_workers), run_jobs)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/exec_pool.rs:20-60 (pool size = max(host, caps); run_jobs = pool().scope called from the non-worker exec thread -> in_worker_cold + LockLatch)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7612-7757 (match timer: serial prep + match_parallel_with)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/panic_scope.rs:14 (catch_recoverable, needed for jobs that run on torus-execution under the caller sub-flag)"], "evidence": "Thread timeline from the all-thread perf dumps p1/p2-mperf-w1-all.script.gz in /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/:\n- Profiled node: val1 on d3cba5e2, which already has the P2-3 pool and the same static LPT chunking as main. The scheduling code is unchanged up to a44000e0; only cloid flags were added.\n- Sampling: 2 ms per thread. Both reps agree. Scripts are in /tmp/torus-explore.\n\nMatch phases (n=127/129):\n- Pool span is 18.5-21 ms, and about 30 of the 32 threads each run exactly one chunk.\n- Per-thread work is balanced: sample var/mean is 0.70-0.86, below the Poisson value of 1.\n- Start times are skewed. Thread first-sample p10/p50/p90 = 0.8-1.1 / 3.4-4.0 / 7.8-8.6 ms after the phase starts.\n- End times are skewed the same way: last-sample p10/p50/p90 = 8.5 / 12.8-13.6 / 18.5-18.7 ms.\n- The last 3 deciles of each phase hold only 16-18% of the samples.\n\nPass A has the same shape: span 12.9-14.2 ms, starts p50 3.0-3.4 / p90 6.5-7.2 ms.\n\nExec-thread handoff: last exec sample to first pool sample is 1.8-2.1 ms, and last pool sample to next exec sample is 2.3-2.8 ms. That is about 1.0 / 0.7 ms per phase after the sampling bias.\n\nModel: dynamic end T solves sum r_i*(T-s_i)=W, using each thread's observed start and rate. It gives span x0.59-0.61, about -7 to -10 ms per match phase and -5 to -7 ms per pass A phase on that build.\n\nCurrent main, hsdb-300m-r2-B summary.json:\n- phase_match 31.37 ms and settle_pass_a 10.79 ms per native block, at 72k fills and 312 ms blocks.\n- The exec pool uses 1.15 cores per node (io-threads), about 371 ms CPU per block.\n- Match runs at about 8 threads concurrently, pass A at about 13, against 32 workers.\n\nThe knob cannot test this: TORUS_MATCH_WORKERS=128 would build a 128-thread pool (exec_pool.rs:24-30).", "est_gain": "-6 to -11 ms per native block at 300m: match pool part about 27 -> 18-21 ms, pass A about 10.8 -> 7-8 ms (modelled saving x0.5-0.7). That is about +2 to +3.5% matched/s at 300m, at about 0.32% per ms with 312 ms blocks and busy 0.997. 10m: about 0, since 10 markets are already one chunk each; the caller sub-flag saves about 1 ms per block. CPU-s/1M and RSS neutral.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 2.5, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Scheduling only. Match results return with market_id and are sorted at native_executor.rs:7771. Pass A plans are scattered back by input index (8705-8713). compute_market_settle_plan and match_market are pure per market. The per-thread MAKER_FREE cache stores a pure function of the frozen reader. Chunk count and the caller's participation cannot change any result. The flag is node-local and a mixed fleet is safe. Flag off keeps chunk count = cap and run_jobs exactly as on main.", "flag": "TORUS_EXEC_POOL_GRAIN=K (jobs per worker; unset/1 = today). Sub-flag TORUS_EXEC_POOL_CALLER=1 (exec thread runs jobs in place).", "impl_size": "small-medium: about 60-120 lines across market_workers.rs (chunk count), native_executor.rs pass A (chunk_indices cap x K) and exec_pool.rs (optional in_place_scope + atomic-cursor runner), plus tests", "test_plan": "1) Differential tests:\n- match_parallel_capped_with and settle_market_results_parallel give byte-identical results for K in {1, 2, 4, 8, n}, with and without the caller sub-flag. Use random market loads and extend parallel_settle_tests and capped_chunking_tests.\n- A panicking market still gives a typed MarketWorkerPanic, and pass A still falls back.\n- On the caller path, a job panic on torus-execution does not trip the fail-stop hook.\n2) Gate before benching: add a per-phase histogram of (max job start - min job start) and of the tail (last job end - median job end). Run one 300m cell on main to confirm the 3-8 ms start skew. It was measured under perf on d3cba5e2.\n3) A/B:\n- Shape: 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=4.\n- Arms: unset vs K=4, then K=4 + CALLER=1.\n- Report: phase_match_ms, settle_pass_a_ms, exec block_ms and ms per 1k fills, matched/s, native blk/s, CPU-s/1M, exec run_delay (schedstat), AGREE in every cell.\n- Then one 10m no-regression pair at depth 1 and depth 4.\nKeep it if the matched/s paired-CI lower bound is >= 0 and match ms is down >= 15%.", "novelty_check": "Not tried before:\n- R6 (583badd) and P2-3 (56914dd1) deliberately kept chunk count = worker cap (\"scheduling only, every site keeps its own deterministic chunking\").\n- The P2-3 wake-path check (mem b9bd776c) estimated wake costs at tens of µs from code reading and found no mechanism. Nobody measured the per-thread start skew; that measurement is new here.\n\nNot an AVOID item:\n- Not among the recovered s123 candidates; the exec lens mem b6d2198b lists cancel-all, PARALLEL_ENGINE, prep stage and liq scan. The 11 unrecovered s123 refutations cannot be checked.\n- Not the TORUS_MATCH_WORKERS/SETTLE_WORKERS cap revisit item: caps change pool width, this changes job granularity at the same width.\n- Unlike the refuted bounded pools (-67.7%, M200k section 6) and SAVE_BOOKS_WORKERS=4 (-34%), it removes no capacity.\n\nCoordinate:\n- With exp/s123-parallel-cancel-all-book-pass, which also uses chunk_indices/run_jobs and edits market_workers.rs; it should inherit the grain flag.\n- With Phase 5, which edits native_executor.rs around pass A.", "risks": "- The start skew on main may be smaller than on d3cba5e2; the gate in step 2 checks this first.\n- More jobs add a few µs per job of scope overhead.\n- Caller sub-flag: jobs on torus-execution must use panic_scope::catch_recoverable. Match currently uses run_contained/catch_unwind, which does not stop the fail-stop panic hook.\n- The gain overlaps with bulk-thread-nice: fewer start delays leave less tail to remove.\n- File overlap with the s123 cancel-all branch and Phase 5."}, {"slug": "bulk-thread-nice", "title": "Run the gossip-verify and ingress-verify pools at nice +N so the exec thread, the exec pool and the checkpoint helper threads win the CPU on the shared host", "mechanism": "At 300m every node's exec thread is the bottleneck and every pool phase is on its critical path. Yet the host is oversubscribed in bursts, and two things push the critical threads down:\n- The kernel is BORE (sched_bore=1, penalty offset 24, scale 1536). Tasks whose CPU bursts exceed about 17 ms lose priority. The exec thread runs 60-125 ms between sleeps, so it competes at roughly nice +3..+6 (inference from the BORE formula, not measured).\n- The 2 x 16 verify threads per node (1.7-1.8 cores per node, about 5.3 cores fleet-wide) run short jobs and keep full weight. They preempt the exec thread and the exec-pool workers, and they share CPUs with them.\n\nThe candidate: setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, gettid(), N) in each verify pool's rayon start_handler. Raising nice is allowed unprivileged, and Linux nice is per thread. A secondary sub-flag uses RocksDB's own Env::lower_thread_pool_cpu_priority for the LOW (compaction) pool.\n\nWith N=10 the bulk threads drop well below the exec thread even after its BORE penalty. The exec thread, pool workers, ckpt-merge, end-resident and hotstuff-algo then preempt bulk work instead of waiting behind it. Bulk work still uses the roughly 40% idle CPU. Unlike pinning or bounded pools, no capacity is removed.\n\nThe set is limited on purpose. Unchanged: the exec pool, the global rayon pool (exec phase-1 verify and the consensus validate path), tokio (networking) and rpc-worker (it serves the bench commit tail at 10m).", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-node/src/main.rs:834-845 (gossip verify rayon pool builder; add start_handler)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:570-584 (ingress verify rayon pool builder; add start_handler)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-state/src/db.rs:749-755 (RocksDB options / max_background_jobs; optional Env::lower_thread_pool_cpu_priority, rocksdb 0.24 env.rs:130)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-node/src/main.rs:849-866 (GOSSIP_VERIFY_MAX_INFLIGHT=1024 inline fallback onto the tokio task: guard)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:979-1009 (native pool write lock held by verify threads; also taken by the consensus thread in drain/remove: inversion guard)"], "evidence": "Exec thread run delay (schedstat.json):\n- 14.9-16.0 s per 602 s (2.46-2.64% of wall) in all six current 300m 600 s cells (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r{1,2,3}-{A,B}).\n- That is about 8.4 ms per native block, over about 95k timeslices (about 51 per native block, 0.16 ms each), so mostly preemptions.\n- Also 2.7-2.9% in p4j4h 120 s cells and in 10m depth-4 cells.\n- hotstuff-algo run delay is 8.3-9.4 s, about 7.5% of its on-CPU time.\n\nOff-CPU context-switch stacks (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4j3-300m-p1/offcpu-sched.txt; Phase 4 2a245321, val1, 45 s):\n- exec-pool threads were preempted 0.56-0.69 s per s (sched_yield about 0.16 of that), against about 1.1 cores of pool CPU.\n- These are timer-tick preemptions in the middle of match_market, place_order_checked, compute_market_settle_plan and PositionCache::update_in_place.\n- verify threads were preempted 1.05-1.38 s per s.\n\nPool threads wait for a CPU: the p4s0 perf timeline (see exec-pool-fine-grain-jobs) shows about 30 woken workers starting 1-8.6 ms apart, with only about 13 on CPU at once.\n\nHost, vmstat-1hz for hsdb r2-B:\n- runnable r p50 20 / p75 47 / p90 77 / p99 153 on 32 hardware threads.\n- 159k context switches per s; us 50%, sy 9.6%, idle 40%.\n\nPer-node CPU (io-threads): gossip-verify 0.94-0.97 and ingress-verify 0.76-0.84 cores, against exec thread 0.85 and exec pool 1.15.\n\nCgroup: the cpu controller is enabled under user.slice and the campaign is one systemd-run unit (detach.sh). Nice therefore applies across all 3 nodes' threads, and autogroup does not apply.\n\nNot tried before: docs/perf/s64-exploration-2026-09-24.md:147 says \"Priority changes (nice, SCHED_BATCH) have never been tried\", and line 195 lists renice of verify/rpc/rocksdb:low at an estimated +3-8% from the consensus-bound era.", "est_gain": "Exec run delay about 8.4 -> 4-5 ms per block, plus faster pool starts and less mid-chunk preemption (-3 to -7 ms per block), plus the ckpt-merge and end-resident helpers. Total about -5 to -12 ms per native block, giving +1.5 to +3.5% matched/s at 300m and at 10m depth 4 (exec-bound). 10m depth 1 neutral, since it is paced by the bench tail. Small block-p99 help from the hotstuff and exec tails. CPU-s/1M about 0, possibly slightly lower from fewer context switches. This overlaps with exec-pool-fine-grain-jobs on the pool part.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 2, "confidence": "low", "determinism": "Thread priority only. No code path, ordering or state output changes. A node-local flag; a mixed fleet is safe. Flag unset: no setpriority call, so behaviour is identical to main.", "flag": "TORUS_BULK_NICE=N (1..19, unset = off; main arm 10, secondary 5). Optional TORUS_ROCKSDB_LOW_CPU_PRI=1 (RocksDB LOW pool at kLow).", "impl_size": "small: about 40-70 lines (env parse, a shared nice helper using libc::setpriority, two ThreadPoolBuilder start_handlers, optional rocksdb Env call), plus a unit test", "test_plan": "1) Unit test: with the flag set, getpriority in a pool thread returns N; with it unset, 0. The exec pool and global rayon pool threads stay at 0.\n2) A/B:\n- Shape: 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=4.\n- Arms: unset vs N=10 (then N=5 if positive).\n- Report: matched/s, native blk/s, exec block_ms, exec and hotstuff run_delay (schedstat.json), phase_match/pass_a ms, checkpoint_settle_wait, log-based block p99 in and out of windows, timeouts, CPU-s/1M, RSS, AGREE every cell.\n- Guards:\n  - block_build_select and mempool_remove_committed p99 (priority inversion on the native pool lock);\n  - rpc_submit_verify p99;\n  - exec verify ms and exec-path trust-cache misses;\n  - a new counter for gossip ingests that fell back inline (in-flight >= 1024).\n3) 10m no-regression cells at depth 1 and depth 4.\n4) Optional host-level diagnostic, not a candidate: one pair with sysctl kernel.sched_bore=0 to confirm the BORE-penalty premise.", "novelty_check": "Not tried before:\n- Listed but never run in s64 section 5 (renice/SCHED_BATCH verify, rpc, rocksdb:low). s64 section 3 states priority changes were never tried. s65 explorers echoed it on the 18-CPU host when the chain was consensus-bound.\n\nNot an AVOID item. Differs from the refuted ones:\n- s75 cpuset pinning (-5.7%) and M200k bench-env-bounded-pools (-67.7%) both capped each node's cores. Nice removes no capacity; it only orders contention.\n- HOTSTUFF_CPUS (s46) pinned the consensus thread.\n- None of the 4 s123 branches or the 3 recovered s123 refutations touch thread priority.\n\nWhat changed since s64: the chain is exec-bound, so the critical threads are the exec thread and exec pool, not consensus. archy runs BORE, which penalises the exec thread's long bursts. Exec run delay (2.5-2.9%) and pool preemption (0.56-0.69 s/s) are now measured.", "risks": "- Priority inversion: niced verify threads hold the mempool native write lock (lib.rs:979-1009) that the consensus thread needs for select and remove_committed. Watch their p99 and timeouts.\n- A gossip-verify backlog over 1024 falls back to inline verify on a tokio networking thread.\n- If gossip verify lags, exec trust-cache misses rise, putting ecrecover on the critical path. Unlikely with about 19 s of exec lag.\n- RPC submit latency goes up.\n- Under BORE, N=5 may not overcome the exec thread's penalty; use N=10 first.\n- Other nodes' pool threads still outrank the exec thread, which may cap the gain.\n- In production with one node per host, the effect differs (less contention)."}], "notes": "Lens: locks, thread pools, scheduling and tail stalls. Main a44000e0, read-only. Analysis used only existing artifacts; nothing was run. Two candidates, best first.\n\nChecked and dropped, with numbers:\n- **Lock contention on the exec critical path is negligible.**\n  - BlockSums/BatchSums memo Mutex and OnceLock: 0.16-0.21% of exec wall (p4s0 step0-table.txt).\n  - Overlay RwLock (is_read_lockable): about 1% of pool CPU (cloidec prof-B).\n  - Exec-thread kernel time: 0.6% of its samples (p4s0 W1).\n  - Trust-cache RwLock<FifoCache>: read-only lookups, trivial write holds.\n- **Rayon idle spin is small on current main.** The 32 global-pool namesake threads (\"torus-execution\" non-main) use 0.025 cores per node (hsdb io-threads). The large sched_yield share seen in the p4s0 perf window is inflated by perf. The exec pool's sys share is 2-3%.\n- **Mempool write-lock convoy among verify threads is real but off the 300m critical path.** Verify threads were blocked 1.84 s/s in submit_native_action_inner (p4j3 off-CPU). The consensus thread's select/remove_committed means are 18-21 ms and 7 ms.\n- **Exec queue handoff, flush handoff (0.01 ms) and end-resident wait (0.3 ms) are negligible.**\n- **ckpt-merge spawns a fresh thread per block** (app.rs:359-386). Settle wait is 8.5-9.4 ms per block, but it scales with layer size (3.7-4.2 ms in 120 s cells), so it is merge work. defer-book-blobs (AVOID) covers it.\n- **THP:** defrag=madvise; compact_stall 716 in 23 h.\n- **Shrinking pools (fewer exec-pool threads) is not proposed.** It conflicts with the refuted bounded-pools (-67.7%) and SAVE_BOOKS_WORKERS=4 (-34%).\n- **Exec verify phase 1** (global-pool par_iter with about 100% hits): left alone. It is the s123 exec-action-keys-parallel area and the \"tiny gain\" revisit item.\n\n**Data caveat:** the pool timeline evidence comes from d3cba5e2 (P2-3 pool and the same chunking as main) recorded under perf. Re-check the start skew on main with a cheap per-phase timer before the A/B.\n\n**Order:** the two candidates overlap on the pool phases. A/B exec-pool-fine-grain-jobs first, then bulk-thread-nice on top.\n\n**Analysis scripts:** /tmp/torus-explore/{pooltl,phases,gaps,startend,disp,model,thr,hist,kern,grp,excats}.py.\n\n**Memory saved:** project:torus-hyperbft, lens:locks-pools-scheduling (exec-pool start skew, run-delay and preemption data, BORE premise)."}, "research:r1:exec-pipeline": {"candidates": [{"slug": "parallel-classic-book-save", "title": "Serialize the Classic book blobs on the exec pool, into presized buffers, instead of one at a time on the exec thread", "mechanism": "Classic is the book mode on the bench and on testnet. In it, save_order_books (native_executor.rs:6252-6279) runs borsh::to_vec(&book) for every dirty market, one after another, on the exec thread. Each Vec starts at 1 KiB and grows by doubling. The blob then goes in with put_cf_raw_owned, and the book's row and level journals are thrown away. Modes 2/3 already save in parallel (drain_books_parallel, :2864-2925), but Classic never takes that path (:6245-6251 sets last_save_workers = 1).\n\nWith TORUS_PARALLEL_BOOK_SAVE=N, N >= 2:\n1. Take &mut handles to the dirty books in one iter_mut pass, sorted by market id, exactly as drain_level_books does.\n2. Each book becomes one work item on the exec pool, heaviest first. The item computes the exact serialized length in a pre-pass (Order is 97 B, +16 B with a cloid, +16 or +32 B for stop types), allocates a Vec of that size, serializes into it, and drops the book's journals.\n3. Back on the exec thread, put_cf_raw_owned runs in ascending market order with today's fault latching and logging, followed by the order-id counter and mode-marker writes.\n\nOptional sub-flag: Classic books stop keeping the row and level journals at all (journal sites in torus-core order_book.rs:2117-3102). Today every block builds them and save throws them away.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:6206-6359 (save_order_books; Classic arm 6252-6279; Classic forced serial at 6245-6251)", "crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:2618-2696, 2864-2925 (mode-2/3 parallel drain pattern and its TORUS_SAVE_BOOKS_WORKERS parse, to mirror)", "crates/torus-bridge/src/exec_pool.rs:20-59 (pool, run_jobs)", "crates/torus-core/src/order_book.rs:4309-4352, 4577-4621 (Order and OrderBook BorshSerialize; exact-size helper goes here)", "crates/torus-core/src/order_book.rs:3950-3962 (discard_level_ops / discard_row_ops)", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:3965-4006 (save_books call site and timer)"], "evidence": "Timers on current main code (save_books per native block):\n- 300m 600 s, ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r{1,2,3}-B (crates identical to main a44000e0): 20.7-21.7 ms, which is 6.9% of exec wall. The last 60 s run 23.4-25.9 ms at 604-719k resting orders; the first 60 s run 9-11 ms.\n- 300m 120 s, ozarchy-p4j4h-300m-r*-A (main minus the own-DB split): 10.0-11.7 ms of 211-226 ms blocks.\n- 10m depth 4, ozarchy-p4tail10-10m-*4: 30.1-36.0 ms of 244-251 ms blocks (12-14% of exec wall).\n- 10m depth 1: 3.4-4.2 ms of 60-72 ms blocks.\n\nProfile: d3cba5e2, 300m 120 s, val1, W1 (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p{1,2}-mperf-w1-exec.script.gz):\n- save_order_books is 5.26-5.48% of exec on-CPU samples, about 10 ms per block. This agrees with the 11 ms timer.\n- Inside it: Order and OrderBook serialize about 59%, Vec growth (do_rallocx memcpy) 13.5-15%, journal BTreeSet drops 9-11%, jemalloc extent work a few %.\n\nWhy it can run in parallel:\n- Each book is serialized independently, and the result depends only on that book.\n- The exec pool's 32 threads per node are idle at this point, because match and pass A have already finished.\n\nNever done for Classic. Section 36 notes only that Classic has no drain/write split, and Phase 5 step 3 still serializes Classic books at freeze.", "est_gain": "The serialization is memory-bandwidth bound: about 97 B per resting order, so about 70 MB of blobs per block late in 600 s cells. Expect a 2.5-3.5x speedup on serialization, plus about 15% from dropping the realloc growth, with the journal drops running in parallel too.\n\nsave_books, by regime:\n- 300m 600 s: 21.7 to about 6-8 ms (-14 to -16 ms per native block).\n- 300m 120 s gate shape: 10.5 to about 3-4 ms (-6.5 to -7.5 ms).\n- 10m depth 4: 30-36 to about 9-12 ms (-20 to -25 ms).\n- 10m depth 1: about -2 to -3 ms.\n\nUsing matched/s = busy x fills per exec-second, with a closed-loop discount:\n- 300m 120 s: +2-3.5% matched/s.\n- 300m 600 s: +3-5%.\n- 10m depth 4: +5-9%.\n- 10m depth 1: about 0-2% (the bench tail paces it).\n\nNative blk/s rises by about the same factor. CPU-s/1M is neutral to slightly better (no realloc copies).", "est_gain_pct_matched": 3, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "Serialization depends only on each book, so the blob bytes are identical. The overlay puts run serially in the same ascending market order, so the frozen set, the running hash h_n, the checkpoint layer L and the checkpoints are byte-identical. The pending set is keyed anyway.\n\nFault handling is unchanged: a put fault latches in the same order, and a to_vec error logs and skips. A panic in a job resumes unwinding on the exec thread, the same outcome as a serial panic (execution_loop catch_unwind, then fail-stop).\n\nWith the flag unset, the code path is today's serial loop.", "flag": "TORUS_PARALLEL_BOOK_SAVE=N: unset, 0 or 1 = today's serial loop; N >= 2 = parallel with N workers. Presizing is bundled under the same flag. Optional TORUS_CLASSIC_NO_JOURNAL=1 for the journal skip. All default off and node-local.", "impl_size": "S-M: about 120-200 lines (Classic arm, env parse mirroring parse_save_books_workers, exact-size helper in torus-core) plus a differential test and a Classic microbench. The journal skip adds about 40 lines in torus-core.", "test_plan": "Tests:\n- Differential, flag unset vs N = 2/4/8, on random Classic block sequences (places, cancels, cancel-alls, fills, stop-limits, modifies; resident books and reload). Per-block write sets and h_n, running hash and full CF dumps must be equal, and every blob must equal borsh::to_vec(book).\n- Re-run reference_paths_match_production_classic_* (app.rs) and order_book_persist_tests with the flag on.\n- Exact-size helper: length equals the serialized length on random books with cloids and stops.\n- A panic planted in one book's job ends the same way as the serial path.\n\nMicrobench before any node cells: a Classic variant of tests/l3_savebooks_ubench.rs with 300 books x 2.4k orders and 10 books x 67k orders, serial vs N = 4/8/16, presize on and off.\n\narchy, one bench at a time, quiet host:\n- 300m 120 s gate shape, ABBA n=4, unset vs =8.\n- One 600 s pair, n=3, for the deep-book regime.\n- 10m depth 1, n=4, to show no regression; 10m depth 4, n=2, for information.\n- Report: save_books ms, block_ms, fills per block, engine ms per 1k fills, matched/s with paired CI, native blk/s, CPU-s/1M (ANCOVA), RSS mean and peak, block p99, timeouts. AGREE in every cell.", "novelty_check": "Not found in any of these:\n- the prior-work brief, including the 10 recovered s123 names;\n- the 4 exp/s123 notes files (none mention the save path);\n- memory search_knowledge for Classic save, parallel and borsh;\n- docs/perf (only the mode-2 save-books-parallel-levels r1 work, the deferred pass 2 for modes 2/3, and the refuted move of pass 1 off-thread across blocks);\n- the s65 explorer JSONs (mode-3 drain ideas only);\n- git log and branches.\n\nPhase 5 step 3 (origin/docs/item6-phase5-plan) keeps Classic serialization at freeze, so this is complementary and its helper can be reused there.\n\nResidual risk: 11 of the 14 refuted s123 candidates are not recovered by name.", "metric_targets": ["save_books_ms", "exec block_ms", "matched/s", "native blk/s", "RSS"], "regime": "300m (largest in 600 s and deep-book cells) and 10m (largest at tail depth 4; small at depth 1)", "risks": "- Memory bandwidth, shared with the other 2 nodes, sets the speedup. Validate with the microbench first.\n- RSS: blobs would come from the pool threads' jemalloc arenas instead of the exec thread's. Watch RSS; to avoid it, allocate the presized buffers on the exec thread, or cap N at 8.\n- Pool wake-up delay limits the gain if static run_jobs chunks are used. Per-book items help (see dynamic-exec-pool-items).\n- Textual overlap with Phase 5 step 3 (Classic rows at freeze) and with the s123 cancel-all branch in native_executor.rs. Coordinate with 18c."}, {"slug": "parallel-match-prologue", "title": "Build each market's matching inputs (reduce-only positions, account margins, requests) inside its pool job, not serially on the exec thread", "mechanism": "Phase 3 of execute_batch_phases (native_executor.rs:7611-7714) builds every market's matching input serially on the exec thread before the books go to the pool. Per prepared order it does:\n- an insert into ReduceOnlyPositions (a HashMap with no presize, so it rehashes);\n- an AccountMargins get plus insert or insert_taker_only (also a HashMap with no presize);\n- taker_margin_limit (an Arc clone plus reserve_price);\n- a push into the MatchRequest Vec.\nPer market it also calls reduce_only_positions_for, margin_tiers and set_mark.\n\nEvery input is read-only and belongs to one market:\n- the AccountReader is Sync (MakerAccountSource: Sync) and the match workers already share it;\n- markets, pools, margin_configs and market_batches are immutable here, and pass A already shares market_batches with pool jobs.\n\nWith TORUS_PARALLEL_MATCH_PREP=1:\n- The exec thread only takes the books, and creates the rare missing book from market_shape.\n- A new MarketWorkerPool entry point takes a Sync per-market prep closure. Each job builds its market's ReduceOnlyPositions and AccountMargins, presized to prepared.len(), and its requests in the same per-market order, then calls match_market.\n- Optionally the job also clears both maps after matching and returns the charged-maker-fill count, which takes those drops off the exec thread.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7611-7714 (serial prologue loop), 7726-7731 (match_parallel_with call), 7771-7796 (epilogue)", "crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:10048-10074 (reduce_only_positions_for), 9494-9515 (margin_tiers, taker_margin_limit), 9788-9800 (d2_taker_px), 2010-2020 (MakerAccountSource for AccountReader)", "crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:115-195 (match_parallel_capped_with; add a prep-closure variant), 293-330 (process_chunk, run_contained)", "crates/torus-core/src/order_book.rs:423-430 (ReduceOnlyPositions), 655-760 (AccountMargins, insert_taker_only; add with_capacity constructors), 801 (MakerAccountSource: Sync)"], "evidence": "Profile: d3cba5e2, 300m 120 s, val1, W1 (ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p{1,2}-mperf-w1-exec-inline.script.gz).\n- Exec-thread samples in the prologue are 4.53% and 4.08% of exec on-CPU: ReduceOnly inserts 1.41-1.61%, AccountMargins 1.30-1.50%, taker_margin_limit 1.02-1.08%, MatchRequest 0.29-0.40%.\n- HashMap reserve_rehash alone is about 1.27%.\n- At 188 ms of exec on-CPU per block (section 64.2), that is about 7.7-8.5 ms per native block, at about 65k placed orders per block. That is about 125 ns per placed order.\n\nThe prologue samples come right before the exec thread's off-CPU match wait: the gaps from prologue samples to the next samples are p50 17-21 ms, n = 107-112 per cell (p1 and p2-mperf-w1-all).\n\nThe prologue code on main a44000e0 diffs identical to d3cba5e2 except take_book_for_matching, which is a plain remove.\n\nPlaced orders per native block, from metrics:\n- main 300m 600 s (hsdb r1-B): 89.9k, so about 11 ms. Match timer 31.4 ms.\n- main-like 120 s (p4j4h r1-A): 63k, so about 8 ms. Match timer 21.4-23.2 ms.\n- 10m depth 4: about 100k, so about 12 ms. Match timer 35-37 ms.", "est_gain": "About 8-11 ms per block leaves the exec thread. The pool's critical path gains about 1 ms at 300m (about 10x effective parallelism; small per-market maps, compute-bound, built where they are used) and about 1.2 ms at 10m (10 markets).\n\nNet per native block:\n- 300m 120 s: -7 ms.\n- 300m 600 s: -9 to -10 ms.\n- 10m depth 4: -10 to -11 ms.\n- 10m depth 1: about -3.5 ms.\n\nmatched/s:\n- 300m: +2-3%.\n- 10m depth 4: +3-4%.\n- 10m depth 1: small but positive.\n\nPresizing alone, serially, would recover only about 30% of this.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 2.5, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Each market's maps and requests are built from the same immutable inputs and in the same order as today, so they are identical. Output order is unchanged: market results are still sorted by market id before settlement, AccountMargins is never iterated, and request order follows prepared order.\n\nReader fault latching is a OnceLock that the match workers already race today. It only affects which message wins on a node-local fatal path.\n\nWith the flag unset, the path is today's.", "flag": "TORUS_PARALLEL_MATCH_PREP=1 (default off, node-local)", "impl_size": "M: about 200-300 lines (prep closure plumbing in market_workers.rs, prologue split, with_capacity constructors in torus-core) plus differential tests.", "test_plan": "Tests:\n- Differential, flag off vs on, on random batches: reduce-only resting and incoming orders, checked and unchecked takers, D2 pools and taker-only markets, maker checks, stop-limits, a missing book, cloid orders. Results, state, h_n and CF dumps must be identical, across pinned worker caps.\n- Re-run tests/perf_equivalence_golden.rs goldens A and B, parallel_matching_tests, m1_correctness_tests and account_margin_tests with the flag on.\n- debug_assert_index_fed must still hold.\n\narchy:\n- 300m 120 s, ABBA n=4. Expect phase_match_ms -25 to -35% and block_ms -3%.\n- 10m depth 1, n=4 (no regression); 10m depth 4, n=2.\n- Report: matched/s with paired CI, native blk/s, engine ms per 1k fills, CPU-s/1M, timeouts. AGREE in every cell.", "novelty_check": "Not in the prior-work brief, the s123 notes or the exec/matching lens memory b6d2198b (its 4 candidates were cancel-all, PARALLEL_ENGINE, prep stage and liq scan).\n\nTORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE shards Phase 2 only and leaves this prologue serial: the prologue code does not read engine_threads.\n\nNot found in memory search (match prologue, AccountMargins, ReduceOnlyPositions), docs/perf, s65 explorer JSONs, git log or branches. Phase 5 does not touch Phase 3.\n\nResidual risk: 11 refuted s123 candidates are unrecovered.", "metric_targets": ["phase_match_ms", "exec block_ms", "matched/s", "native blk/s"], "regime": "300m and 10m (both exec-bound shapes); small at 10m tail depth 1", "risks": "- reduce_only_positions_for overlay reads now run concurrently. They are rare in the bench, since most orders carry pre_pos, and maker reads are already concurrent today.\n- Textual conflict in market_workers.rs and native_executor.rs with the s123 cancel-all branch.\n- The test-only shadow check runs in the workers (Mutex, fine).\n- If blocks shrink in the closed loop, the matched/s gain is smaller than the block_ms gain."}, {"slug": "dynamic-exec-pool-items", "title": "Hand out per-market work items dynamically (heaviest first, exec thread joins) instead of one static LPT chunk per pool thread", "mechanism": "exec_pool::run_jobs (exec_pool.rs:43-59) runs one job per static LPT chunk. The chunk count equals the worker cap, which equals the pool size (32 per node on archy): match at market_workers.rs:146-151 and 208-222; pass A at native_executor.rs:8672-8701 via settle_worker_cap at :579-583. Meanwhile the exec thread blocks inside pool().scope.\n\nWith 3 nodes sharing 32 hardware threads, pool threads wake and run at uneven times. A late or preempted thread holds its whole chunk, so the phase ends long after most threads have gone idle.\n\nWith TORUS_EXEC_POOL_DYNAMIC=1, a new helper `exec_pool::run_items_dynamic` does this:\n1. Hand out per-market items from an AtomicUsize cursor, heaviest first, using the same weights as today: request count for match, orders + 2 x fills for pass A.\n2. Run inside ThreadPool::in_place_scope (rayon-core 1.13.0). The exec thread starts on items at t=0 and up to cap-1 pool helpers join as they wake.\n3. Store results by item index: match results are re-sorted by market id as today, pass A plans by index.\n\nUse it for match and pass A, and for the parallel book save and the s123 cancel-all pass if they are adopted.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-bridge/src/exec_pool.rs:20-59 (pool size = max(host, caps); run_jobs = pool.scope + one spawn per chunk)", "crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:139-195 (chunks = min(cap, markets), LPT assign_chunks 260-285, resolve_worker_cap_named 208-222)", "crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:8621-8717 (settle pass A chunk_indices + run_jobs), 579-583 (settle_worker_cap)", "crates/torus-bridge/src/panic_scope.rs (catch_recoverable for pass A items run on torus-execution)", "~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/rayon-core-1.13.0/src/thread_pool/mod.rs:311 (ThreadPool::in_place_scope)"], "evidence": "Source: pool-thread sample timing in ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p{1,2}-mperf-w1-all.script.gz. That is d3cba5e2, 300m 120 s, val1, W1, with about 1.9 ms per sample per running thread. There are 111-113 match clusters and 108-109 pass A clusters per cell.\n\nMatch:\n- The first sample of the p90 thread comes 5.6-6.5 ms after the phase's first sample (p50 cluster).\n- The median thread's last sample is at 9.9-10.8 ms, while the phase spans 16.3-18.0 ms.\n- 20-21% of match samples fall after the median thread has finished.\n\nPass A:\n- The phase spans 9.9-12.2 ms and the median thread is done at 6.0-7.1 ms.\n- 23% of samples fall after the median thread has finished.\n\nIdeal dynamic finish (same thread start times, same work):\n- match: -7.4 to -7.6 ms p50 (mean -10.5 to -10.8);\n- pass A: -3.9 to -4.7 ms p50 (mean -5.9 to -6.7).\n\nCross-checks:\n- The exec thread's match wait gaps (p50 17-21 ms) agree with these spans.\n- Pool work is about 250 thread-ms per block for match and about 165 for pass A.\n- Pool-thread idle spinning is small (about 12% of pool samples outside jobs).\n- The s114 P2-3 wake-path check (memory b9bd776c) costed only the futex wake (tens of µs), not run-queue latency on the loaded host.\n\nCurrent main has the same pool and chunking: match 31.4 ms and pass A 10.8 ms (300m 600 s); 21.4-23.2 and 7.2-8.0 ms (120 s).", "est_gain": "Assume 40-60% of the ideal is realized. At 300m that is -4 to -8 ms per native block across match and pass A (-1.5 to -2.5% of exec wall), so about +1-2.5% matched/s and the same in native blk/s.\n\n10m: no gain. With 10 markets there are already only 10 items; the exec thread joining may save about 0.5 ms.\n\nThis adds to parallel-match-prologue, which removes serial time; this candidate removes the parallel tail.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 1.5, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Scheduling only. Each market's match and settle plan is independent and depends only on its own inputs, which the existing differential tests already show for any worker cap. Outputs are placed by item index, and the market-id sort and pass B order are unchanged.\n\nPanics:\n- Containment per market is unchanged (run_contained).\n- A match panic is fatal either way, including when it runs on torus-execution (the fail-stop hook exits, the same outcome as today; see the panic_scope docs).\n- Pass A items keep catch_recoverable, so the sequential fallback still works when an item runs on the exec thread.\n\nWith the flag unset, run_jobs and the static chunks are used as today.", "flag": "TORUS_EXEC_POOL_DYNAMIC=1 (default off, node-local), with an optional item cap TORUS_EXEC_POOL_DYNAMIC_WORKERS", "impl_size": "M: about 150-250 lines (helper in exec_pool.rs, use sites in market_workers.rs and pass A) plus tests. Best done on one branch with parallel-match-prologue, under separate flags.", "test_plan": "Tests:\n- Differential, flag off vs on, at worker caps 1..64: existing execute_batch_settle_workers, parallel_matching_tests, parallel_settle_tests and engine_parallel_tests. Shuffling the item order must give identical results and state.\n- A planted panicking market gives the same MarketWorkerPanic (fatal), or the same pass A sequential fallback, including when the item runs on the exec thread.\n- Item results land at the right index.\n\narchy:\n- 300m 120 s, ABBA n=4. Report phase_match_ms, settle_pass_a_ms, block_ms, matched/s with paired CI, native blk/s, CPU-s/1M.\n- One perf-sidecar cell (cycles:u, val1) to re-run the tail analysis: the start/finish spread and the share of work after the median thread finishes, using the scratch scripts /tmp/explore-s124/scripts/mc2.py and mc3.py (copy them into the campaign tools).\n- 10m depth 1, n=4, to show no regression.\n- If the 120 s CI does not resolve it, add a 600 s pair.", "novelty_check": "Not in the prior-work brief:\n- P2-3 (merged) kept per-site static chunking on purpose.\n- The TORUS_MATCH_WORKERS / TORUS_SETTLE_WORKERS knob item is about caps, which also resize the pool (max), not about item granularity.\n- The bench-env-bounded-pools refutation (M200k section 6, -67.7%) was a whole-node core budget, cpusets and tokio sizing.\n\nNot found in memory (thread pool wake path, dynamic chunking), docs/perf, the s123 notes (the cancel-all branch reuses run_jobs with static chunks), the s65 explorer JSONs or git.", "metric_targets": ["phase_match_ms", "settle_pass_a_ms", "exec block_ms", "matched/s"], "regime": "300m only (10m has 10 markets)", "risks": "- The gain depends on how busy the host is; the ideal estimate is an upper bound.\n- Per-item overhead: about 300 items at about 1 µs each.\n- Running match code on the exec thread must keep the panic semantics described.\n- More pool threads busy at once can slightly slow the other two nodes' threads; total CPU is unchanged.\n- Overlaps the code of parallel-match-prologue and the s123 cancel-all branch (both use run_jobs)."}], "notes": "I found three candidates, all new. Per-block timings are from the current-main-code cells (ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r{1,2,3}-B, 600 s; ozarchy-p4j4h A, 120 s; ozarchy-p4tail10, 10m). Profile shares are from the d3cba5e2 perf dumps (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/*.script.gz, 300m 120 s, val1, W1). No profile with call stacks exists for a44000e0, so the profile-based numbers are extrapolated.\n\nI checked that the matching prologue and the Classic save code on main are the same as on d3cba5e2 (the prologue by diff). I read the dumps with my own Python scripts in /tmp/explore-s124/scripts. That directory is temporary, so copy the scripts if you want to keep them. No cargo, perf record or bench was run.\n\n- **How the three combine:** at 300m 600 s, all three together would cut about 25-34 ms per native block, 8-11% of exec wall. They are 1 = book save, 2 = matching prologue, 3 = dynamic pool items.\n  - 2 and 3 edit the same matching dispatch, so build them on one branch with separate flags.\n  - 1 should use 3's helper if it exists, else run_jobs with LPT chunks.\n  - All three touch native_executor.rs and market_workers.rs, which the s123 cancel-all branch also edits.\n  - 1 overlaps Phase 5 step 3 later. Phase 5 still serializes Classic books at freeze, so the parallel helper carries over.\n- **What else the exec thread spends time on** (current main, 300m 600 s, ~313 ms per native block): verify 16.8, checkpoint settle wait 8.7, phase 1 38.7, margin 62.1, pass B 43.3, cache flush 25.9 and liquidation tail 11.9 ms. All of these belong to s123, Phase 5 or refuted work, so I did not propose anything for them.\n  - residual_untimed (22 ms) is mostly the s123 sort-key Keccak (deferred) and the action clone in the replay guard (~1.6 ms). The rest is broken-stack Keccak/libc samples and exec-thread waits for the pool and locks.\n  - The exec thread's on-CPU work outside the timed phases is only ~3% of its samples.\n- **Ideas I looked at but did not propose:**\n  - Lower OS priority for the gossip and ingress verify pools (exec-thread run-queue wait is 8.4 ms per block from schedstat). Effect under the BORE scheduler is uncertain, and it risks a regression at 10m.\n  - Dropping per-block engine structures off the exec thread: about 2.5 ms per block, too small.\n  - Skipping Classic journal upkeep: about 1-2 ms per block; kept as an optional sub-flag of the book-save candidate.\n- **Noise:** at 300m 120 s, n=4 ABBA (CI ±1.3%), candidates 1 and 2 should resolve; candidate 3 is borderline (add a 600 s pair). Check RSS on the book-save candidate's 600 s cells.\n- **Novelty limit:** 11 of the 14 refuted s123 candidates are not recovered by name, so I cannot fully rule out overlap with them.\n- **Memory saved:** the summary of these findings (tags project:torus-hyperbft, lens:exec-pipeline)."}, "research:r1:network-da": {"candidates": [{"slug": "bench-push-manifest-not-full-body", "title": "Send hash manifests instead of full-body pre-proposal pushes (gossip pre-spread already delivers every body)", "mechanism": "Each body now reaches every validator twice. Native gossip pre-spread is on by default (torus-node main.rs:180-192), and the bench env also pins TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD=8000000 (devnet/wsl/env.sh:45; the compiled production default is 512 KB at bridge.rs:97), so every leader also pushes the full bincode body set (~5 MB, 390 actions) to both followers.\n\nWhat each pushed copy costs:\n- Leader: serialize, then a per-peer borsh copy and zstd compress.\n- Follower, on the swarm loop: zstd decompress and a bincode decode of the whole batch (swarm.rs:1661-1679).\n- Then add_native_action_from_gossip runs on the gossip-verify pool and does all of this BEFORE it finds the duplicate (mempool lib.rs:792-835, 1351-1379; native_pool.rs:414-426):\n  - mirror_to_da: a deep clone, then put_batch (compute_action_hash + bincode serialize + a RocksDB put);\n  - verified_cache_key (a canonical-bytes Keccak);\n  - a pool write lock, plus compute_action_hash and serialized_size under that lock.\n\nWith a hash manifest (0xFC, ~12.8 KB), followers run plan_prewarm_requests (swarm.rs:2610-2646): they check DA presence and pull only the missing bodies, from the proposer, on the off-loop serve path. That is the design the CLI doc describes: 'compact proposals need only gap-pulls'.\n\nStep 1 needs no code (an A/B of the existing knob). Optional step 2:\n- 2a: reuse the leader's already-computed native_hashes in the bundle and skip the deep clone in mirror_or_drop_native (app.rs:6628-6629).\n- 2b: if the owner keeps the full push as a WAN safety net, return Duplicate before mirror and Keccak work whenever (sender, nonce) is pooled with identical canonical bytes.", "code_locations": ["devnet/wsl/env.sh:45", "crates/torus-network/src/bridge.rs:97-182,431-446", "crates/torus-network/src/swarm.rs:660,1521-1547,1661-1704,2513-2531,2610-2646,2654-2745", "crates/torus-node/src/main.rs:180-192,827-876,895-904", "crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:141-143,792-900,1351-1379", "crates/torus-mempool/src/native_pool.rs:408-426", "crates/torus-state/src/native_da.rs:97-123", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:6617-6667,7184-7198"], "evidence": "Main-code 300m 600 s cells (ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B, crates byte-identical to a44000e0).\n\nPush volume, from the 'received pre-proposal action batch' and 'pre-proposal FULL-BODY push' log lines:\n- Each node receives 1,202-1,206 full-body batches per cell = 468-471k actions (avg 390 actions, 4.9-5.6 MB). There are 0 manifests.\n- Direct-path zstd counter: 5.7-6.5 GB pre-compression vs 0.47-0.54 GB on the wire per node.\n\nRedundancy:\n- Every pushed action is also delivered by gossip or by the node's own RPC (val1):\n  - trust-cache misses 471,202 = native gossip received 471,199;\n  - hits 1,177,549 = 470,627 push copies + ~706.9k exec lookups.\n  So the ~470k push copies per node are duplicate ingests.\n- The push can only beat gossip when the leader picks its own RPC-admitted action within the 50 ms gossip batch window (NATIVE_BATCH_INTERVAL_MS, swarm.rs:660). Order age at commit is under 0.32 s for only 0.68% of actions (histogram).\n\nDA write amplification:\n- DA puts per node = 235.5k RPC + 471.2k gossip + 470.6k push = 1.18M for 707k distinct actions. Push duplicates are ~40% of consensus-DB WAL bytes (25 MB/s).\n- rocksdb-consensus-LOG: 934k cf_native_pending entries flushed (~244k duplicates collapsed in memtables) and 216k dropped by compaction, so ~24% of flushed DA entries are duplicates.\n\nCPU, from p3cloidprof 300m val1 folded stacks:\n- mirror_to_da 0.344 ms/1k fills (half is duplicates);\n- verified_cache_key encode 0.116, admit/insert 0.114-0.138, decode_pre_proposal_batch 0.049, zstd 0.107 ms/1k.\n- Keccak: a duplicate costs 3 x ~122 Keccak permutations (16.4 KB canonical bytes) vs ~1,666 for a first copy (1,200 EIP-712 item hashes + 95 + 3 x 122). That is ~18% of gossip-verify Keccak (2.19 ms/1k).\n- gossip-verify uses 0.94-0.97 cores/node (io-threads).\n\n10m is the same (p4tail10 a1/a4: direct pre 3.5-4.1 GB per node per 300 s cell).\n\nHistory:\n- s297 made the unicast push the delivery path while gossip was off.\n- r4 raised the bench floor to 8 MB to avoid manifests, with no manifest-vs-push A/B. r3 cap300 ran ~45 manifests per node with 0 exhausted or starved.", "est_gain": "About -0.2 to -0.26 cores per node:\n- gossip-verify about -0.17;\n- swarm decode and zstd about -0.03;\n- leader encode about -0.01;\n- DA compaction share about -0.045;\n- minus about +0.02 for manifest hashing and the presence checks.\n\nThat is CPU-s/1M -3 to -5% (about -0.7 to -1.1 of 23.38) at 300m, with a similar share at 10m.\n\nI/O:\n- consensus-DB WAL -40% (about -10 MB/s per node);\n- DA flush and compaction input about -24%;\n- device writes about -14 MB/s per node (-16% of 85).\n\nAlso ~5-6 ms per received batch leaves the follower's swarm loop, and the leader's per-peer zstd leaves the connection tasks.\n\nmatched/s: 0 to +1% from less host contention, not resolvable.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.5, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Transport-only and node-local. Receivers handle both 0xFD and 0xFC whatever the proposer chose (bridge.rs:99-106 documents per-node A/B safety).\n\nBodies still reach the DA store via gossip, RPC or the pre-warm and hot-path pulls before vote-after-body. Block contents and state are unchanged, so AGREE must hold.\n\nStep 1 is an existing knob at its compiled production default. Step 2 flags would default off, with off identical to main.", "flag": "Step 1: TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD=512000 (existing knob at its compiled production default; the bench env pins 8000000). Optional third arm: 0.\nOptional step 2, new flag, default off: TORUS_PUSH_MANIFEST_HASHES=1 (reuse native_hashes, skip the leader clone).", "impl_size": "Step 1: zero code. Step 2: S (~30-60 lines in app.rs PreProposalBundle, bridge.rs and mempool, plus tests).", "test_plan": "Setup: 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=4. Arm A = env.sh default (8000000); arm M = TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD=512000 on all 3 validators. Add one 10m depth-1 pair, 120 s.\n\nPrimary metrics:\n- CPU-s/1M;\n- per-thread CPU from io-threads (torus-gossip-verify, tokio-rt-worker, rocksdb:low);\n- consensus-DB wal/flush/compact bytes and compaction_cpu from sampler.csv;\n- io-ticks device writes.\n\nGuards:\n- matched/s and native blk/s;\n- block p99 in and out of window;\n- consensus timeouts;\n- view_vote_delay and insert_persist;\n- validate_block_da_wait/flush;\n- gap pulls: follower 'pre-warm pull of MISSING bodies' lines with count, and the proposer's torus_native_da_served_total / serve_dropped;\n- harness total_body_pushes should be ~0 in M;\n- AGREE in every cell.\n\nPass: CPU-s/1M CI below 0, matched/s lower CI bound >= -1%, timeouts not up, pulled bodies < 1% of actions.", "novelty_check": "The brief lists only the knob, as [revisit?] TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD (512 KB prod vs 8 MB bench), never benched. 'Header-first ... hash-only push (merged)' covers the mechanism, not this setting.\n\nSearched:\n- memory: S387 starvation, S388 WAN pulls, r3/r4 floor lift, s297 gossip-off correction;\n- docs/perf body-* and s72;\n- git history.\n\nNothing measures the push/gossip overlap or the DA double write.\n\nWhat changed since the 8 MB tuning:\n- pre-spread is on by default, so every body already arrives by gossip;\n- the S387 fixes landed (off-loop serve, missing-only pre-warm), which removed the 'all followers pull all bodies' wedge;\n- a44000e0's separate consensus-DB counters make the duplicate WAL and compaction cost visible.\n\nNot an AVOID item: it is not parallel DA decode, flush-on-miss, consensus hash reuse or a parent-path change.", "risks": "- WAN robustness: S388 shows a follower whose gossip mesh is broken pulls every body from the proposer. Production at 512 KB already behaves this way; keep the override, or take step 2b, if the WAN safety net matters.\n- Actions the leader selects within ~50 ms of its own RPC admission may be missing at followers. They are then pre-warm pulled (1 RTT, off-loop serve), with a small vote-delay bump on those views.\n- plan_prewarm_requests does ~390 RocksDB point gets on the swarm loop per manifest (~2-4 ms), still less than today's ~5.5 ms batch decode. Bodies still in da_pending (<=50 ms or 128 bodies) read as missing and are pulled needlessly.\n- Manifests are not retained for the T4 re-push on reconnect (rejoin cells).\n- The gain is CPU and I/O; matched/s will likely tie."}, {"slug": "da-pending-blob-files", "title": "Key-value separation (RocksDB BlobDB) for the append-only DA body CF in the consensus DB", "mechanism": "cf_native_pending holds every native-action body ever seen (~11.9 KB average value), keyed by random 32-byte hashes. It is append-only:\n- the pruner never touches it (pruner.rs covers bodies, headers, receipts, trades, status, core-writer and meta only);\n- NativeDaStore::remove has no production caller.\n\nWith leveled compaction (LZ4, ZSTD bottommost, dynamic level bytes; db.rs:794-809), every L0 flush overlaps the whole key range. Compaction therefore keeps rewriting all body bytes: decompress, merge, ZSTD recompress, with read and write I/O.\n\nUnder TORUS_DA_BLOB_FILES=1 the consensus DB's cf_native_pending would use:\n- set_enable_blob_files(true), set_min_blob_size(~4 KB, so 400-order places go to blobs and tiny cancels stay inline);\n- set_blob_file_size(256 MiB);\n- set_blob_compression_type(Zstd by default, about space-neutral with today's bottommost ZSTD; LZ4 as an arm);\n- set_enable_blob_gc(false).\n\nValues are then written once, at flush, into blob files, and compaction only moves keys plus ~20 B blob indexes.\n\nReads are unchanged:\n- get_batch_timed and missing() go through batched_multi_get_cf;\n- the serve path uses multi_get;\n- most recent bodies are still served from the memtable.\n\nAll of this is in rust-rocksdb 0.24 (db_options.rs:3460-3545).", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-state/src/db.rs:366,374-380,446,682-696,714-875", "crates/torus-state/src/native_da.rs:97-123,176-231,244-247", "crates/torus-state/src/pruner.rs (no CF_NATIVE_PENDING)", "crates/torus-state/src/running_hash.rs:653-664 (CF_NATIVE_PENDING not hashed)", "~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/rocksdb-0.24.0/src/db_options.rs:3460-3545"], "evidence": "Sampler, hsdb-300m-r2-B (main code):\n- consensus-DB compaction_cpu is 0.18-0.20 cores per node vs 0.06 for the state DB, i.e. ~76% of the node's compaction CPU;\n- rocksdb:low threads use 0.256-0.272 cores per node (io-threads);\n- consensus compaction writes 11.7-12.2 MB/s and reads 12.6-13.1 MB/s.\n\nCompaction writes rise across the cell as the CF grows: per 100 s, 4.7 -> 11.9 -> 7.6 -> 18.2 -> 14.1 -> 16.3-19.4 MB/s.\n\nrocksdb-consensus-LOG per CF (3 nodes):\n- cf_native_pending: 40-42 compactions, 8.2-8.6 GB in / 7.4-7.7 GB out per cell;\n- cf_consensus_meta: 0.02-0.045 GB;\n- DA flushes: 117-121 files, 1.77-1.83 GB, from 11.1-11.5 GB raw.\nCompaction writes ~4.3x what flush writes, and it is near-pure rewrite (p3s0cf memory 5875672747243702 found the same for the append CFs).\n\nThe DA CF grows by ~1.8 GB flushed per node per 10 min at 300m, with no retention.", "est_gain": "Consensus-DB compaction CPU -80-90% (0.19 -> ~0.02-0.04 cores per node), net of blob ZSTD at flush (~+0.03).\n\n- CPU-s/1M: -2.5 to -3.3% at 300m. About -2.5% if bench-push-manifest-not-full-body lands first and removes the duplicate share.\n- Compaction writes: -10 to -17 MB/s per node (-12 to -20% of device writes); reads -13 MB/s.\n- The gap grows with run length and in production, since the CF is never pruned.\n- matched/s about 0 (exec-bound); possibly slightly less contention.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.3, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Changes only the physical storage layout of a node-local, unhashed CF; running_hash.rs:653-664 asserts it is not hashed. Values and reads are byte-identical, and no consensus or execution path changes.\n\nFlag off is identical to main. Blob references stay readable if the node later reopens with the flag off; enable_blob_files only governs new writes (verify with a reopen test).", "flag": "TORUS_DA_BLOB_FILES=1 (new, default off; applies only to the consensus DB's cf_native_pending). Optional TORUS_DA_BLOB_CODEC=zstd|lz4.", "impl_size": "S: ~30-50 lines in StateDb::open_cfs/open_consensus (gate on the consensus CF set) plus a parse fn and tests.", "test_plan": "Unit tests:\n- open a consensus DB with the flag;\n- put_batch -> flush_cf -> compact;\n- get, get_batch_timed, missing and get_raw_batch must return identical bytes and positions;\n- reopen with the flag off and read back;\n- iterate_cf must equal a flag-off DB;\n- run the existing native_da tests under the flag.\n\nBench: 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=3 (unset vs =1).\n- Primary: torus_rocksdb_consensus_compaction_cpu_micros and compact read/write bytes (sampler.csv), rocksdb:low thread CPU, CPU-s/1M, device writes.\n- Guards: validate_block_da_reconstruct/multiget/decode, block_build_mirror (missing() reads), matched/s, timeouts, AGREE.\n\nAlso one 30-60 min soak pair, to show the rising compaction gap and blob-file count and size.", "novelty_check": "No BlobDB, blob-file or min_blob_size reference anywhere in code, docs or memory.\n\nPrior append-CF work changed codecs, not the rewrites themselves:\n- the s108 design (memory 0f55e61a: per-CF codec, WAL size, universal compaction in reserve);\n- TORUS_ROCKSDB_APPEND_CF_CODEC=lz4, kept off (+48% SST, 'LZ4 only if clearly better');\n- zstd1, no gain.\n\nWhat changed:\n- trade history is off on validators, so DA is now the dominant append-only CF;\n- since a44000e0 it sits alone in the consensus DB, where its compaction cost is cleanly measured (0.19 cores per node, rising).\n\nThis is not 71.8 option A, which is done, and not checkpoint slicing, which is state DB.", "risks": "- SST-resident bodies cost one extra blob-file read; the files are recent and page-cache hot. Watch the reconstruct and missing() timers.\n- Without the manifest candidate, duplicate overwrites (~24% of flushed entries) leave garbage blobs, since GC is off. Enable blob GC with an age cutoff if space matters.\n- First use of BlobDB here: snapshot/backup tooling must carry blob files.\n- Blob ZSTD shifts some CPU into flush.\n- Coordinate with 18c's checkpoint-slicing work, which may touch db.rs."}, {"slug": "rpc-block-body-raw-json", "title": "torus_getBlockBody: serialize each action straight to JSON (RawValue) instead of building a serde_json::Value tree", "mechanism": "get_block_body (torus-rpc torus.rs:1788-1872) decodes the bincode body record. It then builds `serde_json::to_value(a)` for every action into `Vec<serde_json::Value>` (types.rs:750): a full IndexMap/Value tree for ~160k orders in a full 400-action body. jsonrpsee then walks that tree to produce the response string.\n\nUnder TORUS_RPC_BODY_RAW_JSON=1, each action is serialized once with serde_json::value::to_raw_value. torus-rpc already enables the raw_value feature (Cargo.toml:20). The response then embeds Box<RawValue> items, which skips the per-order map, key-string and Value allocations and their drops.\n\nserde_json is built with preserve_order (indexmap is in its Cargo.lock dependencies), so the output bytes are identical to today's for every in-range value. The bench tail parses only nonce, signature and status (tools/bench-throughput/src/in_flight.rs:146-189).", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:1788-1872", "crates/torus-rpc/src/types.rs:748-790", "crates/torus-rpc/Cargo.toml:20", "Cargo.lock (serde_json deps include indexmap)", "tools/bench-throughput/src/in_flight.rs:146-189"], "evidence": "Section 70.5 (results doc lines 10070-10076): val2 CPU minus mean(val0, val1) = 189.7-198.8 ms per 400 actions the tail releases, ~85% of the ~226 ms the tail spends per full body. The handler re-serializes every action to JSON.\n\nhsdb-300m-r2-B io-threads (main code): val2 rpc-worker 0.796 cores vs 0.170/0.172 on val0/val1. That +0.63 cores ≈ 3.1 bodies/s x ~190 ms.\n\nSection 70.7: giving the tail 4x read capacity (depth 4) at 10m gave +4.49% (node A) / +6.52% (node B) matched/s; waves vanished and exec busy went from 0.77 to 0.99. Owner s121 (memory 8f9f6962) shelved the harness change.\n\nCode: `.map(|a| serde_json::to_value(a).unwrap_or_default())` at torus.rs:1800-1804.", "est_gain": "Expected serve cost per full body ~190 -> ~60-90 ms (2-3x; to be confirmed by microbench).\n\n- 300m: val2 -0.3 to -0.45 cores, so fleet-mean CPU-s/1M -2 to -2.8%. matched/s about 0 (the tail already keeps up: lag p50 1).\n- 10m tail depth 1 (the standard gate shape): tail capacity ~1,770 -> ~4,000+ actions/s vs ~1,500 needed. That removes most of the tail pacing, so I expect most of the depth-4 gain: +2 to +5% matched/s, an effect of the harness pacing, as in 70.5/70.7.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 3, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "RPC response encoding only. No consensus, state or exec path changes. Flag off is identical.\n\nFlag on is byte-identical for realistic data. The only difference is for i128/u128 values outside the 64-bit range: today to_value fails there and unwrap_or_default emits null for the whole action, while the raw path emits the number.", "flag": "TORUS_RPC_BODY_RAW_JSON=1 (new, default off)", "impl_size": "S: ~40-60 lines (a second field representation or custom Serialize for native_actions) plus tests.", "test_plan": "Unit test: flag-on vs flag-off JSON bytes are equal for place batches (with and without cloid), stop orders, cancels, modifies, session and EIP-712 signatures, and EVM entries.\n\nMicrobench: serve a 400x400-order body both ways, plus a jsonrpsee round trip.\n\nBench:\n- 10m depth-1 ABBA n=4, 120 s (the gate shape);\n- 300m 600 s n=3.\n\nMetrics: val2 rpc-worker CPU per body (io-threads), taillag.json lag p50/p99, per-wave idle (70.5 waves.py), matched/s, native blk/s, CPU-s/1M, AGREE. Watch 300m matched/s for closed-loop effects.", "novelty_check": "70.5/70.7 sized the serving cost and tested the harness read-ahead (depth 4, shelved). No memory, branch or commit proposes a cheaper getBlockBody encoding (searched for getBlockBody, RawValue, to_value and body JSON; git log --all). The s120 audit (memory 71a4d9e8) only listed the JSON re-serialize as the serve path.\n\nNot in any AVOID item. It overlaps the RPC lens, which may report it too.", "risks": "- The 10m gain comes from harness pacing (the bench's serial commit tail). The owner may treat it as harness-coupled, like depth 4, while the CPU saving is real for any getBlockBody client.\n- A faster tail can change the 300m closed loop (70.7 saw -5.2% at 300m depth 4, n=1), so measure 300m.\n- Item 7 step 1 also edits torus-rpc (a different function).\n- The speedup factor is estimated, not measured."}], "notes": "I found 3 candidates (ranked best first), all from main a44000e0 code and the ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B cells, which run the same code as main. I ran no cargo, benches or profilers; I only read logs and saved metrics.\n\n1. **Why the latency side of this lens doesn't convert to throughput now.** At 300m the view takes 315 ms, the same as exec block_ms (313). Proposal arrival on val0/val1 is ~160 ms because leaders sit in the blocking exec send. On the follower:\n   - validate_block is ~9 ms, of which DA reconstruct is 9.5 ms;\n   - vote delay is ~20 ms;\n   - insert_persist is ~27 ms.\n   \n   So cutting time between proposal and vote, or the DA decode, is hidden by exec back-pressure. At 10m, tail depth 1 is limited by the bench's tail and depth 4 is exec-bound. What this lens can actually change is CPU and disk on the shared host, which is what candidates 1 and 2 target. Candidate 3 is a body-serialization fix that also removes the 10m tail pacing.\n\n2. **Rejected or not proposed:**\n   - **TORUS_BODY_PUSH_MAX_BYTES=65536.** This knob already exists. One thing has changed since July: because a follower now votes only after it holds the body (s84), the body-fetch round trip now sits before the vote. Even so, the July result still holds: loaded views are paced by exec, so it would be neutral.\n   - **Pool-clone reconstruct instead of a DA decode.** This is too close to next-leader-parent-path-slim, which was refuted.\n   - **Turning off gossipsub message signing.** This is a protocol change, not node-local.\n   - **QUIC and UDP tuning.** The 8 MB UDP buffer was already tried with no gain.\n   - **Duplicate short-circuit at gossip ingest.** This is folded into candidate 1 as step 2b.\n\n3. **Side finding outside this lens (build flag, not env-flaggable).** RocksDB computes the WAL CRC32C with the portable table code (DefaultCRC32):\n   - In the p3cloidprof 300m profile, 0.573 ms per 1k fills (~0.13 cores per node) sits under DBImpl::WriteToWAL → log::Writer::AddRecord.\n   - The cause: librocksdb-sys build.rs adds -msse4.2/-mpclmul only when rustc target features include them, and the bench build.sh RUSTFLAGS has no target-feature or target-cpu setting. SST checksums default to XXH3, so this cost is WAL only.\n   - Inferred, not measured: at ~0.5-0.6 GB/s, the ~200 MB checkpoint batch would spend ~0.3 s of its ~0.6 s DB write on CRC while it holds the state-DB write group. That points at the in-window p99 (sec 74.6) and at CPU-s/1M (about -2.4%).\n   - Suggest routing this to the state/RocksDB lens or to 18c's checkpoint-slicing work. Test: rebuild with RUSTFLAGS '+sse4.2,+pclmulqdq' and microbench a 200 MB WriteBatch. It belongs to the shelved 'release profile tuning' item.\n\n4. **Production storage issue:** cf_native_pending is never pruned. It flushes ~1.8 GB per node per 10 min at 300m, and its compaction cost grows with it. A retention or prune design (or FIFO compaction with a large cap) is a follow-up for 18c; candidate 2 removes the CPU and I/O part but not the disk growth.\n\n5. **Combined effect if 1 and 2 both land:** about -0.35 cores per node, so CPU-s/1M about -6 to -8%, and device writes about -25 MB/s per node. All three candidates are node-local, leave consensus and state untouched, and must keep AGREE in every cell.\n\nMemory saved: project:torus-hyperbft, type:learning (DA/network lens numbers)."}, "research:r1:state-write": {"candidates": [{"slug": "da-cf-blob-files", "title": "Integrated BlobDB for the DA body CF (cf_native_pending) so compaction stops rewriting ~14 KB action bodies", "mechanism": "The DA store writes every admitted native action into cf_native_pending in the consensus DB. Each value is a bincode SignedNativeAction: about 14 KB for a 400-order place, about 0.2 KB for a cancel. Keys are random 32-byte hashes. Nothing ever deletes these rows: NativeDaStore::remove has no production caller, and the RH4 GC exists only on origin/perf/p3-throughput 4077726a. The CF is only point-read (Get/MultiGet, no iterator). Leveled compaction over random keys therefore rewrites the whole value payload about 5x, with LZ4 to ZSTD each time: 53 GB uncompressed rewritten per 10 GB flushed per 600 s. The fix turns on RocksDB integrated BlobDB for this one CF: enable_blob_files, min_blob_size 4 KiB, blob_compression_type ZSTD, blob GC off because nothing is deleted. Values are then written once to blob files at flush, and compaction moves only keys plus small blob refs. WAL, memtable and the foreground put path do not change. Reads fetch the blob (page cache) instead of an SST data block. Optional second arm: the same options on cf_block_bodies (3.7 MB per height, about 15.5 s compaction plus 7 s flush per node per 600 s).", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-state/src/db.rs:418-541 (DbTuning / from_env / from_raw: add the knob, same pattern as AppendCfCodec at 430-462)", "crates/torus-state/src/db.rs:838-865 (open_cfs per-CF descriptor loop: set the blob options when name == CF_NATIVE_PENDING, next to the APPEND_ONLY_CFS codec override at 851-862)", "crates/torus-state/src/db.rs:360-380 and 682-696 (CONSENSUS_DB_CFS, consensus DB budgets, open_consensus)", "crates/torus-state/src/native_da.rs:74-123 (put / put_batch, unchanged)", "crates/torus-state/src/native_da.rs:176-263 (get_batch_timed batched_multi_get_cf, missing, get_raw_batch, contains -> db.rs:1347-1350 get_pinned_cf; all keep working on blob values)", "crates/torus-state/src/native_da.rs:251-256 (remove: no production caller, so the CF is never pruned)"], "evidence": "Current main code, campaign ozarchy-hsdb (arm B = a44000e0 crates). Bench-window counter deltas, /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r{1,2,3}-B/metrics-{before,after}-val{0,1,2}.txt:\n- torus_rocksdb_consensus_compaction_cpu_micros: 116-125 CPU-s per node per 600 s, in all 9 node-cells.\n- consensus compact_write_bytes 7.4-7.8 GB and compact_read_bytes 8.0-8.3 GB per node.\n- State-DB compaction is only 37.6-40.6 s per node.\n\nr2-B rocksdb-consensus-LOG-val1.txt, cf_native_pending:\n- Low (compaction): CompMergeCPU 113.3 s over 39 jobs; read 7.3 GB, wrote 6.6 GB (53.1 GB uncompressed).\n- High (flush): 9.4 s over 115 flushes, 10.0 GB uncompressed.\n- Event-log sum agrees: 118.5 s.\n- Arm A (shared DB) LOG gives the same: 118.4 s + 9.9 s.\n\nio-threads-{start,end}.txt: rocksdb:low uses 154-164 CPU-s per node, 4.5-5.3% of node CPU, so DA compaction is about 75% of it.\n\n10m (ozarchy-p4soak10-10m-k1-a/rocksdb-LOG-val1.txt): cf_native_pending compaction 153.2 s + flush 11.9 s per 621 s.\n\nValue size: 10 GB over 1.13M flushed keys is 8.8 KB average, so places are about 14 KB (39% of actions are small cancel-alls). No iterator over the CF in production (grep).\n\nPrior options (docs/plans/append-cf-compaction.md: codec, WAL cap, universal, WAL compression) never included key-value separation. The p3s0 per-CF split (mem 5875672747243702) already put DA at 24.8% of compaction CPU when the trades CFs were on.", "est_gain": "300m: CPU-s/1M down 0.73-0.88 (-3.1% to -3.8% of 23.38). It removes about 95% of the 116-125 CPU-s per node per 600 s, minus about 8-10 s extra for ZSTD (instead of LZ4) at flush and about 2 s of key-only compaction.\n10m: about -0.75 CPU-s/1M (about -4.5% of 17.25), from the soak's 153 s per 621 s.\nDevice writes: -12 to -13 MB/s per node (about -15% of 84-86 MB/s). Compaction reads: -13 MB/s per node.\nmatched/s: tie expected. At most about +0.3% from roughly 0.6 cores freed on the shared 32-thread host, which is below the n=4 CI.\nblock p99 and RSS: no change expected.\nThe gain grows with uptime: the CF is never pruned, so leveled write amplification rises as it grows.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.2, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "Changes only the on-disk layout of a node-local, non-hashed CF. Every Get/MultiGet returns byte-identical values, and consensus, the running state hash and AGREE never see the layout. With the flag off, options are exactly today's. Data dirs open in place both ways: RocksDB reads blob references without the option, and old inline SSTs migrate on their next compaction.", "flag": "TORUS_ROCKSDB_DA_BLOB=zstd|lz4 (unset = today; zstd keeps DA disk size about equal to today's bottommost ZSTD). Optional second arm: TORUS_ROCKSDB_BODY_BLOB for cf_block_bodies.", "impl_size": "small (about 40-60 lines in db.rs plus tests; no reader changes)", "test_plan": "Tests first (torus-state):\n- Parse the knob: unset, zstd, lz4, garbage (falls back to today with a warning).\n- Open the consensus DB with the knob, put at least 4 KiB values plus small ones, flush_cf, and assert blob files exist (rocksdb.num-blob-files or *.blob in the dir).\n- get / get_batch_timed / missing / contains / get_raw_batch return identical bytes and order, including duplicates and unsorted input.\n- compact_range keeps the values intact.\n- Reopen without the knob and read everything back; reopen with the knob over a dir written without it and read everything back.\n- node_dbs reopen_recovers_hotstuff_state_and_da_bodies passes with the knob on.\n\nBench (archy, same binary):\n- 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=3-4, arms unset vs zstd.\n- Report CPU-s/1M (a 0.7% step resolved at n=3 in sec 74.1), consensus compaction CPU and bytes (expect about -95%), device MB/s per node, rocksdb:low thread CPU from io-threads, DA read timers (DaReadTiming multi_get), matched/s, blk/s, block p99, RSS, and the disk total including *.blob.\n- AGREE in every cell.\n- Optional 10m pair and a cf_block_bodies arm.", "novelty_check": "Not in the AVOID list, the s123 survivors, or the 3 recovered refuted s123 candidates.\n- The append-CF compaction plan (mem 0f55e61a) covered codec (TORUS_ROCKSDB_APPEND_CF_CODEC LZ4: off, not clearly better), WAL cap, universal compaction (in reserve) and WAL compression. It never covered blob files.\n- A sibling DA/network lens of this same round (mem 873b7f65) also notes 'no BlobDB ever tried': merge with that finding, not prior work.\n- The s123 storage lens's rejected candidates were not recovered by name, so 18c should confirm it was not among the 11 unnamed ones.\n- No collision with checkpoint slicing, defer-books, item 7 or Phase 5 (consensus-DB per-CF options only).", "metric_targets": ["CPU-s/1M", "bytes written", "RocksDB compaction CPU", "RocksDB compaction write bytes"], "regime": "300m and 10m (DA bytes scale with actions, not markets); larger on long-running nodes", "risks": "- Blob files never get deleted while DA is never pruned. About 115 new blob files per node per 600 s, all kept open (max_open_files -1; nofile limit is 524288) plus MANIFEST growth. Pair it later with DA retention (RH4 port) plus blob GC.\n- ZSTD at flush makes rocksdb:high flushes about 2x longer. No stall expected: L0 SSTs become tiny.\n- Reads of flushed bodies now cost one pread plus decompress per blob (no blob cache). The consensus-thread reconstruct and proposer missing() calls mostly hit the memtable. Watch the DA read timers.\n- The harness disk metric (data-du sst_bytes) must count *.blob files.\n- If the DA lens's dedup lands, the absolute saving shrinks by about 20-25% (fewer DA puts); the two are complementary."}, {"slug": "ingress-view-skip-position-slices", "title": "Stop deep-copying CF_NATIVE_POSITIONS into the per-block ingress-view slices (RSS -0.3 to -0.5 GB at 300m)", "mechanism": "Phase 3 step 2 publishes a ReadView that layers 8 ingress CFs over the DB.\n- For every block since the last checkpoint (about 40 at 300m), the per-block merge thread deep-copies the block's ingress CFs into a slice (FrozenPending::slice -> BTreeMap clone_from).\n- The view keeps all of them until the checkpoint. There E hands about 40 slices to a 2-slot dropper and frees about 37 of them inline on the exec thread.\n- CF_NATIVE_POSITIONS is about 92% of these entries (139 B each).\n\nThe only reader of positions through the view is funded::is_funded's third fallback (anti-spam item A, an 'any open position' prefix seek). It runs only after the perp-collateral and spot checks fail. Item A is off on the bench devnet (TORUS_INGRESS_MIN_COLLATERAL=0). The RPC verify path reads only markets and sessions, and addr-rate reads cum volume from CF_NATIVE_BALANCES.\n\nSimple variant: the flag removes CF_NATIVE_POSITIONS from one ingress_cfs() table used by both the slicer (app.rs:382) and ReadView::layered. Positions are then neither copied nor layered, and a view read of positions goes to the DB.\n\nProduction-safe variant (for item A on): per block, keep only the distinct trader prefixes that had a position write or tombstone (at most 5k addresses). Fallback 3 answers 'funded' for those traders and asks the DB for the rest. This never refuses anyone main admits; it can admit a trader who closed everything within the window, for at most one interval.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-state/src/read_view.rs:58-72 (INGRESS_CFS, ingress_ids OnceLock)", "crates/torus-state/src/read_view.rs:187-192 (ReadView::layered routing)", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:359-392 (spawn_layer_merge; slice cut at 382)", "crates/torus-state/src/backend.rs:1050-1071 (FrozenPending::slice deep clone)", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:181-215 and 418-452 (install_slices, clear_view_blocks, Dropper QUEUE=2 -> inline frees on E)", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:2632-2659 (checkpoint_upkeep_with: view_blocks extend / clear)", "crates/torus-mempool/src/funded.rs:124-147 (fallback 3 is the only positions reader)", "crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:283-287 and 1075-1093 (ingress_state, check_funded)", "devnet/wsl/env.sh:67 (TORUS_INGRESS_MIN_COLLATERAL=0 on the bench)"], "evidence": "Results doc sec 48.2 (lines 5517-5556), jemalloc heap profile:\n- The view's slices hold 260 MB live.\n- The view arm adds +0.33 GB RSS peak under jemalloc (+0.68 GB under glibc), at 120 s cells with about 57k fills per block.\n\nSec 41.1: positions are 92% of state entries at 139 B each.\n\nCurrent main code, /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B/mem-samples.txt, val1:\n- torus_exec_checkpoint_live_bytes rises from about 156 MB just after each checkpoint to 600-712 MB logical before the next, with up to 44-46 live objects.\n- torus_jemalloc_allocated_bytes swings in step, from 2.6-2.9 GB to 3.4-3.8 GB.\n- L itself is only about 188 MB logical per checkpoint (torus_exec_checkpoint_layer_bytes 10.5 GB / 59), so slices are about 250-400 MB logical at the window peak.\n\nmetrics-{before,after}: torus_exec_checkpoint_drop_inline_total 2072-2117 per node per cell over 55-56 checkpoints, about 37 per checkpoint. These are the slices freed on the exec thread.\n\nThe upkeep histogram puts checkpoint-block upkeep at 25-205 ms (34 of 56 checkpoints above 25.6 ms), against under 0.4 ms for normal blocks.\n\n18c probe (mem 020b56cd): slice cut 16-28 ms per block at 2x sizes.\n\nThe owner accepted positions in the view (s112, mem 3a59ff55) as a memory trade-off; no later memory proposes trimming them.", "est_gain": "300m:\n- jemalloc allocated peak -0.45 to -0.65 GB per node.\n- RSS mean -0.2 to -0.4 GB (-4% to -7% of 5.74), peak -0.3 to -0.5 GB (-5% to -8% of 6.55).\n- CPU-s/1M -0.5% to -1%: the slice clone and its later free, about 10-15 ms per native block off the exec thread.\n- Exec thread: checkpoint-block upkeep loses most of its inline frees (roughly -30 to -50 ms at those blocks, about -1 ms per native block on average). That trims exec-time spikes; matched/s gain is at most about +0.3%, which is not resolvable.\n\n10m: small (checkpoint live bytes peak only about 112 MB there).", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.2, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Node-local ingress admission state only: block validity, execution and the state hash never read the view (funded.rs and read_view.rs docs). Flag off keeps today's 8 CFs exactly.\n- Simple variant with item A off (bench): no reader of positions, so no behaviour change at all.\n- Simple variant with item A on: fallback 3 sees positions as of the last durable checkpoint (at most about 16 s stale).\n- Exact variant: only ever admits more, never refuses where main admits.", "flag": "TORUS_INGRESS_VIEW_POSITIONS=0 (unset = today); the exact variant behind the same flag when TORUS_INGRESS_MIN_COLLATERAL > 0", "impl_size": "small (simple variant about 30-50 lines plus tests; exact variant about 100 lines, including a defaulted StateBackend method that ReadView overrides)", "test_plan": "Tests first:\n- Parse ingress_cfs(): unset gives the 8 CFs.\n- With the flag, a slice has no CF_NATIVE_POSITIONS (touches_only) and ReadView reads positions from the DB.\n- The existing read_view random-block equivalence tests and the app.rs ingress_view_* tests pass with the flag off and on (non-position CFs).\n- Exact variant, property test over random position writes and deletes across blocks: is_funded(view) is true whenever main's is_funded is true.\n- check_funded with min_trs=0 is unchanged.\n\nBench:\n- 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=4 (RSS sd about 0.1-0.15 GB per cell; the sec 74 n=3 CI was ±0.45 GB), arms unset vs =0.\n- Report RSS mean and peak (cpu.csv and mem-samples), jemalloc allocated and resident, checkpoint live bytes p50/p90/max, drop_inline per checkpoint, the upkeep histogram at checkpoint blocks, CPU-s/1M, matched/s, blk/s, AGREE.\n- Optional ANTISPAM=1 cell to confirm funded senders are admitted the same.", "novelty_check": "Not in the AVOID list, the s123 work or the perf history. Sec 48 named 'the view's slices (260 MB)' as an RSS lever, but nothing was built.\n- Different code from defer-books (which changes checkpoint_layer deferred slots), checkpoint slicing (the flush-worker batch) and Phase 5 (overlay write hooks / Users).\n- It goes against the s112 owner choice to keep positions in the view, so it needs owner sign-off for nodes with item A on. The exact variant keeps that choice's guarantee.", "metric_targets": ["RSS", "jemalloc allocated", "CPU-s/1M", "exec time at checkpoint blocks"], "regime": "300m (slice size scales with traders x markets touched per block); minor at 10m", "risks": "- Simple variant with item A on: a trader whose first position opened within the current window, holding less than min_trs free perp collateral and less than min spot, could be refused (cancels included) for up to about 16 s. Use the exact variant or enable the flag only where item A is off.\n- RSS is noisy under jemalloc retention: judge on allocated / active and n=4.\n- The size estimate is inferred from gauges plus the sec 48 heap profile, not from a direct per-slice measurement.\n- Optional companion: hand clear_view_blocks' slices to the dropper as one item, so even the remaining small slices are not freed inline."}], "notes": "Lens: state write path, RocksDB and checkpointing on main a44000e0. Read-only; no cargo or bench was run. Memory saved (project:torus-hyperbft). Two candidates passed the self-checks; I found no exec-thread lever in this lens that is above noise and not already on the AVOID list.\n\nConsidered and rejected:\n- **unordered_write on the state DB** (for the in-window p99): small writers would still wait for the 200 MB batch's WAL write (~35-40% of the 0.6 s). It requires turning pipelined writes off. It also breaks snapshot immutability for non-ingress DB readers, such as the R24 per-book snapshots in level-row modes. It targets the same stall as slicing, which is in flight, and does it worse.\n- **WAL-off for the checkpoint batch** (revisit item): besides atomic_flush (the r9 finding), it needs a forced memtable flush before any later WAL'd write that carries the applied marker. Serial EVM blocks are such writes, and without the barrier the marker recovers ahead of the lost checkpoint, which is a fork. It sits on the same code slicing is rewriting; revisit after slicing lands.\n- **Positions CF compaction tuning** (L0 trigger 8, no L0 compression): at most ~0.05 CPU-s/1M. Positions flush plus compaction is only ~40 CPU-s per node per 600 s.\n- **Memtable insert hints** are not in the C API. A **vector memtable** sorts on every Get or iterator.\n- **State-DB block cache** usage is 87 bytes at cell end, so cache size is no RSS lever.\n- **Checkpoint-block inline merges on the exec thread** (2 block merges plus slice frees, upkeep 25-205 ms at checkpoint blocks): average ~1 ms per block, so below matched/s noise. Mostly covered by candidate 2.\n\nOne more finding that cannot be an env flag: librocksdb-sys gets -msse4.2/-mpclmul only from rustc target features, and .cargo/config.toml sets none. RocksDB therefore uses software CRC32C for every WAL record (including the ~200 MB checkpoint batch, lengthening the write window) and for every SST block. This is a build-flag change for 18c and the release-profile item. The sibling DA lens (mem 873b7f65) found it too.\n\nCandidate 1 overlaps that same sibling lens's DA-compaction observation; merge the two. Its DA push-dedup candidate complements candidate 1. The unmerged RH4 DA GC (origin/perf/p3-throughput 4077726a) is related prior work: DA is still never pruned on main.\n\nKey data paths:\n- /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r{1,2,3}-B/: metrics-{before,after}-val*.txt, rocksdb-consensus-LOG-val*.txt, mem-samples.txt, io-threads-*.txt\n- /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4soak10-10m-k1-a/rocksdb-LOG-val1.txt\n- Parse scripts: /tmp/claude-explore-st/{cfstats.py,evsum.py}"}, "research:r1:profile-mining": {"candidates": [{"slug": "settle-passb-deferred-results-scatter", "title": "Settle pass B: write per-order results in one tight scatter after the market loop instead of one cache miss per order (plus optional off-thread drop of match results)", "mechanism": "In the parallel settle's pass B (the serial apply loop, markets in ascending id), every prepared order ends with `results[prep.index] = NativeActionResult::placed(result)` (native_executor.rs:8904).\n- `results` has one 56-byte entry per flattened order: about 95k entries, ~5.3 MB per native block on main.\n- It is filled with \"pending\" placeholders at :7250, long before settle runs.\n- Pass B walks orders market by market, so consecutive writes jump about 300 slots (~17 KB). Every write lands on a cold cache line.\n- The assignment first runs NativeActionResult's drop glue, which loads the old `Option<Cow<str>>` niche word. That load misses.\n- Each pass-B iteration is a long chain of work (balance-cache load/set, persist_trade, metric atomics), so only one miss is in flight at a time. About 90-110 ns is exposed per order.\n\nFix, behind a flag:\n- Inside the loop, push `(prep.index as u32, result.reject)` into a pre-sized sequential Vec (8 bytes per order). `placed()` depends only on `reject`, and FailureReason is Copy.\n- After the market loop, write the identical `NativeActionResult` values in a tight scatter loop. That loop has many misses in flight, so the cost drops to well under 1 ms.\n- The rare fill-failure write (:8874-8883) stays inline.\n- Apply the same change to the sequential settle (:8509).\n\nOptional second part (separate sub-flag): the end-of-iteration drop of each market's `Vec<MatchResult>` (and of `plan.orders`, if the zip iterates by reference) frees ~95k small Vecs on the exec thread. Hand them to a background dropper instead, the same pattern as app.rs `Dropper` / torus-ckpt-drop.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:8589 (settle_market_results_parallel)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:8770-8939 (pass B loop; :8775 into_iter().zip(plans); :8904 results write; :8874-8883 fill-failure write)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:8338 and :8509 (sequential settle, same pattern)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:67-137 (NativeActionResult, placed())", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7250-7252 (results init with pending placeholders)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:395-450 (existing Dropper / torus-ckpt-drop pattern)"], "evidence": "Three independent 300m exec-thread profiles show the same cost.\n\n(1) d3cba5e2 inline stacks, /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p1-mperf-w1-exec-inline.script.gz:\n- 649 of 17,912 exec samples (3.62%) sit on one instruction, vaddr 0x143af5d.\n- Disassembly (objdump of the archived binary /home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-g3s0-stage/m-run/release/torus-node) shows `mov (%rcx,%rax,1),%rsi` with a 0x38 (56-byte) stride, then `cmp $-3,%rsi`. That is the drop-glue load of `results[prep.index]`: a load-miss stall.\n- Inside settle_market_results_parallel: drop_glue<NativeActionResult> 3.66% and drop_glue<Vec<MatchResult>> 1.17% of exec on-CPU.\n\n(2) ef812b02 exec-only W2 profile, 5 events, all 6 cells c0/c100 (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p3cloidprof-300m-r{1,2,3}-c{0,100}/w2.script.gz, mapped through ozarchy-p3cloidprof-run/analyst/a2l-cache.json):\n- The pass-B results drop (native_executor.rs:8403 at that build) is 2.8-3.7% of exec cycles and 3.1-3.7% of exec DRAM demand fills in every cell.\n- That is a lower bound: only ~72-77% of samples map.\n- The exec thread overall runs at IPC 1.18 with 33k DRAM fills per 1k fills (analyst/llc.txt).\n\n(3) c88d3073 prof-B (/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/docs/perf/bench-archive/tools/ozarchy-p3cloidec-tools/analyst/prof-B/frames-self.txt): exec drop_glue self 0.138 ms CPU per 1k fills, 3.8% of the exec thread's 3.669.\n\nCurrent main shape (hsdb-300m-r2-B summary.json and metrics-after-val0.txt):\n- 184.2M orders placed over 2,052 native blocks, about 90-96k orders per block; 72k fills per block.\n- Exec on-CPU 268 ms per block, block_ms 313, settle_pass_b 43.3 ms.\n- The code at :8904 is unchanged on a44000e0.\n\nNot covered elsewhere:\n- Phase 5 step 2 changes pass B's cache merges and Users writes, not the results write.\n- No s123 branch touches settle.", "est_gain": "Results write: about 3.2-3.8% of exec on-CPU, which is 8.5-10 ms per native block on main's 600 s 300m shape. The deferred scatter removes about 80-90%, so -7 to -9 ms.\n\nOptional drop offload: -2 to -3 ms (1.0-1.2% of exec on-CPU).\n\nTotal at 300m: exec block_ms about -2.5 to -3.5%, exec wall per 1k fills by the same amount, matched/s +2 to +3% (rule of thumb ~0.3% per ms at 313 ms blocks). That clears the n=4 paired CI of about +-1.3%.\n\nOther regimes:\n- 10m at tail depth 4 (exec-bound): smaller. With 10 markets the per-market strides are about 10 slots, which the prefetchers partly cover. Unmeasured.\n- 10m at tail depth 1: matched/s about 0 (the bench tail sets the pace).\n- CPU-s/1M: about -1% (exec thread only).", "est_gain_pct_matched": 2.5, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "Byte-identical by construction:\n- Every order slot receives the same NativeActionResult value, written once. Only the moment of the write changes.\n- Pass-B state mutations (balances, position-cache merge, cum_volume, trades, metrics) keep their exact order.\n- The drop offload only moves frees to another thread.\n- No consensus-visible output changes. Flag off runs today's code.", "flag": "TORUS_SETTLE_DEFER_RESULTS=1 (default off). Optional sub-flag TORUS_SETTLE_DROP_OFFLOAD=1 (default off).", "impl_size": "S: ~60-100 lines including tests (one Vec plus a scatter loop in two settle functions; the optional dropper hand-off is another ~40 lines).", "test_plan": "Tests first:\n- Differential execute_batch test on random multi-market blocks, serial and engine-forced, parallel and sequential settle. With the flag on and off, `NativeBatchResult.results`, total_gas, the flushed overlay rows and the trades must be equal.\n- Goldens A and B (tests/perf_equivalence_golden.rs) unchanged.\n- rdeps(torus-bridge) green.\n\nBench, quiet archy:\n- 300m 120 s gate shape, ABBA n=4, flag unset vs =1 (run drop offload as a third arm or a second campaign).\n- Report matched/s with paired CI, native blk/s, exec block_ms, settle_pass_b_ms (expect -7 to -9 ms), engine_ms_per_1k_fills, CPU-s/1M, AGREE in every cell.\n- 10m n=4 at depth 1 as the no-regression gate (depth 4 optional).\n\nOptional confirmation: one cycles:u perf on val1 (works at paranoid 2). The results-slot load stall should disappear from settle_market_results_parallel.", "novelty_check": "Searched with no hit:\n- docs/perf/ozarchy-antispam-item6-pf1-2026-10-04.md (grep drop_glue / NativeActionResult / results[ / scatter). The only drop_glue mention is the unrelated end-of-block context drop in sec 17.3.\n- Memory (search_knowledge for results-vector, drop, pass-B and DRAM-stall terms).\n- The Phase 5 plan (origin/docs/item6-phase5-plan; pass B is rewritten only for cache and Users application).\n- The four s123 exp notes.\n\nPrior pass-B work was different: the reserve and Position v2 merge fixes, the P2-2 batch cache flush (no gain), and parallel shard apply (shelved). None touches the results write or the per-order frees. The s83-era 'not cache misses' finding covered book-code instruction counts in the pool, not this exec-thread store.", "metric_targets": ["matched/s", "exec block_ms", "native blk/s", "engine_ms_per_1k_fills", "CPU-s/1M"], "regime": "300m primary (exec-bound). 10m tail depth 4: smaller. 10m tail depth 1: neutral.", "risks": "- Gains are taken from pre-Phase-4 profiles (d3cba5e2, ef812b02, c88d3073). The code is identical on main, but miss latency depends on host cache pressure. A head re-profile is optional, not required.\n- If a store miss later meets a metric atomic (fence) in pass B, part of the stall could reappear. The deferred tight loop avoids this; batching pass B's per-order counter increments per market is a cheap add-on if needed.\n- Phase 5 step 2 edits the same function, so expect a small rebase.\n- Drop offload: cross-thread frees move jemalloc work to the dropper thread. Keep the queue bounded with an inline-free fallback, as Dropper does today."}, {"slug": "phase2-3-market-major-param-deref-cuts", "title": "Remove scattered PreparedOrder / params loads from three serial exec-thread loops: sell_top_ups, the s92 margin-cut counters and the Phase-3 match-request build", "mechanism": "C2 made PreparedOrder borrow `params` from the block's action list, so no per-order clone is made. Three serial exec-thread loops then visit orders in market-major order and load scattered memory per order:\n\n(a) sell_top_ups (:9858-9920). Main has 44.4k full top-ups per native block. The function collects (index, market, k), sorts it, then reads `market_batches[m][k]` (a random 176-byte PreparedOrder) and `p.params.quantity`, which is a second random line.\n\n(b) The s92 counter loop after matching (:7787-7795). For every order it reads `p.params.is_buy` and does two atomic inc_by. On the bench the counters it feeds stay 0.\n\n(c) The Phase-3 MatchRequest build (:7701-7710). For each checked taker, `taker_margin_limit` reads params.order_type, time_in_force and price, plus an Arc clone of the tiers.\n\nFix, behind a flag:\n(a) stitch_outcome (:8190) runs in flat order on both the serial and the sharded prepare paths, and params is hot there. It appends each top-up candidate (index, market, k, sender, quantity, margin_reserved) to a sequential list. sell_top_ups walks that list in order, with no sort and no PreparedOrder or params reads, keeping the same bal_cache trajectory. Grants (market, k, grant, full) are applied afterwards in a tight loop. Fallback if stitch should stay untouched: gather the same tuple while iterating each market's Vec sequentially.\n(b) Test `r.result.margin_cut_price.is_some()` before touching params. Sum maker_margin_cancels and reduce_only_cuts per market and call inc_by once per market.\n(c) Carry `margin_reserved: Option<FixedPoint>` in MatchRequest, plus the market's tiers once per batch. match_market then builds TakerMarginLimit on the pool worker with the same function. The worker already reads params (req.params.clone(), market_workers.rs:351/359).", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:9858-9920 (sell_top_ups; :9877 sort; :9882 p.params.quantity)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:8190-8228 (stitch_outcome, both prepare paths)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7600-7622 (sell_top_ups call site after stitch)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7787-7795 (s92 counter loop) and :9137-9153 (record_margin_cuts)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:7701-7710 (MatchRequest build) and :9503-9516 (taker_margin_limit)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:17-23 (MatchRequest) and :336-380 (match_market)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:452-477 (PreparedOrder)"], "evidence": "ef812b02 exec-only W2, all 6 cells c0/c100 (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p3cloidprof-300m-r{1,2,3}-c{0,100}/w2.script.gz plus analyst/a2l-cache.json; line numbers at that build map to the same code on main):\n- sell_top_ups: 2.1-2.8% of exec cycles. Its PreparedOrder load (:9371 then, :9882 now) alone is 1.45-2.1%.\n- s92 loop `p.params.is_buy` (:7322 then, :7793 now): 1.0-1.3% of exec cycles and 3.2-3.7% of exec DRAM demand fills.\n- taker_margin_limit (:9001 then): 0.67-1.0% of cycles and 2.8-3.7% of DRAM fills.\n- All of these are lower bounds (72-77% of samples map).\n\nd3cba5e2 inline (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p1-mperf-w1-exec-inline.script.gz):\n- sell_top_ups 5.74% of exec on-CPU: a one-instruction stall on the `p.params` pointer load at :9454 then (1.85% + 0.41%), deref_mut 0.63%, sort 0.56%.\n- The telemetry is_buy load (`movzbl 0x78(%rcx)` at :7378 then): 1.31%.\n- taker_margin_limit (:9082 then): 0.87%.\n\nc88d3073 prof-B: sell_top_ups self 0.100 ms per 1k fills, 2.7% of exec.\n\nMain bench (hsdb-300m-r2-B metrics, val0 and val1):\n- torus_sell_top_ups_full_total 91.16M over 2,052 native blocks, i.e. 44.4k per block.\n- maker_margin_cancels_total 0, reduce_only_cuts_total 0.\n- TORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE unset, so prepare runs serial; sell_top_ups, the s92 loop and the request build are serial on every path.", "est_gain": "Gross 3.8-5% of exec on-CPU, about 10-13 ms per native block on main's 600 s 300m shape. Removable share is about 65-80%:\n- (a) -4 to -6 ms (phase_margin_ms)\n- (b) -2.5 to -3.5 ms (engine_untimed)\n- (c) -1.5 to -2.5 ms (phase_match_ms)\n\nTotal at 300m: about -7 to -10 ms, exec wall per 1k fills -2.5 to -3.5%, matched/s +2 to +3%.\n\nOther regimes:\n- 10m: per-market access is more sequential (10 interleaved streams), so the gain is smaller. Expect neutral to +1% at depth 4 and about 0 at depth 1.\n- CPU-s/1M: -0.5 to -1%.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 2.5, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Byte-identical:\n- sell_top_ups still visits the same candidates in flat order with the same inputs. margin_reserved at stitch equals the value read today, since nothing changes PreparedOrders between stitch and sell_top_ups. Same bal_cache loads and sets in the same order, same grants, same res_price updates and counts.\n- TakerMarginLimit is produced by the same function from the same (tiers, params, post-top-up reserved), only on the worker.\n- The s92 counters are node-local observability; their per-block totals are unchanged.\n- Flag off runs today's code.", "flag": "TORUS_EXEC_PREP_STALL_CUTS=1 (default off). Sub-flags for attribution are optional: _TOPUPS, _S92, _REQ.", "impl_size": "M: ~150-250 lines. A candidate list threaded through stitch_outcome on both prepare paths, a sell_top_ups rewrite with a deferred grant pass, a reorder and per-market accumulation in the s92 loop, and a MatchRequest field change in market_workers.rs.", "test_plan": "Tests first:\n- Differential execute_batch test, flag on vs off, serial and engine-forced (TORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE path). Compare results, overlay rows, the sell_top_ups [full, partial, none] counts, PreparedOrder margin_reserved and res_price after Phase 2, and the per-block s92 counter deltas.\n- Goldens A and B unchanged; rdeps(torus-bridge) green.\n\nBench, quiet archy:\n- 300m 120 s gate, ABBA n=4, unset vs =1.\n- Report matched/s with paired CI, phase_margin_ms (expect -4 to -6), phase_match_ms (expect -1.5 to -2.5), engine_untimed_ms (expect -2.5 to -3.5), engine_ms_per_1k_fills, native blk/s, CPU-s/1M, AGREE.\n- 10m n=4 at depth 1 as the no-regression gate.\n- Can share a campaign with settle-passb-deferred-results-scatter (2x2 or a combined arm).", "novelty_check": "Searched with no hit: the results doc (grep sell_top_ups / record_margin_cuts / taker_margin_limit), docs/perf/*.md, docs/plans/*.md (only Phase 4's mention of sell_top_ups' BalanceCache::load), memory, and the s123 notes.\n\nWhy existing work does not cover it:\n- TORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE (queued A/B) parallelizes only prepare_one. stitch_outcome, sell_top_ups, the s92 loop and the Phase-3 request build stay serial on the exec thread in both paths.\n- Phase 5 does not touch Phase 2/3 setup.\n- The cost was introduced by C2's borrowed params, B-blind (31cea69) and s92 telemetry, and was never measured as memory stalls.", "metric_targets": ["matched/s", "exec block_ms", "native blk/s", "engine_ms_per_1k_fills"], "regime": "300m primary (exec-bound). 10m: small or neutral.", "risks": "- The removable share of (a) is the least certain part. The deferred grant writes still touch random PreparedOrders, but in a tight loop.\n- (c) changes MatchRequest. The s123 cancel-all branch edits market_workers.rs (run_contained), so expect a trivial rebase.\n- Shares come from pre-Phase-4 builds; the code is identical on main.\n- Removing the exec thread's params touch in (c) may move a few L3 hits on the pool into DRAM fills. That cost is parallel and small."}, {"slug": "eip712-batch-item-keccak-x4", "title": "4-way AVX2 Keccak for the 400 per-order EIP-712 item hashes in hash_place_order_batch, the dominant verify-thread CPU", "mechanism": "Verifying a 400-order PlaceOrderBatch hashes each order as an EIP-712 item.\n- Each item is the 32-byte typehash plus 9 fields of 32 bytes: a fixed 320-byte preimage, 3 Keccak-f[1600] permutations (eip712.rs:311-354).\n- That makes 1,200 permutations per action, against ~95 for the item-hash concatenation and ~130 for each canonical-bytes key or hash. So ~78% of verify-thread Keccak is item hashing.\n- The items are independent and equal-length, which fits multi-buffer SIMD exactly.\n\nFix, behind a flag:\n- Add Keccak-256 x4 for fixed 320-byte inputs, using an AVX2 4-lane state type.\n- The keccak 0.1.6 crate already in Cargo.lock exposes a generic `keccak_p<L: LaneSize>` over a public `LaneSize` trait. Its own `simd` feature needs nightly portable_simd; the toolchain is stable 1.98.1. So write a `[u64;4]` newtype lane, or copy the ~60-line round loop, inside a `#[target_feature(enable=\"avx2\")]` function, guarded by `is_x86_feature_detected!`.\n- Use it in hash_place_order_batch (:363) for groups of 4 items; keep the scalar alloy keccak256 for the remainder and for everything else.\n\nAll callers reach this function through eip712_struct_hash: gossip recover, RPC ingress verify, and exec-time recover on a trust-cache miss.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:311-331 (append_place_order_fields, 9 x 32-byte fields)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:350-354 (hash_place_order_item)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:363-383 (hash_place_order_batch per-item loop)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:931-940 (recover_sender_for_chain) and :1227 (batch_verify_native_actions_cached)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:792-830 (gossip ingest recover)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:501 (verify_one_action_with, ingress)", "/home/oz/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/keccak-0.1.6/src/lib.rs:118-136,236 (pub LaneSize trait, generic keccak_p)"], "evidence": "Keccak is the hottest function in every profile.\n\n10m, Phase 4 node, val1 cycles:u (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-p4soak10-run/analyst/perf/top-b-k1-b.txt and commsym-b-k1-b.txt):\n- __KeccakF1600 is 24.39% of node cycles: gossip-verify 12.50%, ingress-verify 6.27%, hotstuff 2.22%, exec 2.04%, rpc-worker 1.36%.\n- encode_place_order_params adds another 1.44%.\n\n300m (/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/docs/perf/bench-archive/tools/ozarchy-p3cloidprof-tools/analyst/noise/keccak-by-thread.txt):\n- Keccak is 0.41 cores in gossip-verify and 0.21 in ingress-verify per node.\n- c88d3073 prof-B: Keccak is 4.75 of 23.96 ms CPU per 1k fills (19.8%); gossip-verify is 54% Keccak, ingress-verify 30%.\n\nCurrent main per-thread CPU (/home/oz/bench-results-matched/ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B/io-threads-{start,end}.txt): gossip-verify 0.94-0.97 cores per node, ingress-verify 0.76-0.84.\n\nMemory 2f21491f (s77): the gossip cost is EIP-712 struct hashing of 400-order batches, not ecrecover; the struct hash is ~620 us per action after the typehash hoist.", "est_gain": "Expect at least 2x on item hashing (to be confirmed by a microbench).\n- 300m: verify-thread Keccak -40 to -50%, about -0.30 to -0.36 cores per node, so CPU-s/1M -5 to -7% (23.4 to about 22.0).\n- 10m: CPU-s/1M -7 to -9% (verify Keccak is 18.8% of node cycles there).\n- matched/s: about 0 at 300m (exec-bound) and at 10m depth 1 (tail-bound). A small indirect gain is possible because archy's parallel phases are CPU-contended across the 3 nodes.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "The node-local flag changes how hashes are computed, never the result, so it must be bit-exact Keccak-256.\n- Exactness is gating: a wrong item hash recovers a wrong sender at ingress, which seeds a wrong trust-cache entry, which an exec HIT would then use. That is a fork risk.\n- Required before any bench: differential tests against alloy keccak256 (asm) on at least 10^6 random 320-byte inputs and on random PlaceOrderParams batches of 1-400 items (sizes not divisible by 4 included), plus known test vectors.\n- Runtime AVX2 detection with scalar fallback.\n- Optional: a startup self-test that disables the x4 path on mismatch.\n- Flag off runs today's code.", "flag": "TORUS_EIP712_KECCAK_X4=1 (default off; ignored without AVX2).", "impl_size": "M: ~200-300 lines. Lane type or round loop, absorb of 3 fixed blocks with 0x01/0x80 padding, 4-lane squeeze, dispatcher, flag, exhaustive tests and a microbench. Needs `unsafe` for target_feature; torus-types has no forbid(unsafe_code).", "test_plan": "1. Differential and vector tests (see determinism).\n2. Microbench on quiet archy: hash_place_order_batch(400) and recover_sender, scalar vs x4, single thread and with the SMT sibling busy. Require at least 1.8x on the item-hash loop and at least 1.4x on the whole struct hash; otherwise stop.\n3. Node A/B: 300m 600 s, ABBA n=3 (CPU-s/1M resolves 0.7% at n=3), plus 10m n=3. Report CPU-s/1M (ANCOVA at equal fills if matched/s moves), per-thread CPU of gossip-verify and ingress-verify from io-threads, matched/s (no regression), AGREE.", "novelty_check": "Prior Keccak work, all different:\n- keccak asm backend 82523da5 (scalar, 1.20x)\n- s77 typehash hoist and one trust-cache key per gossip ingest (merged; gossip-verify CPU -25%, the precedent for cutting verify-thread Keccak)\n- s123 exec-action-keys-parallel (thread-parallel cache keys on the exec thread only)\n- consensus hash reuse (refuted, hotstuff thread)\n\nNo multi-buffer or SIMD Keccak appears in docs, memory or git.\n\nThe AVOID item 'session keys / crypto tuning for ingress' (mem aed2e235, s367, 8-core box) found ingress verify latency to be queueing and contention, and targeted a cheaper signature path. Here the target is CPU-s/1M from cycles:u samples, which are real user cycles, not contention.", "metric_targets": ["CPU-s/1M", "per-thread CPU (gossip-verify, ingress-verify)"], "regime": "300m and 10m (CPU). matched/s neutral.", "risks": "- Crypto exactness (fork risk if wrong); mitigated by the gating tests and self-test.\n- The real x4 speedup on Zen 3 is unknown: 25 lanes of 256 bits exceed 16 YMM registers, and AVX2 has no 64-bit rotate. Hence the microbench gate.\n- Target features do not propagate into non-inlined generic callees, so the round loop must sit inside the avx2 function.\n- Gains CPU only; the headline throughput will not move at 300m."}], "notes": "I found three new candidates; none overlaps the prior-work brief.\n\n**Method.** Read-only. No cargo, perf record or bench was run. I parsed the existing perf-script dumps with Python scripts in /tmp/claude-explore-prof (inl.py, drill.py, w2.py, w2sites.py). For the W2 data, sample addresses were mapped through a2l-cache.json after recovering the load base by matching the low 12 bits of the keys. Stalled instructions were identified with objdump on the archived d3cba5e2 node binary.\n\n**Caveat on the data.** Every exec-thread profile with stacks comes from a pre-Phase-4 build: d3cba5e2 (p4s0, 300m), ef812b02 (p3cloidprof W2, exec-only, 5 events, 6 cells) and c88d3073 (prof-B). For candidates 1 and 2 I confirmed the code at the cited sites is unchanged on a44000e0. One cycles:u profile on head would confirm the shares before an A/B, but it is not required.\n\n**Thread ranking, current main 300m 600 s** (hsdb-300m-r2-B io-threads, per node):\n\n| Thread group | Cores per node | Share |\n|---|---|---|\n| exec-pool | 1.13-1.16 | ~22% |\n| gossip-verify | 0.94-0.97 | ~18% |\n| exec thread | 0.85 | ~16% |\n| ingress-verify | 0.76-0.84 | ~15% |\n| tokio | 0.44 (sys 23%) | ~8% |\n| rocksdb:low | 0.26-0.28 | ~5% |\n| hotstuff | 0.18 | ~3.5% |\n| rpc-worker | 0.17 (val2: 0.80) | — |\n\n**Function ranking.**\n- Node level: Keccak is 19.8% at 300m (c88d3073) and 24.4% at 10m (Phase 4). k256 ecrecover is about 7-10%.\n- Exec thread, unaddressed: the pass-B results store (~3.5%), sell_top_ups (2-6%), the s92 counter loop (1-1.3%) and the MatchRequest build (0.7-1%).\n- Exec thread, already covered: cancel-all (s123 #1), cache flush and save_books (Phase 5), verify and sort keccak (s123 #3), liquidations in the post-engine tail (Phase 5; liq-scan refuted), prepare_one (TORUS_PARALLEL_ENGINE A/B). The post_engine_tail of 11.9 ms is mostly run_liquidations.\n\n**Weaker leads I did not promote:**\n- **libsecp256k1 backend for ecrecover.**\n  - k256 recover is about 7-10% of node cycles, including constant-time select code that public-key recovery does not need. libsecp256k1 (the `secp256k1` crate, 0.30/0.31 already in Cargo.lock) is likely about 2x faster.\n  - A node-local flag is only safe with exact edge-case parity with k256 (high-s, r/s range, recid).\n  - It sits close to the AVOID item 'crypto tuning for ingress'. What has changed since: CPU-s/1M from cycles:u data, versus the s367 latency premise.\n- **torus_getBlockBody.** It runs `serde_json::to_value` for each action and then serializes the Value tree (torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:1788-1866). Serving the bench tail costs val2 0.63 cores at 300m and 0.72 at 10m. `serde_json::value::to_raw_value` (the raw_value feature is already enabled) would avoid the Value tree, but the split between decode, to_value, FixedPoint formatting and HTTP is unmeasured; val2 needs a profile first. A 10m depth-1 matched/s gain is plausible but limited by the client-side parse.\n- **Seeded fixed-width Address hasher.** Address hashing is about 3.6% of exec on-CPU and about 6.9% of pool samples (d3cba5e2): a length prefix plus hash_bytes_long for 20 bytes. Part of this is key-load stalls that candidate 2 removes.\n- **users::find interpolation probe** (users.rs:1205). Each trader's positions are a sorted Vec of about 300 entries at 300m, so the market id predicts the index. Estimated around +1%: below noise.\n- **The pool is host-contended.** Pool CPU is about 370 ms per native block against 42 ms of match plus pass_a wall, so pool CPU cuts shorten those phases on archy.\n\n**Suggested order:**\n- Candidates 1 and 2 can share one 300m ABBA n=4 campaign (2x2 or a combined arm).\n- Candidate 3 should pass its microbench gate before any node A/B.\n- All three edit native_executor.rs, torus-types or market_workers.rs: coordinate with Phase 5 step 2 (pass B) and the s123 cancel-all branch (market_workers.rs).\n\nI saved a memory note (project:torus-hyperbft, type:learning) with the key evidence."}, "research:r1:ingress-verify": {"candidates": [{"slug": "exec-carried-verified-senders", "title": "Carry each committed action's locally verified sender from the dispatch prune to exec, and skip the no-op phase-1 rayon fork-join", "mechanism": "Today `dispatch_to_exec` (consensus thread) already computes `compute_action_hash` for every committed action and calls `remove_committed_native`. That call looks up each pooled entry and reads its `(restash_key, sender)` (the sender this node recovered at RPC or gossip ingest), then throws the pairing away after restashing it into the trust cache. The exec thread then rebuilds the same answer:\n- `batch_verify_impl` re-encodes and keccaks every action into a `verified_cache_key`.\n- It takes the cache RwLock once per action.\n- It runs phase 1 as a `par_iter` on the global rayon pool even when every action hit and there is nothing to recover.\n\nWith `TORUS_EXEC_CARRIED_SENDERS=1`:\n- (a) The prune returns a `Vec<Option<Address>>` aligned to the block: `Some(sender)` only when the pooled entry has `restash_key` set, i.e. it was locally verified and is EIP-712. That vector is stored like `exec_prepared_durable` (so rank-2 retries reuse it) and travels in `CommittedBlockMsg`.\n- (b) The exec pre-pass uses `carried[i]` as the hit and computes key + lookup only where `carried[i]` is `None`.\n- (c) When no EIP-712 action is left as a miss, phase 1 returns `cache_hits` directly instead of dispatching rayon. That is byte-identical to `par_recover`, because `recover_one` returns `*hit` for hits and `None` for session actions.\n\nThe flag is gated on `exec_trust_cache` (same trust model). Replay and sync paths carry nothing and fall back to today's code. The trust-cache key identity is unchanged, so the mempool `lib.rs:197-209` rule \"DO NOT unify the two keys\" still holds: session actions are never carried.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:8006-8021 (dispatch hashes + remove_committed_native)", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:8047-8052 and 468-482 (CommittedBlockMsg)", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:4610-4625 (execution_loop -> execute_committed_block_with)", "crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:3364-3411 (exec verify call, exec_verify timer)", "crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:1564-1579 (remove_committed_native)", "crates/torus-mempool/src/native_pool.rs:357-369 (verified_restash_keys), 241-263 (NativePoolEntry.sender/restash_key)", "crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:1135-1181 (recover_senders_phase1 / par_recover always on the global pool), 1227-1240, 1271-1298 (sequential key pre-pass)"], "evidence": "**Current main, 300m, 600 s** (hsdb-300m-r2-B, val0 `phase_by_node`):\n- verify is 16.84 ms per native block, 5.4% of block_ms 312.96.\n- `torus_exec_verify_seconds_sum` is 33.1 s per node per run.\n\n**Exec misses are about 0.** Trust-cache counters on val0: hits 1,177,365, misses 471,373 (val1 and val2 are within 0.3%).\n- The gossip T2.1 lookup misses on 471,199 gossip first copies and hits on 470,627 pushed duplicates.\n- So exec makes about 707k lookups, all hits, and phase 1 does no crypto.\n\n**Where verify time goes** (d3cba5e2 val1 profile, `ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p1-mperf-w1-all.script.gz`):\n- Exec-thread verify is about 5.2% of exec-thread samples: key encoding 114, lookups 20, plus half of the 1,590 bare Keccak samples (the other half is sort keys, same 133-permutation preimage). That is about 10.1 ms of 193.8 ms on-CPU per block.\n- The timer read 11.48 ms in that cell, so about 1.4 ms per block is the all-hit phase-1 fork-join.\n- Global-pool workers (comm torus-execution, 36 tids) show 1,577 samples in `par_recover` rayon plumbing (join_context, steal, wake_specific_thread, epoch).\n\n**end_resident slack.** The worker takes 22.18 ms per block and end_resident_wait is 0.28 ms. The pre-`begin_resident` window holds settle wait 8.7 + verify 16.8 + replay 3.2 + the untimed sort (~10 ms), so about 1.5 ms of slack remains after the cut.\n\n**Arithmetic.** About -16.5 ms of verify, minus an estimated 1-3 ms of newly exposed end_resident wait, gives block_ms -12 to -15 ms (-3.8 to -4.8%) at 300m 600 s, and about -8 to -10 ms in 120 s cells (verify 11.5 vs end_resident 15.55).", "est_gain": "300m:\n- exec verify 16.8 to about 0.3 ms per native block\n- block_ms -3.8 to -4.8% net of end_resident exposure\n- matched/s +1.3% (section 67.4 ratio) to +4.5% (busy x fills-per-exec-second identity at constant busy); point estimate +2%\n- native blk/s +4%\n- CPU-s/1M about -1% (exec keccak plus the global-pool fork-join)\n\n10m tail depth 1: about 0, because exec busy is 0.77-0.80.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 2, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Node-local; state is byte-identical.\n- A carried sender exists only for a pooled entry this node verified itself (restash_key Some means EIP-712 and locally recovered).\n- It is found by `compute_action_hash`, which commits to canonical bytes + nonce + the tagged full signature (F-2).\n- So it equals the ecrecover of the committed bytes. This is the same argument as the existing trust cache.\n- Session actions are never carried and resolve against exec-time state.\n- A missing entry falls back to today's key + lookup + recover path.\n- The phase-1 skip returns exactly what `par_recover` returns when there are no EIP-712 misses.\n- Flag off is identical code. A mixed fleet is safe.", "flag": "TORUS_EXEC_CARRIED_SENDERS=1 (default off; requires exec_trust_cache on). The phase-1 skip can sit behind the same flag or a separate TORUS_EXEC_PHASE1_SKIP_ALL_HITS=1.", "impl_size": "M (~120-180 LOC):\n- mempool aligned-sender prune variant\n- `CommittedBlockMsg` field plus a `prepared` slot\n- an eip712 entry point that takes carried hits, plus the all-hit short-circuit\n- tests", "test_plan": "**Unit tests (eip712):**\n- With carried hits, `senders`, the drop set and the order of `verified_lookup` calls for non-carried actions are identical to flag off.\n- Fixtures: EIP-712 batches, tampered sig and body, session valid / expired / out-of-scope, duplicates, and a carried sender on a session action, which must be ignored.\n- The all-hit short-circuit equals `par_recover` in every `VerifyMode`.\n\n**Mempool test:** the aligned prune returns `Some` only for locally verified EIP-712 entries; trusted-path and session entries return `None`.\n\n**Consensus test:** the dispatch-to-exec path with a pooled block executes byte-identically to a cold-cache node (state digest).\n\n**Node A/B:**\n- 300m ABBA n=4, 120 s, on one binary: arms unset vs `TORUS_EXEC_CARRIED_SENDERS=1`.\n- If the s123 branch is built, add an arm with `TORUS_EXEC_PARALLEL_KEYS=1`.\n- Then a 600 s pair.\n- Report `exec_verify_seconds` per native block, end_resident_wait, residual_untimed, block_ms, native blk/s, matched/s, CPU-s/1M, AGREE in every cell.\n- Optional 10m pair, expected at about 0.", "novelty_check": "Not the s123 exec-action-keys-parallel mechanism. That branch keeps the key keccak and spreads it over the global pool, adding a second fork-join; this removes both the keys and the phase-1 fork-join for pool-resident actions. The s123 notes list \"carrying senders from dispatch\" under \"Also not done\".\n\nNot the refuted consensus-reuse-action-hashes either: that one cut consensus-thread Keccak, which is not the limiter. This adds no consensus-thread hashing; the hashes and pool lookups already happen in `dispatch_to_exec`, and the cut lands on the exec thread, which is the 300m bottleneck.\n\nThe trust-cache key identity is unchanged.\n\nCOORDINATE with 18c:\n- It edits `batch_verify_impl`, like exp/s123-exec-action-keys-parallel.\n- Best run as a third arm of that A/B. If the s123 branch lands first, this removes its remaining pre-pass, its pool CPU and its extra fork-join.\n- It does not touch the deferred sort-key half.", "metric_targets": ["exec block_ms", "matched/s", "native blk/s", "CPU-s/1M"], "regime": "300m (exec-bound); 10m depth 4 likely small positive; 10m depth 1 ~0", "risks": "- end_resident exposure. The pre-begin window shrinks to about the worker's 22 ms, so per-block variance may eat 1-3 ms. Phase 5 removes most of end_resident apply. If the deferred sort-key half also lands, the window shrinks further.\n- The exec gain depends on the end_resident and checkpoint settle overlaps. Report end_resident_wait.\n- File overlap with the s123 branch in eip712.rs, and app.rs edits near item 7 and propose-defer, which touch different functions.\n- Carried senders hold about 8 KB per block in flight in the exec channel (64 deep): negligible.", "test_plan_note": ""}, {"slug": "ingress-market-spec-cache", "title": "RPC ingress: cache market tick/lot rows and oracle aggregate marks per published ReadView height instead of one state read per distinct market per batch", "mechanism": "`verify_one_action_with` runs `validate_known_markets` and `check_price_band` on every submitted action, and both do one state read per distinct market.\n- `validate_known_markets` reads CF_NATIVE_MARKETS once per distinct market (BTreeMap per batch only).\n- `check_price_band` reads CF_FEE_CONFIG once plus `OracleManager::get_price` once per distinct market.\n- Each read walks the ReadView layer list (a Vec allocation, one lookup per frozen block) and then, for the static market rows, misses every layer and pays a RocksDB `get_cf` plus a borsh decode with two String allocations.\n- The bench draws every order's market uniformly (`tools/bench-throughput/src/main.rs:1280-1283`), so a 400-order batch touches about 221 distinct markets at 300m and at most 10 at 10m.\n\nWith `TORUS_INGRESS_SPEC_CACHE=1`, a node-wide cache in RpcState is keyed by `ReadView::height()`:\n- market id to (tick, lot) or unknown\n- the band parameter bytes\n- market id to the stored aggregate (price, timestamp, block, reporters)\n\nStaleness is still computed per call against the current `now_secs` with the same `max_age_secs`. Read errors are never cached. The cache resets when a newer view height appears, and a view with height `None` (interval 0) bypasses it. Lookups take one read lock per action.\n\nOptional add-on under its own flag `TORUS_RPC_FAST_HEX=1`: `parse_bytes` uses `alloy_primitives::hex` (const-hex SIMD) instead of `hex` 0.4.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:375-429 (validate_known_markets)", "crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:438-466 (check_price_band)", "crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:501-535 (verify_one_action_with), 759-794 (batch verify closure), 1596-1608 (single submit)", "crates/torus-rpc/src/types.rs:90-93 (parse_bytes, hex 0.4)", "crates/torus-state/src/read_view.rs:160-163 (height), 187-217 (layered/lower/get_cf_raw)", "crates/torus-core/src/oracle.rs:381-390 (get_price), 462-473 (stored_aggregate/is_stale)", "crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:283-288 (ingress_state)"], "evidence": "**d3cba5e2 val1, 300m** (`ozarchy-p4s0-run/analyst/p1-mperf-w1-all.script.gz`), callees of `verify_one_action_with`:\n- validate_known_markets 44.3%\n- ecrecover 24.9%\n- parse_bytes hex 11.2%\n- JSON ack 7.0%\n- get_price 3.4%\n\nvalidate_known_markets is 6,752 of 23,827 torus-ingress-verify samples (28.3% of the thread); get_price adds 2.2%. Hex is 6.9%.\n\n**Second build** (c88d3073, p3cloidec prof-B `folded-c0.txt`): validate_known_markets is 5.32% of node samples. 77.8% of it is under ReadView::get_cf_raw, and 67.6% is the RocksDB get itself.\n\n**Code unchanged since:** `git diff c88d3073 HEAD` touches torus.rs only after line 999 (R24 read budgets), and read_view.rs not at all.\n\n**Thread CPU on current main** (io-threads, hsdb-300m-r2-B): torus-ingress-verify is 0.837 / 0.822 / 0.760 cores per node, out of 5.12 / 5.08 / 5.65. The thread shares agree with perf for this pool (14.8% perf vs 16.0% /proc).\n\n**Arithmetic:**\n- Saving: (0.283 + 0.022) x 0.81 ≈ 0.25 cores per node ≈ 4.7% of node CPU ≈ 1.1 of 23.4 CPU-s/1M.\n- Hex add-on: 0.069 x 0.81 ≈ 0.056 cores per node, about 1.1%.\n- With a per-height cache, market reads drop from about 221 x 225 batches/s ≈ 50k/s to about 300 x 3.1 ≈ 930/s per node.", "est_gain": "300m:\n- CPU-s/1M -4.5 to -5% (plus about -1% with the hex add-on)\n- torus-ingress-verify CPU -30 to -37%\n- fleet about -0.75 cores on a host running ~16-17 of 32 CPUs busy\n- matched/s 0 to +1%: contention-mediated, not resolvable at the n=4 CI\n\n10m: about -0.2% CPU, because at most 10 markets per batch.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.5, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "Node-local and non-consensus. The ingress checks only decide RPC admission, and exec re-checks everything (torus.rs:404-406 comment).\n- A cached value is exactly what a read through a view at the same height returned.\n- The view's own freshness contract is only \"as of height >= n\" (read_view.rs:21-26), and per-height reset preserves it.\n- Market and fee rows change only through governance in a new block, which publishes a new view height before its checkpoint lands.\n- Oracle staleness is recomputed with the current now_secs.\n- Errors are not cached.\n- Flag off is identical code.", "flag": "TORUS_INGRESS_SPEC_CACHE=1 (default off); optional TORUS_RPC_FAST_HEX=1", "impl_size": "S-M (~80-120 LOC in torus-rpc: a cache struct in RpcState, a threaded param into verify_one_action_with, tests)", "test_plan": "**Unit tests:** the cached and uncached `validate_known_markets` and `check_price_band` return identical Ok / Err text for each case:\n- known market, unknown market\n- undecodable row (executor 1/1)\n- tick / lot violations\n- band hit and miss with a fresh, stale or missing mark\n- a market listed or changed at height h+1, seen once the view advances\n- view height None (bypass)\n\nRead errors propagate and are not cached.\n\n**Microbench:** `verify_one_action_with` on a 400-order batch over 221 markets, against a real DB with about 45 frozen layers. Report µs before and after, and RocksDB gets per action.\n\n**Node A/B:**\n- 300m ABBA n=4, 120 s, plus one 600 s pair. Arms: unset vs `TORUS_INGRESS_SPEC_CACHE=1`, then optionally adding `TORUS_RPC_FAST_HEX=1`.\n- Report io-threads torus-ingress-verify cores, CPU-s/1M (expect -4.5% or more, resolvable at n=3), matched/s, `rpc_submit_admit_rejects` by reason (must be unchanged), and AGREE.\n- One 10m pair to confirm about 0.", "novelty_check": "Flagged on 2026-08-18 in the markets-scale lens (mem 62fc1733 item 5: \"validate_known_markets does a RocksDB point-get per distinct market per batch => cache the market-id set in RAM\"). It was never built: `git log --all` has no such change, and the code still reads per market. Since Phase 3 the read is worse, because it walks the ReadView layers first.\n\nNot in the AVOID list or the perf map. The s119 note (mem 95df81a4) listed \"RocksDB Get latency on layer misses\" only as a low-ranked p99 lead, not a CPU item.\n\nNot the refuted \"session keys / crypto tuning\": no signature work changes.", "metric_targets": ["CPU-s/1M", "matched/s"], "regime": "300m (scales with distinct markets per batch); ~0 at 10m", "risks": "- Lock contention on the shared cache across 16 ingress threads: take one read lock per action, or use an arc-swap snapshot per height.\n- Production batches touching few markets gain little. Market makers quoting many markets gain the most.\n- The matched/s effect is unproven: the primary metric is CPU-s/1M.\n- The hex add-on changes the error text only for malformed hex.", "test_plan_note": ""}, {"slug": "gossip-ingest-early-dedup", "title": "Gossip/push ingest: hash once, drop already-pooled duplicates before DA re-mirror, key derivation and pool insert", "mechanism": "On the bench every block's bodies reach each follower twice:\n- once by gossip;\n- once by the leader's full pre-proposal push, because devnet `env.sh` sets `TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD=8000000`, so 4.5-7.7 MB body sets go out as full bodies.\n\nBoth copies go through `add_native_action_from_gossip` on the gossip-verify pool:\n- `mirror_to_da` clones the action and re-writes it to the consensus-DB DA CF (`put_batch` re-hashes and re-encodes it).\n- `verified_cache_key` re-encodes and keccaks it.\n- The trust-cache hit gives back the sender.\n- `admit_gossip` then calls `insert_with_restash_key`, which runs `compute_action_hash` and `serialized_size` under the pool WRITE lock and only then returns DuplicateNativeAction.\n\nWith `TORUS_GOSSIP_INGEST_DEDUP=1`, `compute_action_hash` runs once, up front, outside any lock:\n- If `(claimed_sender, hash)` is already in the pool's `seen` set (read lock), the copy returns DuplicateNativeAction immediately.\n- Otherwise the same hash is passed into the DA mirror buffer (`put_batch` with precomputed hashes) and into the pool insert (no recompute under the write lock).\n- Copies of actions that are not pooled (committed, evicted, or arriving first) keep today's mirror-first path, so availability for referenced bodies is unchanged.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:792-835 (add_native_action_from_gossip)", "crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:862-900 (admit_gossip), 949-1024 (submit_native_action_inner), 1351-1379 (mirror_to_da)", "crates/torus-mempool/src/native_pool.rs:408-426 (insert_with_restash_key: hash + serialized_size under write lock), 759-761 (contains)", "crates/torus-state/src/native_da.rs:97-123 (put_batch recomputes compute_action_hash)", "crates/torus-network/src/swarm.rs:1661-1679 (0xFD push -> native_action_inbound)", "crates/torus-node/src/main.rs:826-876 (gossip verify pool ingest)", "devnet/wsl/env.sh:45 (TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD=8000000)"], "evidence": "**Current main** (hsdb-300m-r2-B, val1):\n- `torus_native_gossip_received_actions_total` 471,199.\n- The \"received pre-proposal action batch\" log counts sum to 470,627 over 1,202 pushes.\n- dissemination: 1,805 body pushes vs 6 manifests, body_push_max_bytes 7.74 MB.\n- So every follower ingests a second copy of every action.\n- Trust-cache hits include those 470k duplicates: hits 1,177,365 minus about 707k exec hits.\n\n**Profile** (d3cba5e2 val1), callees of `add_native_action_from_gossip` (12,199 non-Keccak samples):\n- recover_sender 64.9% (first copies only)\n- mirror_to_da 15.6% (put_batch 66.7%, NativeAction clone 28%)\n- admit_gossip 14.3% (pool insert 87.6%, of which compute_action_hash 78%; plus RwLock write_contended)\n- verified_cache_key 4.0%\n- Bare Keccak is 11,784 of 25,779 gossip-verify samples.\n\n**Consensus DB, val1, whole run:** WAL 15.89 GB, flush 1.79 GB, compaction write 7.55 GB, compaction CPU 118.7 s (about 0.19 cores). Pushed bodies are about 470k x ~12.6 KB average ≈ 5.9 GB, about 37% of the WAL.\n\n**Arithmetic** (gossip-verify is 0.94-0.97 cores per node on current main):\n- Duplicate cost now: half of the mirror + admit + key samples plus 19% of the thread's Keccak (3 canonical passes of 133 permutations vs 1,697 for a first copy) ≈ 16.7% of the thread ≈ 0.16 cores per node.\n- After the change: about 0.03 cores (one hash plus a read-lock lookup).\n- Reusing the hash on first copies saves another ~0.04 cores.\n- Plus 25-35% less consensus-DB flush and compaction work, about 0.05-0.07 cores.", "est_gain": "300m and 10m (pushes are full-body in both):\n- CPU-s/1M -3.5 to -4.5%\n- gossip-verify CPU about -17%\n- consensus-DB WAL about -9 MB/s per node (-37%)\n- device writes about -12 to -15 MB/s per node of ~85\n- pool write-lock hold time down by about 60 µs per insert\n- matched/s 0 to +1% (contention); block p99 maybe slightly better in push bursts\n- RSS: negligible", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.4, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Node-local; no state or consensus effect.\n- A pooled `(sender, hash)` pair means the same signed bytes were already verified, mirrored at admission (both RPC and gossip paths call `mirror_to_da`, and failed DA batches are re-queued), and inserted.\n- Today such a copy ends in DuplicateNativeAction after redundant work. It never seeds the trust cache and never charges the address limiter, because both happen only after a successful insert.\n- A forged claimed sender still takes the normal path and is rejected.\n- Non-pooled copies are unchanged, including the availability mirror.\n- Flag off is identical.", "flag": "TORUS_GOSSIP_INGEST_DEDUP=1 (default off)", "impl_size": "M (~80-120 LOC across torus-mempool lib.rs/native_pool.rs and torus-state native_da.rs, plus tests)", "test_plan": "**Unit tests:**\n- (a) A pooled `(sender, hash)` copy returns Duplicate without growing `da_pending`, without a trust-cache lookup or insert, and without taking the pool write lock.\n- (b) A non-pooled copy produces the same pool, DA and trust-cache state as flag off.\n- (c) A forged claimed sender for a pooled action is rejected as today.\n- (d) A committed and pruned action re-pushed is still mirrored.\n- (e) `put_batch` with precomputed hashes writes the same keys and bytes.\n\n**Node A/B:**\n- 300m ABBA n=4, 120 s, plus a 600 s pair. Optionally a 10m pair.\n- Report io-threads gossip-verify and rocksdb:low cores, `torus_rocksdb_consensus_wal_bytes` / `compaction_cpu_micros` / `compact_write_bytes`, io-ticks write MB/s, CPU-s/1M, matched/s, view timeouts, dissemination_clean, AGREE.\n- Optional zero-code arm C, `TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD=0` on main, to bound the upside of dropping the push entirely. That arm changes dissemination, so watch the timeouts.", "novelty_check": "Not in the AVOID list or the perf map.\n- s77 hash dedup (0ff946e, merged) hoisted the typehash and made one trust-cache key per gossip ingest, but every duplicate still pays mirror, key and insert.\n- cc81630c (merged) is the leader-side analog: the proposer writes only missing DA bodies. This is the follower side.\n- s64 listed hash-only push with gossip on as never A/B'd (\"CPU relief only, +0-3%\"). This gets most of that relief without changing dissemination.\n- Not the refuted consensus-reuse-action-hashes, which was about consensus-thread keccak. This work is on the gossip-verify pool and the DA store.", "metric_targets": ["CPU-s/1M", "bytes written", "matched/s"], "regime": "both (300m and 10m); bench-shape amplified by the 8 MB push threshold, also real for any gossip-on fleet", "risks": "- Production with the 512 KB threshold pushes hash manifests for large sets, so it gains less there. The bench gains the most.\n- The DA pending buffer fills more slowly, so buffered first-copy bodies may wait up to the 50 ms age. That is node-local durability timing; selection and the vote path flush first.\n- One extra read-lock acquisition per copy on the pool RwLock (offset by less write-lock time).", "test_plan_note": ""}, {"slug": "eip712-batch-item-keccak-x4", "title": "4-lane AVX2 Keccak for the 400 PlaceOrderItem struct hashes inside hash_place_order_batch", "mechanism": "The EIP-712 struct hash of a 400-order `PlaceOrderBatch` costs about 1,298 Keccak-f[1600] permutations: 400 items x 3 permutations (each item preimage is a fixed 320 bytes: typehash plus 9 words), then 95 for ordersHash and a few more.\n- That is about 10x a canonical-bytes hash (133 permutations).\n- Every node computes it once per action, on RPC ingress or gossip recover; exec skips it on trust-cache hits.\n- The 400 item hashes are independent, fixed-length and computed serially.\n\nWith `TORUS_EIP712_KECCAK_X4=1` and runtime `is_x86_feature_detected!(\"avx2\")`, items are encoded 4 at a time into 4 x 320-byte buffers and absorbed in 3 rate blocks with a 4-lane Keccak-f1600 on stable `core::arch` AVX2:\n- either a small unrolled permutation under `#[target_feature(enable=\"avx2\")]`,\n- or keccak 0.1.6's public generic `keccak_p::<L: LaneSize>` with an AVX2 lane type, if it inlines under the target feature.\n\n32 bytes are squeezed per lane into `acc`. A remainder of fewer than 4 items, and the ordersHash, stay scalar. The output is the same Keccak-256 per item, so the hash is byte-identical.", "code_locations": ["crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:307-327 (append_place_order_fields), 350-355 (hash_place_order_item), 363-382 (hash_place_order_batch)", "crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:925-941 (recover_sender -> eip712_struct_hash), 1145-1153 (exec phase-1 only on misses)", "~/.cargo/registry/src/*/keccak-0.1.6/src/lib.rs:111-131, 236 (pub LaneSize + generic keccak_p usable on stable)"], "evidence": "**Bare __KeccakF1600 at 300m** (d3cba5e2 val1):\n- 45.7% of torus-gossip-verify samples (11,784 of 25,779)\n- 26.9% of torus-ingress-verify samples (6,420 of 23,827)\n\n**At 10m** (p4soak10 val1 `commsym`): Keccak is 12.5% (gossip) plus 6.27% (ingress) of node cycles, the top two symbols.\n\n**Item share of that Keccak** (permutation model per copy):\n- gossip: first copy 1,298 struct + 3 x 133 canonical hashes, push duplicate 3 x 133, so items are about 57%;\n- ingress: struct hash plus the ~441-permutation JSON ack, so items are about 69%.\n- That gives item Keccak ≈ 0.25 (gossip) + 0.145 (ingress) ≈ 0.39 cores per node at 300m on current-main thread CPU (gossip 0.95, ingress 0.81 cores): about 7.6% of node CPU. At 10m it is about 11% of node cycles.\n\n**Expected speedup** (literature, not measured here): a 4-lane AVX2 Keccak-f1600 runs about 2.5-3.5x the throughput per permutation of the 64-bit asm path. Zen3 has 256-bit AVX2 and no AVX-512. Rotations cost 2 shifts + 1 OR, and about 29 rotations plus 132 other ops per round fit about 35-40 cycles for 4 lanes, vs about 1,000-1,300 cycles per scalar permutation.\n\n**Arithmetic:** at 3x, saving ≈ 0.67 x 0.39 ≈ 0.26 cores per node at 300m (~5%) and about 7-8% of node CPU at 10m.", "est_gain": "- 300m: CPU-s/1M -4 to -5%; gossip + ingress verify CPU about -15%.\n- 10m: CPU-s/1M -6 to -8%.\n- matched/s 0 to +1% (contention).\n- These numbers are conditional on a microbench confirming at least 2.5x on the item hashes.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.5, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Byte-identical: each item is still Keccak-256 of the same 320-byte preimage.\n- Known-answer tests against `alloy_primitives::keccak256` pin it.\n- Exec uses the struct hash only on trust-cache misses (about 0), and would get the same value anyway.\n- If the CPU lacks AVX2, it falls back to scalar.\n- Flag off is identical code.", "flag": "TORUS_EIP712_KECCAK_X4=1 (default off; also requires runtime AVX2)", "impl_size": "M-L (~200-300 LOC incl. unsafe AVX2 permutation or LaneSize glue, absorb/pad/squeeze for the fixed 3-block item, KAT + fuzz tests, microbench)", "test_plan": "**Gate first: in-process microbench, no cluster.**\n- `hash_place_order_batch` on 400-order batches, scalar vs x4, on archy.\n- Proceed only if the struct hash drops at least 2.5x.\n\n**Tests:**\n- KAT: random 320-byte inputs and full batch struct hashes for 0-1,024 orders, with and without cloids; assert equality with scalar.\n- `recover_sender` and `batch_verify` fixtures are identical with the flag on and off.\n\n**Node A/B:**\n- 300m ABBA n=4, 120 s, plus a 10m pair.\n- Report io-threads gossip-verify and ingress-verify cores, CPU-s/1M, matched/s, AGREE.", "novelty_check": "No prior multi-buffer or SIMD Keccak work.\n- `git log --all` shows only the asm backend 82523da5 (merged, scalar, 1.20x) and the s77 typehash hoist.\n- The keccak crate's x4 path needs nightly `portable_simd`, so it was never usable as-is.\n- Not \"crypto tuning for ingress\": the signature scheme and the recovery are untouched. This is a byte-identical hashing implementation, like the merged asm backend.", "metric_targets": ["CPU-s/1M", "matched/s"], "regime": "both; larger share at 10m", "risks": "- Unsafe SIMD code correctness; mitigated by KAT plus fuzzing.\n- The speedup is unmeasured on Zen3: register pressure means 25 state words plus temporaries exceed 16 YMM registers and spill.\n- `#[target_feature]` inlining: if the generic `keccak_p` does not inline under AVX2, the lane ops become calls; an own unrolled permutation avoids that.\n- Gains only CPU and contention, not the exec thread.", "test_plan_note": ""}], "notes": "I have four candidates for this lens. Only the first one cuts time on the 300m bottleneck, the exec thread. The other three cut CPU (CPU-s/1M and bytes written); any matched/s gain from them comes only through less contention on the shared host and is not proven. All numbers come from existing profiles and cell outputs; no cargo, bench or perf recording was run.\n\n**Profile caveat that affects how to read archy perf data**\n- On the p4s0 p1 val1 cell, per-thread shares from `perf -F` disagree with `/proc` (io-threads user CPU) for bursty threads: rpc-worker 14.2% in perf vs 3.0% in /proc, and the 32 global rayon threads (they inherit the name torus-execution) 4.75% vs about 0.5%.\n- For the verify pools the two agree within about 4 points (ingress 14.8% vs 16.0%, gossip 16.0% vs 19.6%).\n- So I computed every saving as a fraction within one thread, multiplied by that thread's io-threads cores. I dropped one idea, the CPU cost of the global rayon pool, because of this.\n\n**Held back on purpose**\n- **Redundant verify inside k256 recovery.** `ecdsa` 0.16.9 `recover_from_prehash` (recovery.rs:281-316) ends with a full `verify_prehash`, so each recovery does two lincombs.\n  - `verify_prehash` alone is 3.34% of node samples at 300m (gossip 3,581 + ingress 1,799 samples), about 0.19 cores per node.\n  - A version without that trailing verify, plus an explicit low-S check (k256 `verify_prehashed` rejects high S, ecdsa.rs:202-208), is mathematically equivalent.\n  - I did not list it because the AVOID list has \"Session keys / crypto tuning for ingress\". That item was refuted on a latency premise (mem aed2e235); this one is about CPU. The owner should decide whether that premise still holds.\n- **JSON ack hash.** keccak over serde_json is about 1.5% of node CPU at 300m, but changing it changes the public RPC acknowledgement value.\n- **Hash-only push knob.** `TORUS_HASH_ONLY_PUSH_THRESHOLD=0` needs no code and would bound the upside of the gossip-dedup candidate, but it changes dissemination behaviour.\n\n**Coordination with 18c**\n- `exec-carried-verified-senders` edits the same function as exp/s123-exec-action-keys-parallel (`batch_verify_impl`). It is the \"carrying senders from dispatch\" item that the s123 notes list as not done.\n- It also removes a phase-1 rayon round trip on the global pool for blocks where every action hit the trust cache. That cost is about 1.4 ms per block (verify timer 11.48 ms vs about 10.1 ms on-CPU). The s123 branch would add another fork-join of the same kind.\n\n**Facts checked on current main a44000e0**\n- The exec trust cache misses about 0: hits 1,177,365 and misses 471,373 per node, and the misses are the 471k gossip first copies.\n- Pushed copies match gossip copies 1:1 (val1: 470,627 vs 471,199).\n- The consensus DB, which got its own RocksDB in a44000e0, spends about 0.19 cores per node on compaction.\n\n**Out of lens**\n- On val2, `torus_getBlockBody` uses `serde_json::to_value` per action. That costs about 0.63 extra cores and paces the 10m bench tail; serialising to raw JSON instead would help 10m at tail depth 1.\n\nI saved a memory with these findings (type:learning, project:torus-hyperbft)."}, "research:r1:alloc-hash-serde": {"candidates": [{"slug": "classic-book-encode-parallel", "title": "Encode the Classic book blobs in parallel, into pre-sized buffers, on the idle exec pool inside save_order_books", "mechanism": "In Classic mode (the testnet and bench default), save_order_books re-serializes every dirty market's whole book. It does so one market after another on the exec thread: borsh::to_vec(&*book), then put_cf_raw_owned, then clearing the never-drained row and level journals. At 300m every market is dirty every block. About 640k orders rest, at 97 B each for a Limit order without a cloid, so roughly 62 MB of blobs are encoded per native block. borsh 1.6.1 to_vec starts at 1 KiB, so each blob reaches its size through 8 reallocs (~200 KB books at 300m) to 13 reallocs (~6 MB books at 10m), copying as it grows.\n\nProposal, behind TORUS_CLASSIC_SAVE_WORKERS=N:\n(a) Collect (market_id, &mut OrderBook) for the dirty set, sorted by id, as drain_level_books already does for mode 2.\n(b) Split them into chunks by resting-order count with MarketWorkerPool::assign_chunks, and run each chunk as a job on exec_pool::run_jobs. Per book, the job encodes into a Vec pre-sized from a node-local per-market hint (last blob length plus about 6%), then calls discard_row_ops and discard_level_ops.\n(c) Back on the exec thread, put the blobs with put_cf_raw_owned in ascending market order. The overlay content, the written count and the fault latching stay as today.\n(d) A job that panicked: encode its markets serially, or latch the save fail-stop.\nGate: at least 2 dirty books and total resting orders of at least ~20k; otherwise the serial path runs. During save_books the exec pool is idle: it runs only match, settle pass A and the s123 cancel-all, all earlier in the block.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:6206-6359 (save_order_books; Classic arm 6252-6279: borsh::to_vec at 6259, put_cf_raw_owned at 6262, discard_row_ops/discard_level_ops at 6277-6278)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:3965-4006 (Classic takes ctx.save_order_books() at 3982 on the exec thread, timed as exec_save_books_seconds)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-core/src/order_book.rs:4577-4621 (OrderBook BorshSerialize = every resting order plus stops) and 4309-4350 (Order, 11 write_all calls per order)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/exec_pool.rs:11-59 (persistent pool, 'nothing else runs on it'; run_jobs with catch_unwind per job)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/native_executor.rs:2864-2910 (drain_books_parallel: the mode-2-only precedent) and 6420-6466 (drain_level_books iter_mut pattern)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-bridge/src/market_workers.rs:235-250 (chunk_indices / assign_chunks)", "~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/borsh-1.6.1/src/ser/helpers.rs:5,17 (to_vec initial capacity 1024)"], "evidence": "Timers on current main code (hsdb 300m r2-B, 600 s, summary.json phase_by_node val0):\n- save_books 21.73 ms of a 312.96 ms block (6.9%), at 72.0k fills.\n- r1-B and r3-B are within 1 ms.\n\n300m 120 s cells:\n- p4j4h r1-A: 11.3 of 226.3 ms.\n- p4j4d r1-J: 9.03 of 180.6 ms.\n\n10m, tail depth 4 (exec-bound; p4tail10 a4/b4 on 2a245321; the Classic save code is unchanged since):\n- 30.1-36.0 of 244-248 ms, i.e. 12-14.5% of exec wall, the largest exec item outside the engine.\n- At depth 1 it is 3.8 of 64 ms.\n\nResting orders (metrics-1hz torus_exec_resting_orders):\n- p50 ~638k at 300m 600 s and ~634k at 10m depth 4.\n- This matches an O(resting orders) encode per block. The README says the same: per-fill save cost is 38% higher in 600 s cells.\n\nProfile: p4s0 exec-thread inline dump (d3cba5e2, 300m; my parse of p1-mperf-w1-exec-inline.script.gz):\n- save_order_books = 5.48% of exec on-CPU samples, i.e. on-CPU, not waiting.\n- borsh to_vec/serialize: 4.51%. Of this, do_rallocx (realloc plus libc memcpy) is 1.24%, or 27% of the encode.\n- discard of the row/level journals: 0.89%.\n- W2: do_rallocx carries 4.3% of the exec thread's DRAM fills for 1.35% of its cycles.\n\nParallelism available: the pool reaches ~8.7x effective parallelism in its phases on this host (1.15 cores x 0.32 s per block / 42.2 ms of match + pass A wall).", "est_gain": "Arithmetic: the pre-size removes ~27% of the encode; parallel encode of the rest at 4-6x effective, limited by memory bandwidth because ~160 MB per block per node is read and written.\n\n- 300m 600 s: save_books 21.7 -> ~4-7 ms. Exec block_ms drops 12-18 ms (4-6%), so matched/s rises about 4-6% (sec 73.4 shows block_ms and matched/s moving ~1:1 here; rule of thumb ~0.3%/ms at 313 ms blocks).\n- 300m 120 s gate: save_books 9-11 -> ~2-3 ms, block_ms down 6-9 ms (3-4%), matched/s +3-4%. The n=4 paired CI is +-1.3%, so this should resolve.\n- 10m depth 4: 30-36 -> ~5-8 ms (10 books, at most 10-way), block_ms down 22-30 ms (9-12%), matched/s +9-12%.\n- 10m depth 1 gate: 3.8 of 64 ms and tail-paced, so ~0 to +1%.\n- CPU-s/1M: neutral to -1% (the realloc copies go away).\n- RSS: about +-0.1 GB.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 4, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "Byte-identical:\n- Each blob is borsh of one book, a pure function of that book.\n- Puts stay serial in ascending market order, so overlay content, write count and first-fault latching are unchanged.\n- The journals being discarded are never serialized or hashed (order_book.rs:1175-1181).\n- Only which thread computes the bytes, and the Vec capacity, change.\n- Flag unset, 0 or 1 runs today's serial loop.", "flag": "TORUS_CLASSIC_SAVE_WORKERS=N (unset/0/1 = the serial path as on main; N>=2 = parallel, cap 32). TORUS_CLASSIC_SAVE_MIN_ORDERS gates by total resting orders (default ~20000). The capacity hint is used only when the flag is on.", "impl_size": "S-M: about 120-180 lines in native_executor.rs (Classic arm plus a run_jobs-based encode helper modeled on drain_books_parallel, and a per-market length hint) plus tests.", "test_plan": "Tests first:\n- classic_parallel_save_equals_serial: random books (0-6k orders, all order types, cloids, stops) over 1-300 markets. Flag off vs workers 2, 8 and 32: identical CF_NATIVE_ORDER_BOOKS overlay rows, identical written count, identical counter and marker writes, journals empty afterwards.\n- The capacity hint too small or too large gives the same bytes.\n- An injected job panic falls back to serial, or latches the same fail-stop.\n- Mutation check: putting the blobs out of order must fail the test.\n- nextest rdeps(torus-bridge) with the flag off and on.\n\nBench (archy):\n- 300m 120 s, ABBA n=4: unset vs =8.\n- One 600 s pair.\n- 10m depth 1 (the gate) for no regression; optionally a 10m depth-4 pair.\n- Report save_books ms, exec block_ms, fills per block, native blk/s, matched/s, CPU-s/1M, RSS mean and peak, and AGREE in every cell.", "novelty_check": "- Not in the AVOID list or the s123 set.\n- The mode-2 parallel drain (drain_books_parallel, TORUS_SAVE_BOOKS_WORKERS, Aug matched-200k r1, mem f6e9deb9) covers level-authority modes only. Section 36 records that Classic has no save split, and the Classic arm on main is a serial loop.\n- 'Save books pass 1 off-thread' (refuted) ran the drain asynchronously into the next block. This proposal is fork-join inside the block, so the books are not mutated meanwhile.\n- s123 defer-book-blobs changes the checkpoint-layer merge, not the encode.\n- Phase 5 step 3 ('Classic book rows from a dirty set ... freeze serializes them') moves where the Classic blob is built, but still needs a whole-book encode per dirty market. The helper would carry over: coordinate with 18c, since save_order_books is in Phase 5's touch list. Phase 5 is planned, not started.\n- Memory search found no prior Classic parallel or pre-sized save.", "risks": "- Memory bandwidth: the 3 nodes share one memory controller, and when their save bursts coincide the speedup may cap near 3x. That is still about -10 ms per block at 600 s.\n- Blobs are allocated in pool-thread jemalloc arenas instead of the exec thread's: watch RSS and dirty-page retention.\n- The ~6% capacity slack stays in pending and frozen layers until merge.\n- Collides in place with Phase 5 step 3 and with s123 cancel-all only at file level (native_executor.rs).\n- Low-order 10m blocks: the work gate keeps them on the serial path."}, {"slug": "ingress-market-row-memo", "title": "Memoize market specs, oracle marks and the band parameter per published ingress view in RPC verify; const-hex payload decode", "mechanism": "For each submitted PlaceOrderBatch, verify_one_action_with runs validate_known_markets. It does one ReadView::get_cf_raw(CF_NATIVE_MARKETS) per distinct market, about 221 for a 400-order batch at 300m (300*(1-(299/300)^400)), plus a borsh decode of (String, String, i128, i128, i128) with two String allocations each.\n\nThe market rows are never rewritten, so every lookup misses the view's frozen block layers. Each lookup costs: a Vec of layer refs allocated by lower(), a probe of every layer, then a full RocksDB get_cf (ReadOptions per call, block-cache LRU mutex shared by 16 threads, value copy). check_price_band adds one oracle aggregate read and decode per distinct market, plus a CF_FEE_CONFIG read per action.\n\nProposal:\n- A memo keyed by the published view's identity (its shared Arc<Layers>; one view per executed block). The first lookup of a market in a view reads and decodes as today. Later lookups from any of the 16 ingress threads reuse the decoded (tick, lot) or not-listed answer. Read errors are not cached.\n- The same memo for the stored oracle aggregate (staleness still computed per action at now_secs) and for the band parameter.\n- parse_bytes decodes with alloy_primitives::hex (const-hex SIMD, already a dependency) instead of the hex 0.4 iterator decode: same bytes.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:375-429 (validate_known_markets: per-market get_cf_raw + market_row_shape)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:438-466 (check_price_band: band row + OracleManager::get_price per market)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:501-535 (verify_one_action_with) and 757-788 (batch verify on the ingress pool; state_db = mempool.ingress_state())", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-state/src/read_view.rs:195-217 (lower() allocates a Vec per call, probes every layer, then the DB) and 88-95 (ReadView = StateDb + Arc<Layers>)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-state/src/db.rs:1318-1321 (StateDb::get_cf_raw = allocating RocksDB get_cf)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-core/src/order_book.rs:538-541 (market_row_shape decodes two Strings)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-core/src/oracle.rs:381-390 (get_price = stored aggregate + staleness at now)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:276-289 (publish_read_view per block / ingress_state clone)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/types.rs:90-93 (parse_bytes = hex::decode, hex 0.4.3)"], "evidence": "Profile: p4s0 val1 W1 cycles:u all-threads dumps (d3cba5e2, 300m; my parse of p1- and p2-mperf-w1-all.script.gz). Shares of torus-ingress-verify samples:\n- validate_known_markets closure: 28.33% / 28.07% (two windows).\n- Of that, ReadView::get_cf_raw 26.0%, StateDb::get_cf_raw 23.9%, RocksDB get_cf_opt 22.8%; libc rocksdb::port::Mutex Lock/Unlock adds ~1.5%.\n- check_price_band: get_price 2.19% / 1.89%, plus BTreeMap inserts 0.7-1.0%.\n- parse_bytes (hex 0.4): 7.05% / 6.64%.\n- For comparison: ecrecover 15.9-16.1%.\n- git diff d3cba5e2..a44000e0 shows no change to these functions or to ReadView::get_cf_raw.\n\nCurrent main, per-thread CPU (hsdb 300m r2-B io-threads, 602 s, my computation):\n- ingress-verify 0.837 / 0.822 / 0.760 cores per node, 16.2% of node CPU (5.08-5.65 cores).\n- So the market lookups cost ~0.21-0.23 cores per node, and hex ~0.05.\n\nAt 10m the batch spans only 10 markets: the 10m Phase-4 soak top list has no RocksDB in ingress, but hex from_hex is 0.98% of val1 cycles.", "est_gain": "300m:\n- ingress-verify CPU -30 to -35% (memo ~28%, const-hex ~6%), i.e. -0.24 to -0.28 cores per node.\n- CPU-s/1M -4.5 to -5% (23.38 -> ~22.3).\n- A 0.7% step resolved at n=3 in sec 74, so this resolves.\n\n10m: memo ~0, hex about -1% node CPU.\n\nmatched/s: tie to +0.5%. About 0.7-0.8 cores are freed on the shared 32-thread host, a small cut to exec and pool runqueue wait.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.3, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "State is untouched:\n- These are node-local ingress admission checks; exec re-validates every order (torus.rs:357-374 comments).\n- The memo returns what the view returned at its first read. Each block's newly published view starts empty, so the freshness contract in the read_view.rs header ('answers every ingress key as of height >= n') still holds.\n- const-hex decodes the same bytes.\n- Flags off = today's path.", "flag": "Both default off: TORUS_INGRESS_VIEW_MEMO=1 (market spec, oracle aggregate and band memo per published view) and TORUS_RPC_CONST_HEX=1 (const-hex decode).", "impl_size": "S: about 80-120 lines. A view-identity accessor on ReadView (torus-state), and in torus-rpc a read-mostly memo keyed by view id (or a per-thread memo plus a view-id check), used by validate_known_markets and check_price_band; one line in parse_bytes. Plus tests.", "test_plan": "Tests first:\n- memo_matches_uncached_reads: random batches over listed and unlisted markets, off-tick and sub-lot orders. Same accept/reject verdicts and same error text, flag off vs on.\n- A listing or tick change executed in block n is seen by the first view published after n.\n- An injected read error is not cached.\n- Price band with fresh and stale marks at different now_secs gives identical verdicts.\n- const-hex: random payloads plus odd-length, bad-character and 0x-prefix cases decode identically, keeping the 'invalid hex:' prefix.\n\nBench (archy): 300m 120 s, ABBA n=4, off vs both flags on. Report CPU-s/1M, ingress-verify thread CPU from io-threads, rpc_submit_verify_cpu_seconds, matched/s, AGREE. Also 10m for no regression.", "novelty_check": "- The Aug 18 markets-scale lens (mem 62fc1733, item 5) suggested caching the market-id set but it was never built or measured. This is the first measurement: 28% of ingress CPU at 300m.\n- s91 (mem bf53df1b) mentioned const-hex as an optional shed-path win; it was not built (types.rs:90-93 still uses hex 0.4).\n- Not in the AVOID list. The s123 refutations concern consensus-thread hashing.\n- 'Session keys / crypto tuning for ingress (refuted)' (mem aed2e235) was about latency and crypto, not these state reads.\n- No overlap with item 7, Phase 5 or the s123 branches (torus-rpc ingress path only).", "risks": "- With interval 0 the view is db_only (a new Arc per call): the memo is inert but correct.\n- 16 ingress threads read the memo: use a sharded or read-mostly map to avoid lock-line contention.\n- Memo size is at most the number of markets per view.\n- const-hex error strings for malformed hex may differ in wording; tests check the prefix only.\n- Item 7 edits other parts of torus-rpc: small merge risk."}, {"slug": "eip712-item-keccak-x4", "title": "4-way AVX2 Keccak for the independent PlaceOrderItem hashes in hash_place_order_batch (gossip and ingress verify)", "mechanism": "hash_place_order_batch hashes each order's 320-byte item preimage separately with alloy keccak256 (the single-buffer keccak-asm backend). That is 3 Keccak-f1600 permutations per item, 1,200 for a 400-order batch.\n\nPer batch, against all Keccak work on each thread:\n- gossip-verify: ~1,700 permutations in total (items 1,200, ordersHash ~95, verified_cache_key and compute_action_hash x2 at ~133 each). Items are about 70%.\n- ingress-verify: ~1,770 in total, including the ~470-permutation JSON ack. Items are about 68%.\n\nThe item hashes are independent. Hash 4 at a time with a lane-sliced 4-way Keccak-f1600: AVX2 via std::arch on stable Rust, or the vendored XKCP KeccakP-1600-times4-SIMD256 C file through cc. Fixed 320-byte input means 3 absorb blocks with known padding. The scalar path handles the <4 tail and hosts without AVX2. ordersHash, the struct hash, the signature check and every digest are unchanged.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:350-382 (hash_place_order_item / hash_place_order_batch)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:307-327 (append_place_order_fields: the 10 x 32-byte item preimage)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-types/src/eip712.rs:245-303 (eip712_struct_hash dispatch) and 925-931 / 1044 (recover_sender, validate_with_sessions)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:792-835 (gossip: recover_sender for every new action)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:521-525 (ingress validate_with_sessions)"], "evidence": "Keccak by thread (docs/perf/bench-archive/tools/ozarchy-p3cloidprof-tools/analyst/noise/keccak-by-thread.txt, 300m):\n- Keccak self 0.81 cores per node: gossip-verify 0.41, ingress-verify 0.21, hotstuff 0.07, exec 0.06, rpc 0.05.\n- Intact-chain callers (keccak-intact-chain-callers.txt): 60% gossip eip712_struct_hash<-recover_sender, 32% ingress eip712_struct_hash<-validate_with_sessions.\n\np4s0 (d3cba5e2) W1, my parse:\n- Keccak leaf = 47.7% / 48.0% of gossip-verify samples and 28.2% / 29.3% of ingress-verify samples.\n- recover_sender (non-Keccak part) 30.7% of gossip.\n\n10m Phase-4 soak, val1 (p4soak10 top-b-k1-b, commsym-b-k1-b):\n- __KeccakF1600 is 24.39% of all val1 cycles: gossip 12.50%, ingress 6.27%.\n- encode_place_order_params 1.44%.\n\nCurrent main per-thread CPU (hsdb 300m r2-B): gossip 0.94-0.97 cores, ingress 0.76-0.84 cores per node.", "est_gain": "Item hashing load at 300m:\n- gossip: 0.95 x 0.48 x 0.70 = ~0.32 cores per node.\n- ingress: 0.82 x 0.285 x 0.68 = ~0.16 cores per node.\n\nAt ~2.5x for 4-way AVX2 vs single-buffer x86-64 asm (published XKCP ratios; not measured here) that is -0.27 to -0.30 cores per node:\n- CPU-s/1M -5 to -6% at 300m.\n- About -7 to -8% at 10m, where Keccak is 24% of node cycles.\n- matched/s: tie to +0.5% (host contention only; verify is off the exec critical path).", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.3, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Same function, identical 32-byte digests, so ordersHash, signing hash and ecrecover inputs do not change. The flag only selects the computation path, with runtime AVX2 detection. A wrong implementation would show up at once as failed signature checks: differential tests are the gate, and AGREE in every bench cell is the backstop. Flag off = today's scalar path.", "flag": "TORUS_EIP712_KECCAK_X4=1 (default off; falls back to scalar when AVX2 is absent).", "impl_size": "M: about 250-400 lines for the x4 permutation plus fixed-length absorb/squeeze in std::arch AVX2, or a vendored XKCP times4 C file (~600 lines, CC0) built through cc. Plus the 4-at-a-time loop in hash_place_order_batch and tests.", "test_plan": "Tests first:\n- keccak_x4_equals_scalar: at least 1e5 random 320-byte inputs and known vectors against alloy keccak256, all 4 lanes, with lanes differing in the last byte only.\n- batch_struct_hash_x4_equals_scalar: random PlaceOrderParams batches of 0-401 orders (all order types, TIFs, cloids, reduce-only), flag off vs on.\n- Signed batches still recover the same sender.\n- Forced-scalar fallback test.\n\nMicrobench: hash_place_order_batch(400), scalar vs x4; expect at least 2x on the item part.\n\nBench (archy): 300m and 10m 120 s, ABBA n=4. Report CPU-s/1M, gossip-verify and ingress-verify thread CPU (io-threads), matched/s (tie expected), AGREE.", "novelty_check": "- The keccak asm backend (82523da5, 1.20x) is single-buffer.\n- s77 (mem 2f21491f) hoisted the per-item typehash out of the loop: different work.\n- s123 exec-action-keys-parallel spreads trust-cache keys across threads on the exec path. That is not SIMD and not gossip/ingress; it touches batch_verify_impl in the same file, so coordinate the merge.\n- consensus-reuse-action-hashes (refuted) concerns the hotstuff thread's compute_action_hash.\n- Memory and git searches show no multi-buffer Keccak work.\n- Not in the AVOID list.", "risks": "- SIMD correctness (mitigated by differential tests).\n- The 2.5x figure is from published XKCP numbers on other x86 CPUs, not measured on Zen 3 here.\n- The verify pools stay CPU-bound, so the gain is CPU-s/1M, not latency.\n- Adds C or unsafe intrinsics to a crypto path: needs a Codex adversarial review.\n- The JSON ack and canonical-bytes hashes stay single-buffer."}, {"slug": "block-body-raw-json", "title": "torus_getBlockBody: serialize each native action straight to JSON (RawValue) instead of building a serde_json::Value tree", "mechanism": "get_block_body decodes the stored body and then calls serde_json::to_value(a) for every native action. RpcBlockBody.native_actions is a Vec<serde_json::Value>.\n\nFor a 300m body (~357 actions, ~88k orders) this builds:\n- ~88k IndexMaps, because serde_json preserve_order is on in this workspace (indexmap is a serde_json dependency in Cargo.lock);\n- 8 String keys per order, each hashed;\n- Value::String nodes for the order type and TIF.\nIt then walks the tree to serialize and drops it all: about 15-20 small allocations per order.\n\nWith the flag on, each action becomes serde_json::value::to_raw_value(a), giving Vec<Box<RawValue>>. The raw_value feature is already enabled for torus-rpc. The JSON bytes are identical, because preserve_order keeps declaration order. There is one pathological case: an i128 outside the i64 range, where to_value fails and today prints null.\n\nThe bench's commit tail fetches every committed body from val2 this way, and so do production explorers and indexers.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/torus.rs:1788-1804 (get_block_body: decode_body_record then serde_json::to_value per action)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/src/types.rs:748-751 (RpcBlockBody.native_actions: Vec<serde_json::Value>)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-rpc/Cargo.toml:20 (serde_json with raw_value)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/tools/bench-throughput/src/main.rs:2716-2722 (bench reparses each action and hashes its JSON, so it is unaffected by how the server builds the bytes)"], "evidence": "Current main, per-thread CPU (hsdb 300m r2-B io-threads, my computation):\n- rpc-worker on val2 (which serves the commit tail) 0.796 cores, vs 0.171 and 0.172 on val0 and val1: about +0.63 cores.\n- taillag.json in the same cell: 1,912 bodies fetched in 582 s, fetch p50 278 ms, so ~190 ms of val2 CPU per body.\n\n10m:\n- ozarchy-p4swap10-tools/analyst/waves/cpu-per-action.txt and p4tail10 tail.txt: val2 extra CPU 186-205 ms per 400 released actions, 0.72 cores at depth 1; full-body fetch p50 264-270 ms.\n\nThe split within that cost is estimated from the code (Value tree build + serialize + drop ~60-70%), not profiled: perf has only ever run on val1.", "est_gain": "About 100-135 ms of ~190 ms CPU per body saved:\n- val2 -0.25 to -0.45 cores at 300m (-0.3 to -0.5 at 10m).\n- Fleet CPU-s/1M (mean of 3 nodes) -1.5 to -3%.\n- Body fetch latency for the bench tail -40 to -60%.\n- matched/s tie at 300m: the in-flight cap never binds (6-8k of 20k).\n- A possible gain at the 10m depth-1 gate, where the tail paces the load (sec 70.5), is not counted.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.2, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "RPC response formatting only; no state, consensus or exec code is touched. The JSON is byte-identical except the pathological i128 case above. Flag off = today's Value path.", "flag": "TORUS_RPC_RAW_BODY=1 (default off).", "impl_size": "S: about 30-60 lines. RpcBlockBody gets an enum or a second field type for the raw actions; get_block_body builds Box<RawValue>; adjust tests that construct RpcBlockBody.", "test_plan": "Tests first:\n- block_body_json_identical_flag_on_off: bodies with every action variant (place batches with and without cloids, stop orders, cancels, cancel-by-cloid, oracle submissions, session-signed actions), executed and unexecuted. The serialized RPC response bytes must be equal.\n\nUbench: get_block_body on a 357-action / 88k-order body, CPU ms per call, off vs on. This confirms the split before any bench.\n\nBench (archy):\n- 300m 120 s, ABBA n=4: val2 rpc-worker CPU from io-threads, fleet CPU-s/1M, taillag fetch p50, matched/s, AGREE.\n- One 10m depth-1 pair: tail lag, waves, matched/s.", "novelty_check": "- Sections 69.3, 70.5 and 70.7 measured val2's body-serving cost and the tail pacing at 10m. The only remedy proposed was bench tail depth 4 (shelved, harness-side).\n- No node-side serialization change was found in git or memory.\n- Not in the AVOID list.\n- Trade-history / RPC items in the perf map concern trade rows, not block bodies.", "risks": "- Much of the saving shows up because the bench tail drives this endpoint. In production it matters for explorer and indexer RPC nodes.\n- The per-body split is unprofiled (no val2 perf data); the ubench must confirm it first.\n- A type change in a public RPC struct.\n- If the client-side parse dominates the 10m tail fetch, the 10m matched/s effect is nil."}], "notes": "I found four candidates, all behind node-local flags that are off by default; only the first raises matched/s. It cuts exec-thread time by encoding the Classic book blobs in parallel; the other three cut CPU (CPU-s/1M).\n\nMethod: read-only. I parsed the existing perf-script dumps with Python and ran no cargo, perf, tests or benches. Sources:\n- p4s0 300m, d3cba5e2, val1: the W1 all-thread and exec-inline dumps and the W2 5-event dump.\n- p4soak10 10m Phase-4 top-symbol and per-thread tables.\n- p3cloidprof/p3cloidec frames and Keccak-by-thread files.\n- Current-main 300m cells (hsdb r1-r3 B): summary.json, io-threads per-thread CPU, taillag.json, metrics-1hz.\n- p4j4 120 s cells and p4tail10 10m cells.\n\nThe newest CPU profiles with call stacks predate Phase 4. I used them only where the code is unchanged up to a44000e0 (verified with git: the Classic save arm, the ingress checks and eip712.rs). I also checked that no bench or build was running before parsing.\n\nProfile limits: Keccak leaf samples have no caller (the asm breaks frame-pointer chains), so I split Keccak by permutation counts per batch. perf_event_paranoid is 2.\n\nObservations I did not turn into candidates:\n1. Ack hash: on the ingress threads, keccak256 over the serde_json form of each action, used only to acknowledge the submit (torus.rs:526-533), costs ~12% of ingress-verify CPU (serde 4.5-4.7% measured, hashing ~64 KB of JSON estimated). That is ~0.1 cores per node, ~1.9% of node CPU. Returning the canonical content hash the pool already computes would save it, but it changes the RPC acknowledgement value: an owner decision.\n2. Classic row/level journals (row_journal / level_journal BTreeSets) are built at every book mutation and only discarded in Classic. That is 3.2% of exec-pool CPU and ~1.2% of the exec thread at 300m. It overlaps Phase 5 step 3's dirty-site edits, and the discard part is already absorbed by the first candidate: below noise alone.\n3. k256 ecrecover is 29% of gossip-verify and 16% of ingress-verify, ~0.4 cores per node: for a crypto lens (libsecp256k1-class recovery).\n4. libp2p gossipsub ed25519 sign/verify (SHA-512) is ~25% of tokio-rt-worker CPU, ~0.11 cores per node. Changing it is not node-local on the publish side: network lens.\n5. mirror_to_da deep-clones every gossiped action (2.3% of gossip-verify), and compute_action_hash runs twice on gossip (a [revisit?] item): ~1% of CPU in total.\n6. On the exec thread, cross-thread frees of match results and results-array writes in settle pass B cost ~1-3%. That code is in Phase 5's zone and the attribution is uncertain.\n7. The jemalloc tcache_max knob was never A/B'd. Large-extent work (eset/edata/extent) is ~1-1.5% of exec and pool time, but there is RSS risk and it sits next to the refuted decay knobs: untested, not proposed.\n\nCoordination notes:\n- Candidate 1 touches save_order_books, which is in Phase 5's step-3 list.\n- Candidate 3 shares eip712.rs with s123 exec-action-keys-parallel (a different function).\n\nSaved to memory: 3 notes tagged project:torus-hyperbft, lens:alloc-hash-serde."}, "research:r1:consensus-cadence": {"candidates": [{"slug": "hold-light-proposals-under-consensus-rate-limit", "title": "Pace near-empty proposals so consensus gossip from honest validators stays under the 50 msg/s per-author limit. This removes the 1.2 s rate-limit view timeouts behind the 10m block p99.", "mechanism": "How the drops happen:\n- The receiver-side consensus gossip limiter is a token bucket per cryptographic author: 50 tokens, refilled at 50/s (swarm.rs:1492-1497, peer_scoring.rs:171-210).\n- It is hardcoded at config.rs:280 and has no env override; main.rs:776-781 uses NetworkConfig::default().\n- It silently drops consensus-topic gossip beyond the limit. The only trace is a debug! line; there is no metric.\n- Each validator authors about 2 consensus gossip messages per view:\n  - leader: ProposalHeader broadcast (impl.rs:990) plus the pacemaker AdvanceView re-broadcast (pacemaker:506-527);\n  - each collector: the HotStuff AdvanceView (impl.rs:1778-1785) plus the pacemaker tick AdvanceView (pacemaker:192-215).\n- Light or empty views run at 4-5 ms each. At more than about 25 views/s the bucket empties after about 27 views.\n- The next leader's ProposalHeader is then dropped at both receivers. Votes ride /torus/direct and are not limited.\n- The view waits for its full 1.2 s timeout, then a TC forms.\n\nFix (leader side):\n- When the selectable native payload is below K actions and no EVM txs are pending, defer the fresh proposal until D ms after view entry.\n- Use the existing proposal_deferred retry (algorithm.rs re-polls every 10 ms while deferred), next to the S470 yield (impl.rs:584 and :796).\n- D of about 50 ms caps light views at about 20/s, which is at most about 40 msgs/s per author.\n- Heavy proposals, TC re-proposals and recovery proposals are never held.\n\nOptional companion: a TORUS_CONSENSUS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_PEER knob for headroom. Never raise the limit alone: it lets empty blocks run at about 200/s.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-network/src/swarm.rs:1492-1497 (consensus_rate_limiter.check_and_increment drops consensus gossip, debug log only)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-network/src/peer_scoring.rs:171-210 (ConsensusRateLimiter token bucket: max 50, refill 50/s)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-network/src/config.rs:280 (consensus_rate_limit_per_peer: 50, no env)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-node/src/main.rs:776-781 (NetworkConfig default used)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/hotstuff/implementation.rs:563-649 (deferred-proposal retry), 784-860 (fresh proposal path, S470 yield at 796), 1778-1785 (collector AdvanceView broadcast)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/pacemaker/implementation.rs:192-215 and 506-527 (AdvanceView tick broadcast and re-broadcast)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/algorithm.rs:208-215, 323-335 (enter_view retry, 10 ms poll while deferred)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/app.rs:141-300 (App trait: add a default-false hook)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:7612-7727 (select_block_payload in-flight set) and the TorusApp impl; crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:1122 (native_pool_size)"], "evidence": "Idle chain before the bench (ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B val0):\n- 14 commit gaps of exactly 1.21 s in 18.5 s, between bursts of 27-28 views. 91% of idle time is spent in timeouts.\n- val1 torus_gossip_messages_received_total rises about 111 per 28 views from 2 authors, i.e. 1.98 msgs per author per view.\n- A burst of (50 + refill) / 2 is about 27.5 views. idle_blk_s 21.5 = 27.5 / 1.28 s, matching the summary's idle_blk_s 16-21 across cells.\n\n10m depth 1 (ozarchy-p4tail10-10m-r1-a1, r2-b1):\n- 50-61 commit stalls >1 s per node per 303 s, totalling 102 s (33.6% of the window).\n- 98-100% of them follow at least 25 views in the prior 1 s (p50 36-38), against 31% for non-stall commits.\n- Traced at 20:09:19.104 (view 456) and 20:11:08.989 (view 1773): val0 and val2 never logged leader val1's header, while val1's own vote reached them in 1.4 ms. The next leader re-proposed the same height after about 1.2 s.\n\nGate cells (ozarchy-p4gate-10m-m1-a, m2-a, m1-b; 120 s):\n- 23-26 stalls per cell, 32-35% of the window.\n- Exec idle is 22-26 s per cell, of which 17-20 s overlaps the stalls.\n- consensus_timeouts: 52/cell at depth 1 vs 8 at depth 4.\n\nCounterfactual (drop the stalls that follow fast views): fleet block p99 goes from 1,311-1,342 to 521-549 ms (p4gate m1-a, tail10 r1-a1, r2-a1). Depth 4 is unchanged (772 to 766).\n\n300m: only 1-3 such stalls per cell, all in the first 22 s. In 3 of 5 cells one hits t0+0.1-0.3 s, right as exec starts.\n\nPrior flags: memories ca5430950aebcb36 and 64f83a7bc60ba95a (July) flagged the 50/s limit as a silent-drop hazard. It was never measured or fixed.", "est_gain": "Expected effect by regime:\n- 10m default gate shape (tail depth 1):\n  - block p99 down about 55-60% (about 1.32 s to about 0.53 s);\n  - consensus timeouts about 50 per cell to about 0;\n  - the 33% of the window spent in stalls is removed;\n  - matched/s 0 to +2%, most likely about 0, because the bench tail limits depth-1 throughput.\n- 300m 120 s gates: removes the t0+0.3 s start-up stall (about 1.3 s of idle exec), up to about +1% matched/s in 120 s cells and about +0.2% in 600 s cells.\n- Idle chain: a smooth ~20 blk/s instead of bursts and 1.2 s gaps.\n- 300m steady state: no change.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0.5, "confidence": "high", "determinism": "Timing only. The hold changes only when a leader proposes and how many actions it includes. That is proposer-local policy: validate_block accepts any valid payload. A limiter knob changes only message delivery. Committed state is still a deterministic function of the committed chain.", "flag": "TORUS_LIGHT_PROPOSE_HOLD_MS=D (default 0 = off, identical to main) and TORUS_LIGHT_PROPOSE_MAX_ACTIONS=K (default 100). Optional: TORUS_CONSENSUS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_PEER (default 50 = today).", "impl_size": "About 100-150 lines plus tests:\n- hotstuff_rs: an App hook hold_light_proposal(elapsed) with default false, a view-entry Instant, and 2 checks in enter_view;\n- TorusApp: env parse, then native_pool_size() minus the in-flight hash count, plus a no-EVM check;\n- optional 5-line env read in torus-network config.", "test_plan": "0. Zero-build confirmation on an idle 3-val devnet for 30 s:\n   - add torus_network::swarm=debug to RUST_LOG and expect 'consensus rate limit exceeded' lines just before each 1.21 s gap;\n   - or set TORUS_CONSENSUS_DIRECT_FAN=1 on all 3 nodes (the direct path is not limited) and expect idle blk/s to jump from about 21 to well above 50.\n1. Unit tests:\n   - the hold applies only when D > 0, the payload is light and elapsed < D;\n   - never on a TC, a re-propose or the catch-up path;\n   - flag-off proposal timing is identical.\n2. A/B, 10m default gate shape (IN_FLIGHT_TAIL=commit, depth 1), 120 s, ABBA n=4:\n   - arms: main vs HOLD_MS=50; optional arm C adds limit 200;\n   - metrics: log-based block p99 (a-cells), consensus_timeouts, count of >1 s stalls that follow at least 25 views/s, matched/s, native blk/s read with fills per block, CPU-s/1M, idle blk/s probe, AGREE.\n3. One 300m 120 s pair as a no-regression check (expect the start-up stalls to go from about 3 to 0).", "novelty_check": "Not on the AVOID list or in the s123 set.\n- It differs from propose-defer, the S470 watermark and the throttle watermarks: those act at exec saturation, while this only paces light payloads below the gossip limit and does nothing when blocks are full.\n- The timeout-base work (s55, s68) changed the timeout length, not the cause.\n- The B2 direct fan was only tested in the old wedge regime and does not pace.\n- Memories ca5430950aebcb36 and 64f83a7bc60ba95a flagged the limiter as a hazard but never measured or fixed it.", "risks": "- The K threshold must never catch heavy blocks.\n- The hold caps the idle cadence at about 1/(D + 5 ms).\n- native blk/s at 10m depth 1 counts the empty blocks between load waves (all_exec_blocks = committed), so it can move a few % with no real change.\n- Raising the limit alone floods about 200 empty blocks/s. That would slow the depth-1 bench tail, which fetches every body at about 5.6 ms per empty one.\n- Same enter_view site as s123 propose-defer (App::proposal_backpressure) and item 7: coordinate with 18c.\n- Keep the limiter for non-validator DoS protection.", "metric_targets": ["block p99 (10m)", "consensus timeouts", "native blk/s (10m, read with fills/block)", "matched/s (300m 120 s ramp)"], "regime": "Mainly 10m at tail depth 1 (the default gate shape). Also the 300m ramp (first ~22 s), idle and drain."}, {"slug": "proposer-commit-on-advance-view", "title": "Let a proposer commit from the AdvanceView carrying its own block's QC, gated on a non-full exec channel. This removes val0's structural ~1.2 s out-of-window block p99.", "mechanism": "Why val0 sees long gaps:\n- Votes for view v go only to leader(v+1) (primary) and leader(v+2) (backup) (roles.rs:118-163), never to the proposer.\n- The pacemaker's AdvanceView path only advances highest_pc, \"without commit processing\" (pacemaker:481-489).\n- So the proposer of view v commits the block that QC(v) finalizes only when the next leader's proposal arrives (justify, committed in try_insert_body).\n- The leader order is val0, then val2, then val1. val2's exec channel is full at 75% of its sends.\n- After forming QC(v), val2 runs the app feed, including the blocking exec send (app.rs:8108), inside on_receive_phase_vote. That happens before both its AdvanceView broadcast (impl.rs:1751 vs 1784) and its v+1 proposal. In a traced example val2 blocked 447 ms there.\n- val0 therefore learns about a third of the commits roughly 350-450 ms late, then sees 2 commits within 3 ms.\n\nFix:\n- In algorithm.rs, after the pacemaker accepts an AdvanceView with a Generic PC, call a new HotStuff method.\n- If the PC's block is in the tree, safe_pc holds and App::commit_feed_ready() is true, run BlockTree::update(&pc) plus process_update_result. This is the same code on_receive_phase_vote runs; no extra AdvanceView broadcast.\n- TorusApp returns true only when the flag is on, exec_failed is clear, deferred_exec is empty and exec_queue_len < 64. Slow nodes therefore keep today's behaviour.\n- Optionally also skip while a checkpoint write is in flight.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/hotstuff/roles.rs:118-163 (vote recipients leader(v+1), leader(v+2))", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/pacemaker/implementation.rs:453-541 (on_receive_advance_view: highest_pc only, no commit)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/algorithm.rs:377-387 (PacemakerMessage dispatch: add the early-commit call)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/hotstuff/implementation.rs:508-524 (process_update_result), 1665-1790 (on_receive_phase_vote: update and feed at 1744-1751 before AdvanceView at 1778-1785)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/hotstuff_rs/src/app.rs:141-300 (App trait: default-false readiness hook)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:7377-7506 (on_committed_block), 7948-8136 (dispatch_to_exec, blocking send at 8108), exec_queue_len mirror"], "evidence": "ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B (same crates/ as main a44000e0):\n- 224 of 226 val0 commit-event gaps >0.9 s contain val0's own produce_block (r3-B: 222 of 227).\n- val0's intervals that contain its own proposal: p50 838, mean 759, p99 1,556 ms. Its other intervals: p50 60 ms.\n- Per-height commit lag (send-log) for blocks committed by QC of val0's own view (h%3==1): val0 minus min(val1, val2) p50 +389 ms, p90 +543 ms. The other residues: -73 and +24 ms.\n- Channel occupancy at send: val2 at 64 (blocking) for 75% of sends, val0 2%, val1 1%.\n- Log trace: val2 formed QC(1418) at 06:28:48.891, logged 'sending 1417' at 48.933, blocked until its exec freed a slot at 49.380, and only then ran produce_block at 49.384. val0 logged the commit at 49.654 vs val1 at 48.910.\n\nPer-node out-of-window p99 (hsdb-analysis.md, j3x):\n- val0 1,202, val1 687, val2 835 ms.\n- Intervals over 1 s out of window: 66 / 0 / 2.\n\nCounterfactual (val0 commits no later than val1 + 50 ms): val0 p99 1,418 to 1,077 ms; intervals >1 s 133 to 31.\n\nAt 10m depth 4 (ozarchy-p4tail10-10m-r1-a4): all 17 of 17 val0 gaps >0.9 s contain its own proposal; val0 p99 932 vs val1 621.\n\nThis also explains val0's p50 of 98 ms noted in the bottleneck map (mem 2a94cf27).", "est_gain": "Expected effect:\n- 300m: val0 out-of-window p99 about 1.2 s to about 0.7-0.85 s.\n  - Fleet out-of-window p99 936 to about 770-800 ms (-12 to -17%).\n  - Fleet whole-window p99 1,169 to about 1,060-1,080 ms (-7 to -9%).\n- 10m depth 4: fleet p99 772 to about 680 ms.\n- val0 (the bench's submit RPC) learns commits about 350 ms earlier on about a third of heights.\n- matched/s, exec and CPU: about 0.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "Changes only commit timing on a node. The committed chain is QC-certified and identical. Exec still receives heights strictly ascending through the same at-least-once feed (committed_feed.rs). No state or format change.", "flag": "TORUS_EARLY_COMMIT_ON_ADVANCE_VIEW=1 (default off: the hook returns false and today's code path runs)", "impl_size": "About 100-150 lines plus safety tests. hotstuff_rs: hook, method and algorithm call. torus-consensus: hook impl.", "test_plan": "Unit and in-process tests:\n- 3 replicas where leader(v+1) blocks in its app feed: the proposer commits on AdvanceView before v+1 arrives.\n- No early commit for an unsafe PC, a block not in the tree, a non-Generic phase, or a full exec channel.\n- Lock stays monotonic; feed order with the flag off is identical.\n\nBench: 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=5 (p99 needs n=4-5 to resolve ~150 ms). Metrics:\n- per-node in- and out-of-window p99 (j3.py);\n- val0 count of intervals >1 s;\n- val0 per-height learn lag (send lines);\n- matched/s tie, timeouts, AGREE.\nPlus one pair at 10m depth 4.", "novelty_check": "T5.2 attribution was open (avoid=false). This adds new attribution: val0's outlier p99 is structural, not exec or GC.\n- It is not s66 defer-commit-feed or s72 defer-parent-feed: those reorder feed vs proposal on a leader, and on the steady path the next leader commits in on_receive_phase_vote, which those flags do not touch.\n- It is not the s70 collector local advance (the collector entering its own view).\n- It is not consensus-reuse-action-hashes (that was CPU work).\n- It makes no throughput claim, consistent with 300m being exec-bound.", "risks": "- Safety-adjacent: it must reuse safe_pc, the lock clause and BlockTree::update, require the PC's block in the tree, and handle Generic PCs only.\n- In checkpoint windows the early feed's state-DB writes can stall the proposer's thread just as the next proposal arrives, delaying its vote. Guard by skipping while a checkpoint write is in flight, and watch in-window p99.\n- Without the exec-channel gate, a slow node would block before voting.\n- Re-verify each PC once per view, deduplicated.\n- Shares files with item 7 (hotstuff, algorithm.rs, consensus app.rs).", "metric_targets": ["block p99 (300m out-of-window)", "block p99 (300m whole window)", "block p99 (10m depth 4)"], "regime": "300m (exec-bound) and 10m at depth 4. It applies to the predecessor of the slowest leader (val0 on this bench, because val2 serves the bench tail)."}, {"slug": "compact-exec-channel-handoff", "title": "Queue committed blocks for exec as compact references and rematerialize them just in time (prefetch depth 2). This frees the ~0.4 GB of decoded blocks parked in the 64-deep exec channel.", "mechanism": "Today:\n- dispatch_to_exec sends the fully decoded TorusBlock (CommittedBlockMsg, app.rs:468-482, blocking send at 8108) into sync_channel(EXEC_QUEUE_DEPTH=64) (app.rs:5760; rate_limit.rs:363).\n- At 300m that channel holds 59-60 blocks for 14-19 s. Each is about 7 MB decoded (about 244 place actions × 400 PlaceOrderParams with i128 and Option<u128> fields).\n\nChange:\n- Keep the consensus thread's FIX 1a durable persist of header and body at commit exactly as it is.\n- Instead of the Ready block, send the header, the action hashes (CompactBlock::from_block, about 13 KB), the evm and core-writer refs, the durable flags, slashes and committed_unexecuted.\n- A new torus-exec-prefetch thread takes from the 64-deep channel. It rematerializes each block all-or-nothing from the durable DA store (reconstruct_compact_from_da), falling back to CF_BLOCK_BODIES (load_replay_body), and fail-stops on a miss.\n- It hands Ready blocks to the unchanged execution_loop through sync_channel(2).\n- The same demote-and-rematerialize pattern already exists for the rank-2 nonblocking park (park_deferred, demotion to Compact under deferred_exec_ready_bytes_budget), but only off-channel.", "code_locations": ["/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-consensus/src/app.rs:468-482 (CommittedBlockMsg), 5760 (sync_channel(EXEC_QUEUE_DEPTH)), 4610-4662 (execution_loop), 7948-8136 (dispatch_to_exec), 7890-7936 (materialize_owned incl. ExecSource::Durable), 6234-6265 (reconstruct_compact_from_da), 8150-8200 (park_deferred Compact demotion pattern)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-mempool/src/rate_limit.rs:363 (EXEC_QUEUE_DEPTH 64)", "/home/oz/projects/torus-economy/Torus-hyperBFT/crates/torus-mempool/src/lib.rs:1997 (test committed_native_body_survives_commit_prune_in_durable_da)"], "evidence": "- Results doc section 48.2 (jemalloc heap profile, 300m): 'decoded actions / committed blocks / exec queue' is 381-451 MB live per node at 54.6-56.9 queued blocks, about 7 MB per queued block. The backlog delta was +226 MB.\n- Current main (section 74): exec queue at 59.5-60.3 blocks, so about 0.4 GB of live heap is parked.\n- Drain of ozarchy-hsdb-300m-r2-B val1: RSS fell from 5.26 to 4.05 GB between t0+606 and t0+615 s while about 50 queued full blocks drained. The mempool was already empty at t0+606.\n- Section 73: the W arm (queue 38.6 vs 61.6, blocks 17k vs 51k fills) had RSS mean 4,253 vs 5,134 MiB.\n- Rematerialization costs about the same as validate_block_da_reconstruct: 9.67 ms per block (r2-B val0).\n- DA bodies survive the commit prune (mempool test, lib.rs:1997).", "est_gain": "Expected effect at 300m:\n- RSS mean -0.3 to -0.45 GB per node (-5 to -8% of 5.74 GB): live heap about -0.4 GB, with a jemalloc RSS-to-live ratio of about 0.7-1.0. Peak similar.\n- CPU-s/1M about +0.6%: one extra ~10 ms decode per block on a helper thread, 3.1 blk/s × 10 ms = 0.03 core of about 5.3.\n- matched/s and exec block_ms 0: the exec thread is unchanged and the prefetch runs 2 blocks ahead of 313 ms blocks.\n- Proportionally less at 10m.\n- If s123 propose-defer lands with W=16, this shrinks to about 0.1 GB.", "est_gain_pct_matched": 0, "confidence": "medium", "determinism": "The exec input is byte-identical: the same committed actions, rematerialized all-or-nothing by content hash from durable stores, in the same strict height order. A miss fail-stops (or falls back to CF_BLOCK_BODIES). Nothing ever executes partially.", "flag": "TORUS_EXEC_COMPACT_HANDOFF=1 (default off: today's Ready channel)", "impl_size": "About 200-300 lines plus tests: message enum, prefetch thread, a 2-deep Ready channel, and fail-stop and metrics wiring (prefetch wait timer).", "test_plan": "Unit and integration:\n- state digest identical with the flag on and off over N blocks, serial and pipelined;\n- replay, and a crash between commit and prefetch;\n- DA miss leads to the CF_BLOCK_BODIES fallback or a fail-stop;\n- new timer: exec wait on prefetch, which must stay about 0.\n\nBench: 300m, 600 s, ABBA n=3. Metrics: RSS mean and peak (mem-samples, minutes 5-10), CPU-s/1M (ANCOVA at equal fills), matched/s, exec block_ms, AGREE.", "novelty_check": "Not on the AVOID list.\n- Propose-defer and its TORUS_EXEC_QUEUE_DEPTH arm cut queue depth through cadence. This keeps the depth and cadence and shrinks each queued entry.\n- They target the same memory, so whichever lands first takes most of the RSS gain.\n- The rank-2 nonblocking park demotion is off-channel only and never runs with blocking dispatch.\n- No prior memory or branch proposes it.", "risks": "- About +0.6% CPU-s/1M, which may resolve as a regression under the 'no CPU regression' gate rule.\n- Adds one thread on a host that is already busy.\n- A materialization miss must fail-stop, never skip.\n- The consensus-thread FIX 1a persist must stay to keep crash ordering.\n- Value drops about 4x if propose-defer ships with a small watermark.\n- Coordinate with 18c on the shared app.rs code.", "metric_targets": ["RSS mean", "RSS peak", "CPU-s/1M (guard)"], "regime": "300m (exec queue at 59-60 of 64) and 10m depth 4. Less at 10m depth 1."}], "notes": "I report 3 candidates for the consensus-cadence lens, best first. All are read-only findings; I edited no repo files and ran no cargo. My analysis scripts are in /tmp/cr-lens and only read existing bench logs. I saved 2 memories (project:torus-hyperbft): the rate-limiter finding and the val0 p99 attribution.\n\n1. **The 10m block-p99 floor is self-inflicted** (high confidence).\n   - The per-author consensus gossip token bucket (50 tokens, 50/s, silent drop) trips whenever views run above about 25/s.\n   - When it trips, a proposal header is dropped and the view times out at 1.2 s.\n   - This drives the idle chain's 16-21 blk/s, about 50 timeouts per 10m depth-1 cell, and block p99 of about 1.32 s. Without these stalls the p99 would be about 0.53 s.\n   - Fix by pacing light proposals. Do not just raise the limit: that floods empty blocks and would slow the depth-1 bench tail.\n\n2. **val0's out-of-window p99 of 1.2 s at 300m is structural.**\n   - A proposer gets no votes for its own block and AdvanceView does not commit.\n   - So val0 learns those commits only after val2 (whose exec send blocks 75% of the time) finishes its blocking exec send and proposes.\n   - 224 of 226 of val0's gaps over 0.9 s contain its own proposal.\n\n3. **The exec channel holds about 0.4 GB of decoded blocks per node** (section 48.2 heap profile). This one trades about +0.6% CPU for -5 to -8% RSS. Drop it if s123 propose-defer is going ahead.\n\nChecked and not proposed:\n- **No consensus-side throughput lever at 300m.** Commit cadence equals the slowest node's exec. Per-node exec block_ms differs by under 0.6% (val0 309.7, val1 309.9, val2 311.1 ms, r1-B). Commit timing per height has p50 within 25-73 ms across nodes, except val0's lag on its own-view blocks (+389 ms p50, candidate 2).\n- **The 10m depth-1 waves are set by the bench tail.** The tail reads every body from val2; full bodies take about 265 ms each, mostly JSON re-serialization. Faster getBlockBody serving is an RPC/bench item, not this lens.\n- **Remaining 300m tails.** Those in checkpoint windows come from the state-DB write queue: the commit-manifest put and header/body persist on the consensus thread (seen as an up to 560 ms stall at val1 06:28:44.98). Checkpoint slicing, already planned, covers them. Epoch-boundary exec blocks on val2 (433 vs 340 ms) overlap only 5 of 37 of val1's slowest 2% of commit intervals.\n- **The 'on_commit_block fired height=' log line reports the previous height.** It reads find_latest_height on the event-bus thread before dispatch_to_exec persists the header. Intervals stay consistent, but the labels are shifted by one.\n- **Empty blocks at 300m** occur only in the first 2 minutes of the ramp (100 + 12 per cell).\n- **Duplicate-action skips** are negligible (61-261 per cell).\n\nCode overlap: candidate 1 shares the enter_view deferral site with s123 propose-defer, and candidates 1 and 2 share files with item 7. Coordinate with 18c."}}
